window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["pgibams-2023"] = {"id": "pgibams-2023", "title": "PGIBAMS Raipur 2023", "fullTitle": "PGIBAMS Raipur 2023, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The five Ps of case formulation include:", "options": {"a": "Presenting Problems, Precipitating Factors, Predisposing Factors, Perpetuating Factors & Protective Factors", "b": "Predisposing Factors, Precipitating Factors, Persisting Factors, Perpetuating Factors & Protective Factors", "c": "Perpetuating Factors, Precipitating Factors, Personality factors, Presenting Problems & Positive Factors", "d": "Pre-morbid Factors, Precipitating Factors, Perpetuating Factors, Presenting Problems & Positive Factors"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the five Ps of case formulation are the presenting problem, predisposing factors, precipitating factors, perpetuating factors and protective factors. A case formulation is the clinician's working explanation of why this particular person has this problem at this particular time, and it guides the treatment plan. The five Ps framework, taught widely in CBT and psychiatry training (for example by Craig Macneil and colleagues, 2012), sorts that explanation into five boxes. The presenting problem is what brings the person in: the symptoms, their severity and their effect on daily life. Predisposing factors are vulnerabilities present before onset, such as family history, temperament, early adversity or perfectionism. Precipitating factors are the triggers that set the problem off now, such as a loss, an exam failure or a move. Perpetuating factors keep it going once it has started: avoidance, rumination, substance use, an unsupportive family. Protective factors are the strengths and resources treatment can build on, such as a supportive friend, good insight or a steady job. Each box can be filled with biological, psychological and social entries, which turns it into a biopsychosocial formulation (George Engel, 1977). Example: Arjun, 21, has low mood and poor sleep (presenting); his mother had depression and he is a perfectionist (predisposing); he failed his third attempt at an entrance exam (precipitating); he now stays in his room and ruminates (perpetuating); he is close to his sister and willing to attend weekly sessions (protective). Option a is right because it lists exactly the five standard Ps and nothing else. Exam trap: each wrong option swaps one or two real Ps for invented ones ('persisting', 'personality', 'positive', 'pre-morbid'), so check all five words, not just the first two.", "topic": "Five Ps case formulation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match the following. 1. Learned helplessness, 2. Flashbulb memory, 3. Freeze, 4. Shock. i. A state of extreme alertness where one is also unable to move or take action against the danger, ii. A highly vivid and detailed \"snapshot\" of a moment in which a consequential, surprising and emotionally arousing piece of news was learned, iii. A state that occurs after a person has experienced a stressful situation repeatedly, iv. A state of dissociation, where your body and mind feel disconnected and nothing feels real", "options": {"a": "1-i, 2-ii, 3-iii, 4-iv", "b": "1-iv, 2-iii, 3-ii, 4-i", "c": "1-iii, 2-ii, 3-i, 4-iv", "d": "1-ii, 2-iii, 3-iv, 4-i"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: learned helplessness matches iii, flashbulb memory ii, freeze i and shock iv (1-iii, 2-ii, 3-i, 4-iv). Learned helplessness was found by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967). Dogs that had received shocks they could not escape later lay down and took shocks in a shuttle box, even when jumping a low barrier would have ended them, while dogs that had been able to switch the shock off learned to escape. Repeated uncontrollable stress, not the shock itself, taught them that action is useless, which matches iii; Seligman (1975) later used it as a model of depression. Flashbulb memory, named by Roger Brown and James Kulik (1977), is a vivid, detailed 'snapshot' of where you were when you heard surprising, consequential and emotional news, such as the assassination of John F. Kennedy; that is ii. Such memories feel certain, but Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) showed with the Challenger explosion that they change a great deal over time. Freeze is the third member of the fight, flight or freeze response: the person is highly alert, heart racing, yet cannot move or act, sometimes called tonic immobility; that is i. Shock, in the psychological sense used here, is the stunned, dissociated state just after a traumatic event, when body and mind feel disconnected and nothing feels real; that is iv. Example: when Meera hears that her cousin has been in an accident, she first freezes, fully alert but unable to move; for hours afterwards she feels numb and unreal; years later she still remembers exactly where she stood when the call came. Option c is right because it is the only code that pairs all four terms correctly. Exam trap: 'repeatedly' in iii is the clue to learned helplessness, and 'extreme alertness but unable to move' is freeze, not shock; once 1 and 3 are fixed, only c fits.", "topic": "Learned helplessness, flashbulb memory, freeze", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Injury to the hypothalamus may cause a person to sleep abnormally for a:", "options": {"a": "Short period", "b": "Long period", "c": "Normal pattern", "d": "Abnormal pattern"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: injury to the hypothalamus can make a person sleep abnormally for a long period. The hypothalamus is a small region at the base of the brain, below the thalamus, that regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, hormones and the sleep-wake cycle. The classic evidence came from Constantin von Economo (1917), a Viennese neurologist who studied the epidemic of encephalitis lethargica, the 'sleeping sickness' that swept Europe during and after the First World War. Most of his patients slept for days or weeks, waking only briefly to eat, and at post-mortem their damage lay in the posterior hypothalamus and upper brainstem. A smaller group had the opposite problem, severe insomnia, and their lesions lay in the anterior hypothalamus near the preoptic area. Von Economo concluded that the posterior hypothalamus holds a waking centre and the anterior hypothalamus a sleep centre, and later lesion experiments in animals confirmed this. Today we know that the posterior and lateral hypothalamus contain orexin (hypocretin) and histamine neurons that keep us awake, and loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy, while the ventrolateral preoptic area switches sleep on. A third hypothalamic region, the suprachiasmatic nucleus just above the optic chiasm, is the circadian clock that times sleep across the 24-hour day. Example: after a viral brain infection damages his posterior hypothalamus, Rahul sleeps 16 to 18 hours a day and is hard to rouse. Option b is right because the classic result of hypothalamic injury, seen in von Economo's patients, is prolonged sleep (somnolence). Exam trap: 'abnormal pattern' (d) sounds safe and general, but the stem asks how long the person sleeps, and the textbook answer is a long period. Key note: damage to the anterior hypothalamus gives the opposite, sleeplessness, so the stem is a simplification; the classic textbook statement is prolonged sleep after hypothalamic injury, and b stands.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and sleep", "fig": "Constantin von Economo"}, {"q": "MMPI was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley", "b": "Theodore Millon and Alfred Binet", "c": "Glanzmann and Riniker", "d": "None"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the MMPI was developed by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built at the University of Minnesota Hospitals by Hathaway, a psychologist, and McKinley, a neuropsychiatrist; work began in the late 1930s and the test was published in 1943. Its key innovation was empirical criterion keying. Instead of writing items that merely looked as if they measured depression, the authors wrote a large pool of true-false statements and kept only those that patients with a given diagnosis (for example, depressed inpatients) answered differently from normal controls, mainly visitors to the hospital. An item stayed in the Depression scale because depressed patients endorsed it more often, whatever its content. This produced ten clinical scales, such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia and Schizophrenia, plus validity scales (L, F and later K) to detect lying, careless or odd responding, and defensiveness. The MMPI-2 (1989) restandardised it on a broader sample with 567 items, and the MMPI-A serves adolescents. Example: if Kavita answers 'true' to an item that depressed patients endorsed far more often than controls, it adds to her Depression score even if the item seems unrelated to mood. Option a is right because Hathaway and McKinley are the authors of the original MMPI. Exam trap: Theodore Millon did build a well-known clinical inventory, the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI), so pairing him with Alfred Binet sounds plausible, but neither man had any part in the MMPI.", "topic": "MMPI authors and criterion keying", "fig": "Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "Analysis of avoidance learning suggests that many phobias are acquired through ______ conditioning:", "options": {"a": "Classical", "b": "Operant", "c": "Intermittent", "d": "Reinforcement"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: analysis of avoidance learning suggests that many phobias are acquired through classical conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927), a neutral stimulus paired with one that naturally causes a reaction comes to cause that reaction by itself. John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) applied it to fear with an 11-month-old boy, Little Albert. Albert at first played happily with a white rat. Each time he reached for it, a steel bar was struck behind his head, and the loud noise frightened him. After a few pairings the rat alone made him cry and crawl away, and the fear generalised to a rabbit, a dog and a fur coat. But why do phobias last, when a conditioned response should extinguish once the feared object is met without harm? Orval Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory (1947) answers this. Factor one: fear is acquired by classical conditioning. Factor two: the person then avoids the feared object, and the drop in fear that avoidance brings is negative reinforcement, an operant process. Because the person never stays long enough to learn that the object is safe, the fear never extinguishes. Example: Kabir is bitten by a dog at age 7, so dogs become paired with pain (classical); afterwards he crosses the road whenever he sees one, and the relief he feels each time strengthens the avoidance (operant). Option a is right because the fear itself, the core of the phobia, is learned by classical conditioning; operant reinforcement only maintains the avoidance. Exam trap: the stem's words 'avoidance learning' pull you toward operant (b), but in the two-factor theory operant conditioning keeps the phobia going, while classical conditioning acquires it.", "topic": "Phobias and two-factor theory", "fig": "Orval Hobart Mowrer"}, {"q": "One of the concepts of the humanistic-existential approach is:", "options": {"a": "Rationalism", "b": "Transference", "c": "Deviation from the statistical norm", "d": "Unconditional positive regard"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: unconditional positive regard is a concept of the humanistic-existential approach. This approach, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, sees people as free, responsible and naturally growing toward their potential; its leading figures include Abraham Maslow (1954), Rollo May (1958) and above all Carl Rogers. Carl Rogers (1957), in his paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change, named three core therapist conditions: congruence (being genuine), accurate empathic understanding and unconditional positive regard. Unconditional positive regard means warmly accepting and valuing the client as a person, without conditions, even when you do not approve of what he has done. Rogers argued that problems grow from conditions of worth: a child learns that he is lovable only if he behaves in certain ways, so he denies parts of his experience and his self-concept becomes incongruent with what he actually feels. A therapist who offers acceptance without conditions lets the client drop that defensiveness, look honestly at his feelings and resume growth. Example: Rohan tells his counsellor that he cheated in an exam and hates himself. A person-centred counsellor neither lectures nor reassures; she accepts Rohan as a worthwhile person, listens closely and reflects his feelings, so he can explore why he felt such pressure. Option d is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions, the heart of humanistic therapy. Exam trap: 'rationalism' sounds like a philosophical, existential word, but it points to Albert Ellis's (1955) rational emotive behaviour therapy, a cognitive approach; and unconditional positive regard means accepting the person, not approving every behaviour.", "topic": "Unconditional positive regard", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "The link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is due to the presence of?", "options": {"a": "Corpus callosum", "b": "Reticular formation", "c": "Cerebellum", "d": "Hypothalamus"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the hypothalamus links the nervous system and the endocrine system. The nervous system sends fast electrical signals along neurons; the endocrine system sends slower chemical messages, hormones, through the blood. The hypothalamus, a small structure at the base of the brain below the thalamus, belongs to both. It sits just above the pituitary gland, the 'master gland', and is joined to it by a stalk, the infundibulum. It controls the pituitary in two ways. First, neurons in its supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons to the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, it secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal cortex, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary, which is why the hypothalamus is called a neuroendocrine organ. Example: before her viva, Ananya worries. Her hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol. A worry has become a hormone through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Option d is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones by ruling the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is called the master gland, but it takes its orders from the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus; the other three options are purely neural structures with no hormonal role.", "topic": "Hypothalamus as neuroendocrine link", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Excessive emotional attachment of a daughter to her father is called the:", "options": {"a": "Electra complex", "b": "Oedipus Complex", "c": "Inferiority Complex", "d": "Superiority Complex"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: excessive emotional attachment of a daughter to her father is called the Electra complex. In Sigmund Freud's (1905) psychosexual theory, the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals and on a family triangle. A boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival, fearing castration: the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek king who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother. For girls, Freud described a parallel sequence: the girl discovers she lacks a penis, blames her mother, turns her love to her father, and resolves the conflict, less completely in Freud's view, by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) named this the Electra complex, after the Greek heroine Electra, who helped her brother avenge their father by killing their mother. Freud himself rejected the term and preferred to speak of the female Oedipus complex, or 'feminine Oedipus attitude', but exams pair Electra with girls and Oedipus with boys. In classical theory an unresolved complex can leave lasting traces, such as a woman who keeps choosing older, father-like partners. Example: four-year-old Anjali announces that she will marry Papa when she grows up and sulks when he hugs her mother; in Freudian terms she is in the Electra phase. Option a is right because the Electra complex is the name for a girl's attachment to her father and rivalry with her mother. Exam trap: the Oedipus complex is the boy's version (son to mother), and examiners rely on students mixing up the two Greek names; the inferiority and superiority complexes belong to a different theorist altogether.", "topic": "Electra complex", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "In Sternberg's triarchic theory, the type of intelligence that involves the ability to think critically and analytically is called:", "options": {"a": "Componential intelligence", "b": "Contextual intelligence", "c": "Crystallized intelligence", "d": "Experiential intelligence"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory, the ability to think critically and analytically is componential intelligence. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, argued that IQ tests capture only one part of intelligence and proposed three sub-theories, hence 'triarchic'. Componential (analytical) intelligence is the ability to analyse, compare, judge and solve well-defined problems. It runs on three kinds of mental components: metacomponents, which plan, monitor and evaluate problem solving; performance components, which carry out the strategy; and knowledge-acquisition components, which learn new information. This is the intelligence that school and IQ tests reward. Experiential (creative) intelligence is the ability to deal with novel situations and to make familiar tasks automatic, combining experiences in original ways. Contextual (practical) intelligence, often called street smarts, is the ability to adapt to the environment, shape it or select a better one. Sternberg later folded the three into his theory of successful intelligence, the balanced use of analytical, creative and practical abilities to reach one's goals. Example: Priya tops her class by dissecting arguments in a logic paper (componential); her brother Kabir invents a new game from scrap materials (experiential); their cousin Dev, with poorer marks, runs a busy shop and handles every customer well (contextual). Option a is right because critical, analytical thinking is the defining feature of componential intelligence. Exam trap: crystallised intelligence (c) belongs to a different theory, Raymond Cattell (1963) and John Horn's fluid and crystallised abilities; examiners slip it in because it sounds like a fourth 'c' word beside componential and contextual.", "topic": "Sternberg's triarchic theory", "fig": "Robert Sternberg"}, {"q": "Which one of the following types of love does not belong to Rollo May's classification of love?", "options": {"a": "Eros", "b": "Philia", "c": "Agape", "d": "Narcissism"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: narcissism is not one of Rollo May's types of love. Rollo May (1969), the American existential psychologist, described four kinds of love in his book Love and Will. Sex, or libido, is the biological drive, satisfied by the release of tension. Eros is the drive to procreate or create, a longing for union with another person and for a lasting, deepening relationship; May thought modern culture had split sex from eros. Philia is friendship or brotherly love: an easy, relaxed liking of the other person as they are. Agape is selfless love devoted to the welfare of the other with no expectation of return, like a parent's love or the love of God for humanity. May held that mature, authentic love blends all four, and that love cannot be separated from will, the capacity to organise oneself to move toward a goal. Narcissism, love directed at oneself, is a different idea, from Sigmund Freud's paper On Narcissism (1914); it describes self-absorption, and in its extreme form narcissistic personality disorder, not a kind of love for another person. Example: Rhea's bond with her school friend Tara is philia; her devotion to caring for her ill grandmother, wanting nothing back, is agape. Option d is right because narcissism is absent from May's list of sex, eros, philia and agape. Exam trap: 'eros' sounds like Freud's life instinct, so students strike it out; but in May's scheme eros is a valid type of love, and only narcissism is foreign to it.", "topic": "Rollo May's four kinds of love", "fig": "Rollo May"}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Spinal cord", "c": "Frontal Lobe", "d": "Medulla"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "topic": "Reflex arc", "fig": "Charles Sherrington"}, {"q": "Cattell's personality theory is also known as:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic theory", "b": "Factor Analytic theory", "c": "Behaviouristic theory", "d": "Projective theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Raymond Cattell's personality theory is known as factor-analytic theory. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes the correlations among many measures and groups them into a few underlying dimensions, called factors. Raymond Cattell (1946, 1950), a British-born psychologist working in the United States, used it to find the basic structure of personality. He started from the trait words that Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, cut the list down to about 171 terms and then to a few dozen clusters, and gathered three kinds of data: L-data (life records, such as school reports and ratings by people who know the person), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests, where the person cannot easily tell what is being measured). Factor analysis of these data yielded 16 source traits, the deep underlying causes of behaviour, such as warmth, emotional stability, dominance and sensitivity; clusters of visible behaviour that merely go together he called surface traits. He measured the source traits with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). Cattell also divided traits into ability, temperament and dynamic traits, and his split of intelligence into fluid and crystallised came from the same method. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and high dominance but low emotional stability; these scores place her on dimensions the statistics discovered, not on categories a theorist imagined. Option b is right because Cattell derived his traits from factor analysis, so his approach is called factor-analytic. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (1947) also used factor analysis but arrived at a few broad superfactors; 16 source traits and the 16PF identify Cattell.", "topic": "Cattell's factor-analytic trait theory", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "The Children's Apperception Test (CAT) method of personality assessment was given by:", "options": {"a": "Skinner", "b": "Bellak", "c": "Holland", "d": "Murray"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Children's Apperception Test (CAT) was given by Bellak, that is, Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak (1949). The CAT is a projective test adapted from the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), which Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray introduced in 1935. In the TAT, adults tell stories about ambiguous pictures of people, and the themes are thought to reveal their needs, conflicts and relationships. Leopold Bellak reasoned that young children identify more readily with animals than with adult human figures, so the CAT shows 10 pictures of animals in human situations, such as chicks around a bowl of food, bears in a tug of war and a lion with a pipe. It is meant for children of about 3 to 10. Each picture is chosen to pull for typical childhood themes: feeding, sibling rivalry, attitudes to parents, fear of aggression, being alone at night and toilet training. The child tells what is happening, what led up to it and how it ends, and the examiner looks for recurring themes, the hero the child identifies with, and how conflicts are resolved. A human-figure version, the CAT-H (1965), followed, and the Bellaks also built the Senior Apperception Technique (1973) for older adults. Example: shown the bears pulling on a rope, six-year-old Aarav says the baby bear is losing and will be sent away; repeated themes of losing and rejection would be explored further. Option b is right because the CAT is Bellak's test. Exam trap: Henry Murray (d) made the TAT, the parent test, so his name is the trap; the children's animal version is Bellak's.", "topic": "Children's Apperception Test", "fig": "Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak"}, {"q": "Which part of the nervous system is called the centre of Emotions?", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Thalamus", "c": "Cerebral cortex", "d": "Hippocampus"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the hypothalamus is called the centre of emotions. An emotion has three parts: a feeling, an appraisal of the situation, and a bodily reaction such as a racing heart, sweating or a flushed face. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, organises the bodily side. It controls the autonomic nervous system, which speeds or slows the heart and breathing, and through the pituitary it controls stress hormones such as cortisol. The classic evidence came from Philip Bard (1928), who removed the cerebral cortex of cats and found that they flew into 'sham rage' at the slightest touch: hissing, clawing, arching the back and raising the fur, rage with no real target. The rage disappeared when the posterior hypothalamus was removed as well, so the hypothalamus was needed to produce the emotional response, while the cortex normally held it in check. Walter Hess then stimulated parts of the hypothalamus in awake cats through fine electrodes and produced coordinated rage or fear on demand, work that won him a Nobel Prize (1949). Example: when Vikram hears a loud bang at night, his hypothalamus sets off the fight-or-flight reaction; his heart pounds and his palms sweat before he has worked out that it was a firecracker. Option a is right because the hypothalamus drives the bodily expression of emotion and is the structure standard textbooks call the centre of emotion. Exam trap: the thalamus (b) is the relay in the Cannon-Bard theory, and the two similar words are easy to swap. Key note: modern work places emotion in the limbic system, especially the amygdala (James Papez's circuit, 1937; Paul MacLean's 'limbic system', 1952), and the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, 1927) gave the thalamus a central role; among these options the hypothalamus, called the centre of emotion in standard Indian textbooks, is the expected answer, and a stands.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the term used to denote the long-term increase in IQ scores over the past few decades?", "options": {"a": "The Flynn effect", "b": "Two generation gap", "c": "The IQ effect", "d": "The Wechsler effect"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the long-term increase in IQ scores is called the Flynn effect. James Flynn (1984), a political scientist in New Zealand, compared how American samples scored on old and new versions of IQ tests. Each time a test is restandardised, the new norm group must answer more items correctly to earn the average score of 100, so the same performance gives a lower IQ against new norms than against old ones. Flynn (1987) then gathered data from 14 nations and found the same pattern everywhere: average scores rose about 3 points per decade across the twentieth century, with the largest gains on tests of fluid reasoning such as Raven's Progressive Matrices rather than on vocabulary. Richard Herrnstein and Charles Murray (1994) gave it the name Flynn effect in The Bell Curve. The gains are far too fast to be genetic. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and better schooling, smaller families, a more cognitively complex environment and greater familiarity with abstract, test-like thinking. In some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, the rise has slowed or reversed in recent decades. The practical lesson is that tests must be re-normed regularly, because an old test inflates IQ. Example: if Arjun takes an IQ test whose norms are 20 years old, his score may be about 6 points too high, which matters when a cut-off around 70 decides a diagnosis of intellectual disability. Option a is right because the generational rise in IQ is named after Flynn, who documented it. Exam trap: the 'Wechsler effect' and the 'IQ effect' sound technical but do not exist; only the Flynn effect is a real term.", "topic": "Flynn effect", "fig": "James Flynn"}, {"q": "Which of the following types of discipline developed as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline many adults had been subjected to during their childhood?", "options": {"a": "Authoritarian discipline", "b": "Democratic discipline", "c": "Motivational discipline", "d": "Permissive discipline"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: permissive discipline developed as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline many adults had been subjected to as children. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), in Child Development, a textbook widely followed in Indian courses, described three types of discipline used by parents and teachers. Authoritarian discipline is the traditional kind: strict rules, harsh punishment for breaking them, little or no explanation, and no say for the child. Permissive discipline means little or no discipline: the child is seldom taught rules, rarely punished and largely left to make his own decisions. Hurlock explains that it grew up as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline that many adults had suffered in their own childhood; resolving not to repeat it, they swung to the other extreme. The result, she warns, is often a child who is confused and insecure, unsure what is expected of him. Democratic discipline uses explanation, discussion and reasoning to help the child understand why certain behaviour is expected; it stresses learning rather than punishment and rewards good behaviour. The scheme parallels Diana Baumrind's (1966, 1971) parenting styles, authoritarian, permissive and authoritative, with democratic discipline close to authoritative parenting. Example: Kavya's father was beaten for every small mistake as a boy, so he vows never to punish her; he sets no rules for bedtime or homework, and Kavya grows up unsure of limits. Option d is right because permissive discipline is the type Hurlock traces to adults rebelling against their own strict upbringing. Exam trap: democratic discipline (b) also departs from authoritarian methods and sounds like the reformed choice, but it is reasoned guidance with rules; the revolt that throws rules away is permissive.", "topic": "Hurlock's types of discipline", "fig": "Elizabeth Hurlock"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a projective test of personality?", "options": {"a": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "b": "Sociometric test", "c": "Word Association test", "d": "Bell's Adjustment Inventory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the word association test is a projective test of personality. A projective test presents an ambiguous or open stimulus and lets the person answer freely; because there is no fixed set of answers, the response is thought to reveal hidden needs, conflicts and feelings, the projective hypothesis set out by Lawrence Frank (1939), who coined the term projective methods. Francis Galton (1879) first tried word association on himself, noting the ideas each word called up. Carl Jung (1904 to 1910) made it a clinical tool: he read out a list of 100 stimulus words, asked the person to reply with the first word that came to mind, and timed each reply. Complex indicators, such as a long delay, no reply, repeating the stimulus word, an odd or very personal answer, or visible emotion, suggested that the word had touched an emotionally charged complex. Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff (1910) published a 100-word list with norms from 1,000 normal adults, so that each answer could be scored as common or unusual. Example: to most words Sanjay answers within a second, but when he hears 'father' he pauses for eight seconds and then says 'nothing'; that delay and odd answer would be explored as a possible complex about his father. Option c is right because the word association test uses free, unstructured responses to reveal unconscious material, the defining feature of projective methods. Exam trap: the MMPI and Bell's Adjustment Inventory are personality tests too, which tempts you, but they are structured self-report inventories with fixed answers, not projective tests.", "topic": "Word association as projective test", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Which among the following is a non-experimental research design?", "options": {"a": "Randomized controlled design", "b": "Quasi-experimental design", "c": "Case-study", "d": "Cross-sectional study"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the case study is a non-experimental research design. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable and, ideally, assigns participants to conditions at random, so that a change in the dependent variable can be attributed to the manipulation. In non-experimental (descriptive or observational) research nothing is manipulated: the researcher observes, measures or describes things as they are. The case study is the classic example. It is an intensive, in-depth study of one person, family or small group, drawing on interviews, observation, records, tests and history, often over a long time. A famous case is H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed parts of both his medial temporal lobes in 1953 to stop his seizures, Brenda Milner (with Scoville, 1957) studied him for decades and showed that he could no longer form new long-term memories, which revealed the role of the hippocampus. Case studies are rich, suit rare conditions and generate hypotheses, but they cannot show cause and effect and may not generalise to other people. Example: a clinician writes up how Neha, a girl with a rare dissociative disorder, developed her symptoms and responded to therapy; she has manipulated nothing, only described. Option c is right because a case study describes a single case without manipulating any variable or using a control group. Exam trap: quasi-experimental sounds 'half non-experimental', but it does manipulate the independent variable, only without random assignment. Key note: a cross-sectional study is also non-experimental (observational: nothing is manipulated), so d is defensible; the case study is the textbook prototype of non-experimental, descriptive research, and c stands.", "topic": "Case study as non-experimental design", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Reliability and validity are two dimensions of:", "options": {"a": "Test efficiency", "b": "Test procedure", "c": "Test construction", "d": "Retest value"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: reliability and validity are two dimensions of test construction. Building a psychological test follows a fixed sequence, set out in standard texts such as Anne Anastasi's Psychological Testing (1954). First comes planning: the purpose, the population and a blueprint of content. Next the author writes many items, tries them out on a sample and runs item analysis, keeping items of suitable difficulty that discriminate well between high and low scorers. Then the test as a whole must be shown to work. Reliability is consistency: does the test give similar scores on retesting, across parallel forms, across its two halves or its items (Lee Cronbach's alpha, 1951), and across scorers? Validity is accuracy: does the test measure what it claims, shown through content, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive) and construct validity? Finally norms are built from a standardisation sample so that a raw score can be interpreted, and a manual reports it all. A test can be reliable without being valid (a bathroom scale that always reads 2 kg too heavy is consistent but wrong), but it cannot be valid without being reliable. Example: when Dr Sharma builds a Hindi anxiety scale, she must show that it gives Pooja nearly the same score two weeks apart (reliability) and that it correlates with clinicians' ratings of anxiety (validity) before anyone uses it. Option c is right because reliability and validity are the core psychometric properties every constructed test must establish. Exam trap: 'retest value' (d) echoes test-retest, which is only one way of estimating reliability, and so is far too narrow. Key note: 'test efficiency' (a) is a loose phrase for how well a test does its job overall and is the near miss; reliability and validity are the formal dimensions every constructed test must show, and c stands.", "topic": "Reliability and validity in test construction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Community-based services that offer support in substance abuse include self-help services such as:", "options": {"a": "Alcoholics Anonymous", "b": "Crack Crack", "c": "Cannabis Collective", "d": "Hashish Home"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Alcoholics Anonymous is a community-based self-help service for substance abuse. Self-help (mutual-aid) groups are run by members who share the same problem, not by professionals; they are free, rooted in the community and meet as often as members need. Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) was founded in Akron, Ohio, in 1935 by Bill Wilson and Bob Smith, a stockbroker and a surgeon who were both struggling with alcoholism. Its programme is the Twelve Steps, which begin with admitting powerlessness over alcohol and move through a moral inventory, making amends and carrying the message to other alcoholics. Members attend regular meetings, share their stories, keep each other's anonymity and are guided by a sponsor, a member with longer sobriety. The goal is total abstinence, taken one day at a time. The model spread to other problems: Narcotics Anonymous (1953) for drugs, Gamblers Anonymous for gambling, and Al-Anon for the families of drinkers. Research links regular AA attendance, and professional Twelve Step Facilitation that encourages it, with higher rates of continuous abstinence. Example: after detoxification, Suresh's psychologist suggests that he attend AA meetings twice a week; there he finds a sponsor who has been sober for ten years and whom he can phone whenever a craving strikes. Option a is right because AA is the best-known community-based self-help group for alcohol problems. Exam trap: the other three options borrow drug names (crack, cannabis, hashish) to sound like support groups, but none is a real organisation.", "topic": "Alcoholics Anonymous self-help", "fig": "Bill Wilson and Bob Smith"}, {"q": "Match the following. 1. Personality structure, 2. Personality process, 3. Personality development, 4. Psychopathology and change. (i) Psychological reactions and motives that change dynamically over relatively brief periods, (ii) Stable and enduring qualities that define the individual and distinguish individuals from one another, (iii) People sometimes change, and sometimes resist change or are unable to change, (iv) Everybody changes, and comes to be a unique individual by adulthood. Codes in the order 1, 2, 3, 4:", "options": {"a": "(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)", "b": "(ii), (iii), (iv), (i)", "c": "(ii), (i), (iv), (iii)", "d": "(iii), (i), (ii), (iv)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: structure matches (ii), process (i), development (iv), and psychopathology and change (iii). Daniel Cervone and Lawrence Pervin, in their textbook Personality: Theory and Research, argue that every complete personality theory must answer four questions, and they use these to compare the major theories. Structure refers to the stable, enduring units of personality, the relatively unchanging qualities that define a person and make people differ from one another, such as Sigmund Freud's (1923) id, ego and superego, or traits such as extraversion; that is (ii). Process refers to the dynamic side: the motives, emotions and psychological reactions that change over relatively brief periods, such as anxiety and defence or a person's goals at a given moment; that is (i). Growth and development asks how everybody changes over time and comes to be a unique individual by adulthood, through genes, parents, culture and experience; that is (iv). Psychopathology and behaviour change asks why some people develop problems and how they change, or resist change, or cannot change, especially in therapy; that is (iii). A memory aid: structure is the architecture of a house, process is the activity inside it, development is how it was built over the years, and psychopathology and change is what happens when it needs repair. Example: Aditi's high conscientiousness is structure; her anxiety before tomorrow's exam is process; the way her strict grandparents shaped her is development; whether therapy can ease her perfectionism is change. Option c is right because it is the only code that gives (ii), (i), (iv), (iii) in order. Exam trap: 'change' appears in both (iii) and (iv); 'everybody changes and becomes unique' is development, while 'sometimes resist change' is psychopathology and change.", "topic": "Four elements of personality theory", "fig": "Daniel Cervone and Lawrence Pervin"}, {"q": "Choose the correct sequence of development of the following methods for interpreting the Rorschach inkblot test:", "options": {"a": "Exner's Comprehensive System, Beck's system, Klopfer's system, Piotrowski's, Rapaport-Schafer's", "b": "Rapaport-Schafer's, Klopfer's, Piotrowski's, Beck's, Exner's Comprehensive System", "c": "Beck's, Exner's Comprehensive System, Klopfer's, Piotrowski's, Rapaport-Schafer's", "d": "Rapaport-Schafer's, Piotrowski's, Klopfer's, Exner's Comprehensive System, Beck's"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the expected sequence is Rapaport-Schafer's, Klopfer's, Piotrowski's, Beck's and finally Exner's Comprehensive System. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published his ten inkblots in Psychodiagnostik but died the next year without leaving a full scoring system. In the United States five separate systems grew up side by side from the late 1930s onward. Samuel Beck (1937, 1944) stayed close to Rorschach and stressed norms and statistics. Bruno Klopfer (1937, 1942) took a more clinical, phenomenological approach and founded the Rorschach Research Exchange. Zygmunt Piotrowski (1937, 1957) developed his 'perceptanalysis'. David Rapaport and Roy Schafer (1945 to 1946) built a psychoanalytic, ego-psychology system in Diagnostic Psychological Testing, and Marguerite Hertz added a fifth. Because the systems scored the same responses with different codes, research findings could not be compared, and clinicians argued over which was right. John Exner (1974) solved this with the Comprehensive System, which kept the empirically supported elements of all five, standardised administration and scoring, and summarised a record in a Structural Summary of ratios and indices with norms. It became the international standard, and the newer Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011) grew out of it. Option b is right because it is the only sequence that places Exner's Comprehensive System last, after the earlier American systems it integrated. Exam trap: options a and c put Exner near the start, and d puts Beck after Exner, but a synthesis can only come after the systems it combines. Key note: the strict order is Beck and Klopfer, then Piotrowski, then Rapaport-Schafer, with Exner last; b is the only option that ends with Exner's 1974 integration, the point the question tests, and b stands.", "topic": "Rorschach scoring systems", "fig": "John Exner"}, {"q": "Paraschemazia is:", "options": {"a": "Distortion of the body image", "b": "Sensory deprivation of weightlessness", "c": "The belief that one's bodily intactness and integrity has been destroyed", "d": "None"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: paraschemazia is distortion of the body image. The body image, or body schema, is the mental picture each of us has of our own body: its size, shape, position and how its parts are joined. Frank Fish (1967), in Clinical Psychopathology, the standard British text on descriptive psychopathology, groups its disturbances as disorders of the body image and names three by their Greek-based prefixes. In hyperschemazia a part of the body is felt as larger, heavier or more prominent than it is, as when a lip feels swollen after a dental injection. In hyposchemazia a part feels smaller or absent, as in the neglect of one side of the body after right parietal damage. In paraschemazia the body image is distorted: parts feel twisted, separated from the rest of the body, displaced or changed in shape. It is reported with hallucinogens such as LSD, in epileptic auras and with lesions of the parietal lobe, the region that builds the body map. Example: after taking a hallucinogen, Karan says his arms feel as if they have come away from his shoulders and his head is twisting round; he knows his body has not changed, but it feels distorted. Option a is right because 'para-' means beside or abnormal, and paraschemazia is the distorted body image in Fish's scheme. Exam trap: option c describes a nihilistic delusion, the belief that the body has been destroyed, is rotting or is dead, as in Cotard's syndrome (Jules Cotard, 1880); that is a disorder of belief, not a distortion of the felt body image.", "topic": "Paraschemazia and body image disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques involves presenting target people with a small request and then following up with a larger request, the one desired all along?", "options": {"a": "Low ball", "b": "Foot in the door", "c": "Door in the face", "d": "Door in the foot"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. It is one of the compliance tactics of social psychology, ways of getting people to say yes to a direct request. Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) demonstrated it with homeowners in California. When a researcher asked people cold to put a large, badly lettered 'Drive Carefully' sign on their front lawns, only about 17 per cent agreed. But among people who, two weeks earlier, had agreed to a tiny request, displaying a small 'Be a safe driver' sign, about 76 per cent agreed to the big one. Two explanations are offered. Consistency: having said yes once, a person feels pressure to stay consistent. Self-perception (Daryl Bem, 1967): watching themselves agree, people infer that they are the kind of person who supports such causes. The first request must be small enough that almost everyone accepts it, or the technique fails. Example: a volunteer asks Ishaan to sign a petition to save a neighbourhood park; a week later she asks him to spend a Sunday planting trees there, and he agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face (c) is the mirror image, a large request that is refused followed by a small one, and examiners count on students swapping the two; 'door in the foot' (d) is a scrambled name for no real technique.", "topic": "Foot-in-the-door technique", "fig": "Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser"}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "options": {"a": "That two tests are done at the same time", "b": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "c": "The items on the test consistently relate to each other", "d": "Scores on the test correlate highly with scores on another test that measures the same attribute"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on the test correlate highly with scores on another test that measures the same attribute, taken at about the same time. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. One family of evidence, criterion-related validity, asks how well test scores agree with an outside standard, the criterion. It comes in two forms, separated by timing. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later: an entrance test is valid if it predicts first-year marks. In concurrent validity the criterion is measured now, alongside the test: a new scale is given together with an established measure of the same trait, or with a current diagnosis, and the two sets of scores are correlated. A high correlation, reported as a validity coefficient, means the new test agrees with the accepted measure. Concurrent validity is especially useful when a shorter, cheaper or local-language test is meant to replace a longer one. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), following the American Psychological Association's technical recommendations (1954), listed concurrent validity beside predictive, content and construct validity. Example: a psychologist translates a depression scale into Marathi and gives it to 100 patients on the same day as Aaron Beck's Beck Depression Inventory (1961); if Sneha's scores and everyone else's on the two tests correlate around .80, the new scale shows good concurrent validity. Option d is right because concurrent validity is agreement with an established measure of the same attribute taken at the same time. Exam trap: option a takes 'concurrent' literally, as two tests done at the same time; timing alone proves nothing unless the scores also correlate with a valid criterion.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in the:", "options": {"a": "Parietal lobe", "b": "Frontal Lobe", "c": "Occipital lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow (1868), reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes.", "topic": "Frontal lobe damage and personality", "fig": "Phineas Gage"}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of mental age?", "options": {"a": "Binet", "b": "Stern", "c": "Terman", "d": "Guilford"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the concept of mental age was introduced by Alfred Binet. Asked by the French education ministry to pick out children who needed special schooling, Alfred Binet and the physician Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test: 30 items of rising difficulty that tapped judgement, comprehension and reasoning, rather than the simple sensory tasks Francis Galton (1884) had tried. In the 1908 revision they grouped the items by age level, placing each item at the age at which most ordinary children could pass it. A child's mental age is the age level of the items he or she passes. Binet himself spoke of the child's mental level; 'mental age' is the term that stuck once Henry Goddard translated the scale into English. For example, if eight-year-old Riya passes all the items normally passed by ten-year-olds, her mental age is 10 although her chronological age is 8: she is two years ahead. Mental age on its own is not a single index of brightness, because a two-year lag means much more at age 4 than at age 14. That is where the later steps came in. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age to give the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied it by 100 and popularised the label IQ. Option a is right because the age-graded scale and the idea of mental age came from Binet's work with Simon. Exam trap: examiners pair 'mental age' with Stern or Terman. Stern contributed the quotient (mental age divided by chronological age) and Terman the multiplication by 100 and the American revision; neither invented mental age itself.", "topic": "Mental age", "fig": "Alfred Binet"}, {"q": "A 10-year-old boy has a mental age of 12 years. What will be his IQ?", "options": {"a": "100", "b": "120", "c": "90", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: his IQ is 120. This is the ratio IQ, the original way of turning a mental age into a single number. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1908) gave each child a mental age (MA), the age level of the test items the child can pass. William Stern (1912) saw that the same lag means different things at different ages, so he divided mental age by chronological age (CA). Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove the decimal. The formula is IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. Working: MA = 12 years and CA = 10 years; 12 / 10 = 1.2; 1.2 x 100 = 120. So the boy performs like an average 12-year-old although he is 10, and his ratio IQ is 120. A quick check helps: when mental age is above chronological age the IQ must be above 100; when the two are equal it is exactly 100; when mental age is lower it is below 100. Arjun, aged 8 with a mental age of 6, would score (6 / 8) x 100 = 75. The ratio IQ has a flaw: mental age stops growing in the mid-teens, so the formula breaks down for adults (a 40-year-old would score absurdly low). David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which compares a person with others of the same age on a scale with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option b is right because (12 / 10) x 100 = 120. Exam trap: inverting the ratio, dividing CA by MA (10 / 12 x 100, about 83), is the classic slip; an IQ below 100 for a child who is ahead of his age should warn you that the division has been turned upside down.", "topic": "Ratio IQ calculation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Light passes through the eye in which of the following sequences?", "options": {"a": "Cornea, pupil, lens, retina", "b": "Cornea, lens, pupil, retina", "c": "Lens, cornea, pupil, retina", "d": "Lens, pupil, cornea, retina"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: light passes through the cornea, then the pupil, then the lens, and finally reaches the retina. The full path is cornea, aqueous humour, pupil, lens, vitreous humour, retina. The cornea is the clear, curved window at the front of the eye. Light bends most where it passes from air into the eye, so the cornea does most of the focusing, roughly two-thirds of it. Behind the cornea lies the aqueous humour, a watery fluid. Light then passes through the pupil, which is not a structure but an opening in the iris, the coloured ring of muscle that widens the pupil in dim light and narrows it in bright light. Next comes the lens, a flexible, transparent disc attached to the ciliary muscle. It does the fine focusing: it thickens for near objects and flattens for distant ones, a process called accommodation. Light then crosses the jelly-like vitreous humour and lands on the retina, the inner lining at the back of the eye, where rods (dim light) and cones (colour and fine detail) turn light into neural signals that leave through the optic nerve. When Meera reads her phone in a dark room, her pupils widen to let in more light and her lenses thicken to focus the near text. Option a is right because it is the only sequence that follows the anatomy from front to back. Exam trap: the lens is the famous focusing part, so students put it first or in front of the pupil; in fact the lens sits behind the iris and pupil, and the cornea, not the lens, does most of the bending of light.", "topic": "Path of light through the eye", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"The quality of performance on any task is an inverted U-shaped function of arousal.\" This law is known as:", "options": {"a": "Law of Effect", "b": "Broadbent filter theory", "c": "Yerkes-Dodson law", "d": "Law of constancy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the inverted-U relation between arousal and performance is the Yerkes-Dodson law. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose a white box over a black one, giving electric shocks of different strengths for mistakes. Learning was fastest at an intermediate shock, slower when the shock was weak or very strong, and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later textbooks turned this into a general law about arousal (alertness, anxiety, physiological activation). Performance improves as arousal rises from a low level, peaks at a moderate, optimal level, and then falls as arousal becomes excessive, which gives the inverted U. The optimum depends on the task: simple or well-practised tasks are done best at fairly high arousal, while complex or new tasks are done best at lower arousal. Donald Hebb (1955) linked the idea to his optimal arousal theory, the view that organisms seek a moderate level of stimulation. Kabir, preparing for an entrance exam, studies poorly when he is bored and sleepy, best when he is mildly keyed up, and freezes when panic sets in on the morning of the exam; because the exam is a complex task, his best level of arousal is moderate rather than high. Option c is right because Yerkes and Dodson are credited with the inverted-U function linking arousal and performance. Exam trap: the curve is often confused with Clark Hull's (1943) drive theory, in which more drive gives more performance in a rising line; the Yerkes-Dodson law says that too much arousal hurts, especially on difficult tasks.", "topic": "Yerkes-Dodson law", "fig": "Robert Yerkes and John Dodson"}, {"q": "Who gave the theory of cognitive dissonance?", "options": {"a": "Likert", "b": "Doob", "c": "Leon Festinger", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Leon Festinger gave the theory of cognitive dissonance. In A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance, Leon Festinger (1957) proposed that holding two cognitions that contradict each other (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of one's own behaviour) creates an unpleasant state of tension, dissonance, which people are motivated to reduce. They can change the behaviour, change one of the beliefs, add new consonant beliefs, or play down the importance of the conflict. The classic test is the study by Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959). Students spent an hour on dull tasks such as turning pegs, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant that the tasks were enjoyable. Afterwards, those paid $1 rated the tasks as more enjoyable than those paid $20. Twenty dollars was enough to justify the lie, but one dollar was not, so the $1 group reduced their dissonance by changing their attitude: the effect of insufficient justification. Dissonance also explains effort justification (we value what we suffered to get) and post-decision dissonance (we upgrade the option we chose). For example, Vikram smokes but knows that smoking causes cancer. He may quit, or he may tell himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to 90', adding a consonant cognition that eases the tension. Option c is right because the theory, its name and its founding experiments are Festinger's. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 versus $20 result without any tension, by people inferring their attitudes from their own behaviour; Bem, or Fritz Heider's balance theory (1946), is the usual near miss for the founder of dissonance theory.", "topic": "Cognitive dissonance theory", "fig": "Leon Festinger"}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of stages in creative thinking? 1. Illumination, 2. Preparation, 3. Evaluation, 4. Implementation, 5. Incubation", "options": {"a": "2, 5, 1, 3, 4", "b": "1, 5, 2, 4, 3", "c": "5, 4, 1, 3, 2", "d": "3, 5, 2, 4, 1"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the sequence is preparation (2), incubation (5), illumination (1), evaluation (3) and implementation (4). The classic model comes from Graham Wallas (1926), who in The Art of Thought drew on the accounts that scientists such as Hermann von Helmholtz and Henri Poincare gave of their own discoveries. He described four stages. Preparation: the person defines the problem, gathers information and works on it consciously, often without success. Incubation: the problem is set aside; conscious work stops, but processing goes on outside awareness, and rest or a change of activity helps. Illumination: the solution appears suddenly, the 'aha' or insight moment, often while the person is doing something else; Poincare described an idea arriving just as he stepped onto a bus. Verification: the idea is tested, checked and refined. Later versions of the model, like the one in this question, split verification into two steps: evaluation, judging whether the idea really works and is worth keeping, and implementation, putting it into practice. Imagine Ananya, a design student stuck on a poster for a water-saving campaign. She studies past campaigns for a week (preparation), gives up and goes home for a holiday (incubation), suddenly pictures a dripping tap shaped like a clock while on the train (illumination), checks the idea with her teacher and classmates (evaluation), and then produces and prints the poster (implementation). Option a is right because it is the only order that starts with preparation, places incubation before the flash of insight, and ends by evaluating and then applying the idea. Exam trap: students put illumination first because it is the most famous stage, or place incubation before preparation; incubation only helps after real preparatory work has been done.", "topic": "Stages of creative thinking", "fig": "Graham Wallas"}, {"q": "The clinical term for causal factors is:", "options": {"a": "Epidemiology", "b": "Etiology", "c": "Prognosis", "d": "Relapse"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the clinical term for causal factors is etiology (British spelling aetiology). The word comes from the Greek aitia, meaning cause. Aetiology is the study of why a disorder develops: biological factors (genes, brain chemistry, infection, injury), psychological factors (learning, thinking styles, early experience) and social factors (poverty, trauma, family stress). Clinicians rarely find a single cause; the diathesis-stress model (Paul Meehl, 1962) and George Engel's biopsychosocial model (1977) treat causes as interacting. Aetiology belongs to a family of terms used in every case discussion. Epidemiology studies how often and in whom a disorder occurs in a population (prevalence, incidence, distribution). Prognosis is the predicted course and outcome. Relapse is the return of symptoms after a period of improvement. For example, when a psychiatrist discusses Sanjay's depression, the aetiology might be a family history of depression plus a recent job loss; the prognosis is good with treatment; and stopping medication too early raises the risk of relapse. Option b is right because etiology is the only term that names the causes of a disorder. Exam trap: epidemiology also talks about 'risk factors', so it tempts; but it describes how disorders are distributed across populations, not the causal explanation of the disorder itself.", "topic": "Aetiology and related terms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The procedure used to assess the brain wave pattern in waking and sleep stages is:", "options": {"a": "EEG", "b": "ECG", "c": "EMG", "d": "ECT"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: brain wave patterns in waking and in the stages of sleep are recorded with the EEG, the electroencephalogram. Hans Berger (1929) first recorded human brain waves through electrodes placed on the scalp. The EEG picks up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons and displays it as waves that differ in frequency and amplitude. Alert waking shows fast, low beta waves; relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows alpha waves (8 to 13 Hz); light sleep shows theta; deep slow-wave sleep shows large, slow delta waves; and REM sleep returns to fast, irregular, wake-like activity. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) used the EEG together with eye-movement recording to discover REM sleep. In a sleep laboratory the EEG is combined with the EOG (eye movements) and the EMG (muscle tone) in polysomnography. The EEG has excellent timing, in milliseconds, but poor spatial detail, and it is also the standard test for epilepsy. Option a is right because the EEG is the only listed procedure that records the brain's electrical activity. Exam trap: the ECG and EMG are also electrical recordings, and the EMG is even part of a sleep study, but they record the heart and the muscles, not brain waves.", "topic": "EEG and sleep recording", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is derived from the hemp plant \"Cannabis sativa\"?", "options": {"a": "Opium", "b": "Marijuana", "c": "MDMA", "d": "Crack"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: marijuana is derived from the hemp plant Cannabis sativa. Marijuana is the dried leaves and flowering tops of the plant, usually smoked or eaten. Its main psychoactive compound is delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), which acts on CB1 cannabinoid receptors in the brain. These receptors are dense in the hippocampus (hence the memory problems), the cerebellum and basal ganglia (hence the clumsiness) and the cortex. Hashish is the concentrated resin of the same plant, so it is stronger. In India the plant gives three familiar forms: bhang (leaves and seeds, often drunk at Holi), ganja (dried flowering tops) and charas (hand-rubbed resin, the Indian hashish). Effects include euphoria, relaxation, a slowed sense of time, increased appetite, red eyes and a fast heart rate; heavy use, especially in adolescence, is linked to poor memory, dependence and a raised risk of psychosis. DSM-5 treats cannabis as a substance class of its own, with cannabis use disorder and cannabis withdrawal. For example, Rohit, a college student who drank bhang at Holi, felt time stretching and laughed for hours; both effects come from THC. Option b is right because marijuana is the only option made from Cannabis sativa. Exam trap: opium and crack also come from plants, so 'derived from a plant' is not enough; you must match the plant: cannabis gives marijuana, hashish, bhang, ganja and charas; the opium poppy gives opium; the coca plant gives cocaine and crack.", "topic": "Cannabis and its products", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to ______ with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to ______ with age.", "options": {"a": "Increase, decrease", "b": "Increase, stay the same", "c": "Decrease, increase", "d": "Decrease, stay the same"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence", "fig": "Raymond Cattell and John Horn"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a method used to teach autistic children?", "options": {"a": "PECS", "b": "Braille", "c": "Taylor Frame", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS) is a method used to teach autistic children. Andy Bondy and Lori Frost (1985) developed it at the Delaware Autistic Program for children with autism who had little or no functional speech. PECS rests on applied behaviour analysis: the child learns to hand a picture card to a communication partner in exchange for the item shown, and getting that item is the reward. It moves through six phases: (1) the physical exchange of a single picture; (2) distance and persistence (going to the partner and to the picture book); (3) discriminating between pictures; (4) building a sentence strip ('I want' plus a picture); (5) answering 'What do you want?'; and (6) commenting ('I see', 'I hear'). Because the child must approach another person, PECS teaches initiation and the social nature of communication, a core difficulty in autism, and many children go on to develop some speech. It is a form of augmentative and alternative communication (AAC). Aarav, a four-year-old autistic boy who used to scream when hungry, learns to pick up a picture of a biscuit and give it to his teacher; the request replaces the scream. Option a is right because PECS was designed specifically for autistic children with communication difficulties. Exam trap: Braille and the Taylor frame are also special education methods and appear in the same lists, but they serve children with visual impairment, not autism.", "topic": "PECS for autism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is Spearman's rho?", "options": {"a": "Measure of correlation", "b": "Measure of dispersion", "c": "Measure of Normal probability", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: Spearman's rho is a measure of correlation. Charles Spearman (1904) introduced the rank-difference method, written with the Greek letter rho (ρ). It shows how closely two sets of ranks agree. Each person is ranked on both variables, the difference between each person's two ranks (D) is found, and ρ = 1 - 6ΣD² / N(N² - 1), where N is the number of pairs. Like Karl Pearson's r (1896), rho runs from -1 (perfect reversed agreement) through 0 (no relationship) to +1 (perfect agreement). Because it works on ranks, it suits ordinal data, small samples, relationships that rise or fall steadily but not in a straight line, and data with outliers; it is the usual non-parametric correlation. For example, if two teachers, Mr Sharma and Ms Iyer, rank the same ten essays and their orders nearly match, rho will be close to +1; if the essay one ranks first the other ranks last, and so on down the list, rho will be close to -1. Option a is right because rho is a correlation coefficient calculated on ranks. Exam trap: the formula contains squared differences, so students confuse it with variance or standard deviation; but those are measures of dispersion that describe the spread of one variable, while rho measures the association between two.", "topic": "Spearman's rank correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:", "options": {"a": "Primary, secondary, tertiary", "b": "Cognitive, emotional, physiological", "c": "Id, ego, superego", "d": "Cardinal, central, secondary"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport organised traits into cardinal, central and secondary traits. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make behaviour consistent across situations. He ranked a person's traits by how pervasive they are, and in Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he renamed these individual traits 'personal dispositions'. A cardinal trait dominates almost everything a person does, so much that the person becomes known by it, as in Machiavellian, narcissistic or Christlike; few people have one. Central traits are the 5 to 10 broad qualities that describe most people well, the words you would use in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious, anxious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent dispositions that appear only in particular situations, such as a food preference or irritability in traffic. For example, Neha's central traits might be friendly, hard-working and talkative, while a secondary trait is that she becomes nervous only when speaking on stage; she has no single cardinal trait. Allport studied individuals in depth (the idiographic approach), for instance in Letters from Jenny (1965). Option d is right because cardinal, central and secondary are Allport's own three levels. Exam trap: 'secondary' also appears in option a, which makes 'primary, secondary, tertiary' look familiar; but Allport never used primary or tertiary, and 'primary' belongs to Raymond Cattell's (1949) 16 primary (first-order) factors, a different model.", "topic": "Allport's levels of traits", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Match List I with List II. A. Carl Rogers, B. Albert Ellis, C. Eric Berne, D. Albert Bandura, E. Kurt Lewin. i. Token economy, ii. Phenomenological reality, iii. Disputing, iv. Life space, v. Rackets and games. Codes in the order A, B, C, D, E:", "options": {"a": "ii, iii, v, iv, i", "b": "i, ii, iii, iv, v", "c": "iv, iii, ii, i, v", "d": "v, iv, iii, ii, i"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the code ii, iii, v, iv, i is the intended matching. Learn each pair. Carl Rogers (1951) built person-centred therapy on phenomenology: each person reacts to the world as he or she perceives it, so the client's subjective perceptual field is his or her reality (phenomenological reality), and the therapist tries to see the world through the client's eyes. Albert Ellis (1955) founded rational emotive behaviour therapy on the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it and emotional Consequences; therapy adds D, Disputing irrational beliefs ('I must be perfect'), and E, the Effective new belief. Eric Berne (1964), in Games People Play, described transactional analysis: games are repeated ulterior transactions with a predictable bad payoff, and rackets are familiar substitute feelings a person collects to justify a life script. Kurt Lewin (1936) described the life space, the whole psychological field of person and environment at a given moment, in his formula B = f(P, E). The token economy, in which tokens earned for target behaviours are exchanged for rewards, was built by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) and belongs to the behaviour modification tradition that Albert Bandura (1969) surveyed in Principles of Behavior Modification. When a therapist challenges Riya's belief that one bad mark makes her a failure, that is Ellis's disputing. Option a is right because it is the only code that matches Rogers, Ellis and Berne correctly. Exam trap: check Rogers first; phenomenological reality is the hallmark of the humanistic approach, and that single pairing rules out b, c and d. Key note: the strictly correct matching is ii, iii, v, i, iv (Bandura with token economy, Lewin with life space), and no code prints it exactly; code a gets Rogers, Ellis and Berne right and only swaps the last two, while every other code gets Rogers wrong, so a is the intended answer and a stands.", "topic": "Therapists and key concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ is the description of the basic biological response to sustained and unrelenting physical stress.", "options": {"a": "General Adaptation syndrome", "b": "Diathesis-stress paradigm", "c": "Two factor theory", "d": "Stress Inoculation theory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the general adaptation syndrome (GAS) describes the body's basic biological response to sustained, unrelenting stress. Hans Selye (1936) noticed that rats injected with many different harmful substances, or exposed to cold, injury or forced exercise, all showed the same triad: enlarged adrenal glands, a shrunken thymus and lymph nodes, and bleeding stomach ulcers. The response was general (the same whatever the stressor), adaptive (it helps the body cope) and a syndrome (a set of signs that occur together). In The Stress of Life (1956) he described three stages. Alarm reaction: the body detects the threat, and the sympathetic nervous system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis release adrenaline and cortisol; resistance dips briefly, then rises. Resistance: if the stress continues, the body stays mobilised at a high level and seems to cope, but it is using up its reserves. Exhaustion: with prolonged stress the reserves run out, resistance collapses and 'diseases of adaptation' such as ulcers, high blood pressure and lowered immunity appear. For example, Arjun works night shifts for months while caring for his sick father: at first he feels wired and alert (alarm), then he manages for a while (resistance), then he falls ill again and again and feels drained (exhaustion). Option a is right because the GAS is exactly Selye's model of the physical response to prolonged stress. Exam trap: the GAS is purely biological and the same for every stressor; it ignores appraisal, which is why Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) proposed the transactional model, in which stress depends on how a person evaluates the demand.", "topic": "General adaptation syndrome", "fig": "Hans Selye"}, {"q": "In which of the following cases has learned helplessness theory been applied to depression?", "options": {"a": "Battered Woman Syndrome", "b": "Personality disorder", "c": "Substance misuse", "d": "Alcohol abuse"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: learned helplessness theory was applied to depression in the battered woman syndrome. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) found that dogs given inescapable electric shocks later failed to escape shocks they could easily have avoided by jumping a low barrier; they simply lay down. Lack of control, not the shock itself, taught them that nothing they did mattered. Seligman (1975) then proposed learned helplessness as a model of depression: after repeated uncontrollable bad events, people show passivity, low motivation, sadness and an inability to see that their actions could work. Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later added attributions: helplessness turns into depression when people blame internal, stable and global causes. Lenore Walker (1979), in The Battered Woman, used the theory to explain why many abused women do not leave violent partners. Beatings that come whatever the woman does teach her that her actions cannot stop the violence, so she becomes passive, depressed and unable to see ways out; Walker called this pattern the battered woman syndrome. She also described a cycle of violence: tension building, an acute battering incident, then loving contrition. Consider Meera, beaten by her husband whether she cooks, stays silent or argues; after years she stops trying, believes leaving is impossible and becomes deeply depressed, which is learned helplessness in human form. Option a is right because the battered woman syndrome is the best-known application of learned helplessness to human depression. Exam trap: substance misuse and alcohol abuse tempt because depressed people may drink, but those problems are explained mainly by reinforcement, tension-reduction and expectancy models, not by learned helplessness.", "topic": "Learned helplessness applications", "fig": "Lenore Walker"}, {"q": "The physical attractiveness of a person, or any other trait, can influence multiple judgements and lead to a more favourable view of the whole personality. This tendency is called:", "options": {"a": "Primacy effect", "b": "Pygmalion effect", "c": "Halo effect", "d": "Non-common effect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: when one attractive trait colours our judgement of the whole person, it is called the halo effect. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after studying how army officers rated their soldiers on physique, intelligence, leadership and character. The ratings correlated far too highly: a soldier judged handsome and well built was also rated more intelligent and more loyal, as if one good impression cast a glow over everything else. The halo is a general impression, or one striking trait, spreading to other, unrelated traits. Physical attractiveness is the strongest trigger. Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster (1972) showed students photographs of attractive, average and unattractive people; the attractive ones were judged kinder, more sociable, more successful and happier: 'what is beautiful is good'. The reverse, one bad trait dragging down the whole judgement, is sometimes called the horns effect. The halo also biases ratings by teachers, interviewers and clinicians, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. At a job interview, for example, Rohan is smart, confident and well dressed, and the panel also assumes he is honest and hard-working, although they have no evidence on those traits. Option c is right because the stem describes one trait shaping many unrelated judgements and a more favourable view of the whole personality. Exam trap: the Pygmalion effect also involves expectations about a person, but it concerns how those expectations change the person's actual performance; the halo effect is a bias in the perceiver's judgement only.", "topic": "Halo effect", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT considered to be a risk factor for personality disorders?", "options": {"a": "Living in inner cities", "b": "Low socioeconomic class", "c": "Gender", "d": "Being a young adult"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: gender is not counted as a risk factor for personality disorders as a whole. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, begins by adolescence or early adulthood and causes distress or impairment (DSM-5, 2013). About one adult in ten in community surveys meets the criteria for at least one. Epidemiological studies agree on several correlates. Age: rates are highest in younger adults and fall with age, especially for the dramatic, impulsive cluster B disorders such as antisocial and borderline, which tend to soften by middle life. Place: rates are higher among people living in inner cities, where poverty, crime, family breakdown and weak social support cluster. Social class: low income, unemployment and less education are consistently linked with higher rates. Gender is different. Specific disorders do differ: antisocial personality disorder is diagnosed far more often in men, and borderline personality disorder is diagnosed more often in women in clinics, although community rates are closer to equal. When all personality disorders are counted together, however, studies show no consistent difference between men and women, so gender is not listed as a general risk factor. Consider Rahul, a 24-year-old unemployed man living in a crowded inner-city area: he carries three of the listed risk markers (youth, place and class), while being male raises only his chance of the antisocial type in particular. Option c is right because youth, inner-city living and low socioeconomic class are established risk markers, whereas overall prevalence does not differ consistently by gender. Exam trap: the well-known sex ratios for antisocial (men) and borderline (women) tempt students to call gender a risk factor; those ratios apply to single disorders, not to personality disorders overall.", "topic": "Personality disorder risk factors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Behavioural Self-Control Training (BSCT) is based on conditioning principles. These include which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Stimulus control", "b": "Using rewards to reinforce abstinence", "c": "Learning to be aware of when and how frequently drug taking occurs", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: stimulus control, rewards for abstinence and self-monitoring are all parts of behavioural self-control training (BSCT). BSCT was developed in the 1970s by William Miller, with Ricardo Munoz (How to Control Your Drinking, 1976), for problem drinkers, often those aiming to cut down (controlled drinking) rather than stop, and it is used with other drugs too. It treats substance use as learned behaviour: situations become cues for drinking (classical conditioning) and the effects of the drink reinforce it (operant conditioning), so the client learns to rearrange the cues and the consequences. The main components are these. Goal setting: the client sets clear limits, such as no more than two drinks and none on weekdays, or chooses abstinence. Self-monitoring: a diary of when, where, with whom and how much drinking or drug use occurs, which makes patterns and triggers visible (option c). Stimulus control: changing the cues that set off use, for example avoiding certain bars, not keeping alcohol at home and drinking only with food (option a). Rate control: sipping, spacing drinks and alternating with soft drinks. Self-reinforcement: rewarding oneself for meeting the limits or staying abstinent (option b). Coping skills: refusal skills, other ways of handling stress, and relapse prevention. Kabir's diary shows that he drinks heavily every Friday with office friends; he starts meeting them at a cafe instead, keeps to two drinks on Saturdays, and buys himself a cricket ticket after a month within his limits. Option d is right because BSCT combines all three techniques in one package. Exam trap: each option is genuinely part of BSCT, so a candidate who recognises one component may pick it alone; 'all of the above' is correct when every statement is itself a component.", "topic": "Behavioural self-control training", "fig": ""}, {"q": "On what basis did Jean Piaget give his theory of cognitive development?", "options": {"a": "Evaluation Research", "b": "Fundamental Research", "c": "Applied Research", "d": "Action Research"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: Jean Piaget's theory of cognitive development rests on fundamental research. Fundamental (basic or pure) research seeks knowledge for its own sake: it aims to discover general principles and build theory, not to solve an immediate practical problem. Applied research, by contrast, uses knowledge to solve a specific practical problem. Jean Piaget (1936), trained as a biologist, wanted to answer a philosophical question: how does knowledge grow? He called this field genetic epistemology. While standardising reasoning tests in Paris around 1920, he became curious about the systematic wrong answers children gave. He then used the clinical method, a flexible questioning interview, with Swiss schoolchildren, and made detailed observations of his own three children, Jacqueline, Lucienne and Laurent, from infancy. From this he built a general theory: children actively construct knowledge through assimilation and accommodation and pass through four stages (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational). Nothing in this work aimed to fix a school, treat a child or judge a programme; it aimed to explain how thinking develops. Teachers later applied the theory (discovery learning, readiness), but that use was applied work built on Piaget's basic research. If Priya studies how children of different ages understand conservation of liquid, to learn how thinking develops, that is fundamental research; if she tests whether a new maths textbook improves Class 3 results in Pune, that is applied research. Option b is right because Piaget's aim was a general theory of how knowledge develops, the hallmark of fundamental research. Exam trap: because Piaget's ideas are used so widely in classrooms, students call his work applied research; the classroom use came later, while his own studies sought general understanding.", "topic": "Fundamental versus applied research", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "A researcher plans to approach counselors as a participant seeking their services and to record their sessions without the counselors' knowledge to assess their competence. What ethical violations should the researcher safeguard against?", "options": {"a": "Participant should be able to understand the reasonable need for deception", "b": "Protection of participants from harm", "c": "All of the above", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the researcher must guard against both problems: the deception must be justifiable to the participants, and the counsellors must be protected from harm. The plan raises three ethical issues: the counsellors are deceived (the 'client' is really a researcher), they have not consented to take part, and their sessions are recorded without their knowledge. The American Psychological Association's Ethics Code (2002, standard 8.07) allows deception only under strict conditions: the study must have significant scientific, educational or applied value; no effective non-deceptive method can be available; the deception must never concern anything likely to cause physical pain or severe emotional distress; and participants must be told the truth (debriefed) as early as feasible, preferably at the end of their participation, and allowed to withdraw their data. That is why option a matters: at debriefing, the counsellors should be able to see that the deception was reasonably needed, since a counsellor who knows he or she is being tested behaves differently. Option b matters too: the counsellors could suffer real harm, such as embarrassment, damage to their reputation or even loss of their jobs, if recordings that judged them incompetent were shared. Protection from harm here means keeping the recordings confidential, anonymising the results, storing the data securely and never letting the findings affect anyone's employment; because recording normally needs consent, the counsellors' permission to keep and use the recordings must be sought at debriefing. Option c is right because the plan raises both a justified-deception issue and a risk of harm, so both safeguards apply. Exam trap: many candidates see the word 'deception' and choose a alone, forgetting that the people being deceived are themselves research participants entitled to protection from harm.", "topic": "Deception and harm in research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A diagnostic formulation is:", "options": {"a": "More explanatory and longitudinal in nature, and attempts to offer a rationale for the development and maintenance of symptoms and dysfunctional life patterns", "b": "Descriptive statement about the nature and severity of the individual's psychiatric presentation", "c": "Serves as an explicit blueprint governing treatment interventions", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: a diagnostic formulation is a descriptive statement about the nature and severity of the individual's psychiatric presentation. In the scheme of Len Sperry and colleagues (1992), a full case formulation has several parts. The diagnostic formulation is descriptive and cross-sectional: it answers 'what is going on, and how severe is it?' by summarising the presenting symptoms, the mental status findings and the resulting diagnosis (ICD or DSM). The clinical formulation is explanatory and longitudinal: it offers a rationale for how the symptoms and maladaptive life patterns developed and why they persist, often organised by the five Ps (presenting problem plus predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors). The treatment formulation follows from both and serves as an explicit blueprint for intervention: goals, methods and the order of treatment. Tracy Eells (1997), in the Handbook of Psychotherapy Case Formulation, defines a case formulation as a hypothesis about the causes, precipitants and maintaining influences of a person's problems that guides treatment. Consider Arjun, 30, with six weeks of low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest. 'Moderate depressive episode, no psychotic features' is the diagnostic formulation; the account linking his perfectionism, his father's criticism and a recent job loss is the clinical formulation; and the plan of twelve sessions of CBT plus a medication review is the treatment formulation. Option b is right because the diagnostic formulation describes and classifies the presentation rather than explaining it or planning treatment. Exam trap: options a and c are genuine descriptions of formulation, so 'all of the above' looks safe; but they describe the explanatory (clinical) and treatment parts of a case formulation, not the diagnostic part.", "topic": "Diagnostic versus case formulation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 recognised the following about persons attempting suicide, in decriminalising attempted suicide:", "options": {"a": "Suffering being undergone by a person who attempts suicide", "b": "The need to provide persons who attempt suicide with care and consideration, instead of subjecting them to criminal prosecution", "c": "Presumption that persons attempting suicide are suffering from mental illness unless otherwise proven", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: in decriminalising attempted suicide, the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 recognised all three ideas. Before the Act, section 309 of the Indian Penal Code (1860) made attempted suicide an offence punishable with up to one year of simple imprisonment, a fine or both, so survivors could be prosecuted at the very moment they most needed help. Section 115(1) of the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 says that, notwithstanding section 309, any person who attempts suicide shall be presumed, unless proved otherwise, to have severe stress and shall not be tried and punished under the Code. Section 115(2) places a duty on the appropriate government to provide care, treatment and rehabilitation to such a person to reduce the risk of another attempt. These words carry the three ideas in the options: recognition of the suffering the person is going through (a), care and consideration instead of criminal prosecution (b), and a legal presumption that protects the person unless the contrary is proved (c). The Indian Penal Code itself has since been replaced by the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. For example, when Pooja, 19, survives an overdose after failing her exams, the hospital treats her and refers her for counselling instead of reporting her to the police for a case to be registered. Option d is right because the Act's approach rests on the person's suffering, a duty of care rather than punishment, and a protective presumption. Exam trap: students remember 'decriminalisation' but forget the presumption clause and the government's duty of care. Key note: the Act's actual words presume 'severe stress', not 'mental illness', so option c is loosely worded; the question intends that the Act recognised all three ideas (the suffering, care instead of prosecution, and a presumption that protects the person), and d stands.", "topic": "MHCA 2017 and attempted suicide", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The definition of mental illness in the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act (2016) includes:", "options": {"a": "Disorder of thinking, mood, perception, orientation or memory", "b": "Arrested or incomplete development of mind of a person, specially characterised by subnormality of intelligence", "c": "Mental conditions associated with the abuse of alcohol and drugs", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 defines mental illness as a disorder of thinking, mood, perception, orientation or memory. The Act replaced the Persons with Disabilities Act 1995, widened the recognised disabilities from 7 to 21, and lists mental illness as one of them. Its schedule defines mental illness as 'a substantial disorder of thinking, mood, perception, orientation or memory that grossly impairs judgment, behaviour, capacity to recognise reality or ability to meet the ordinary demands of life'. It then adds an exclusion: the term 'does not include retardation which is a condition of arrested or incomplete development of mind of a person, specially characterised by subnormality of intelligence'. Intellectual disability appears separately in the Act as a disability of its own, so it is not counted twice. A person with 40 percent or more of a specified disability has a 'benchmark disability', which opens reservations and other benefits. For example, Farhan, who has schizophrenia with hallucinations and poor judgement, falls under the RPwD category of mental illness, while his cousin with intellectual disability is covered under the separate category of intellectual disability. Option a is right because the five domains of thinking, mood, perception, orientation and memory form the core of the Act's definition. Exam trap: option b quotes the Act's own words, but they come from the exclusion clause, so a candidate who recognises the phrase may tick it. Key note: the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 definition adds 'mental conditions associated with the abuse of alcohol and drugs', which the RPwD definition does not; so c belongs to the MHCA, b is explicitly excluded, and a stands.", "topic": "RPwD Act definition of mental illness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled from the:", "options": {"a": "Frontal lobe", "b": "Spinal cord", "c": "Medulla", "d": "Pons"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a rapid, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your foot off a sharp stone or the knee jerk when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the circuit behind it, the reflex arc. The arc has five links: a receptor picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal into the spinal cord through the dorsal root; an interneuron in the grey matter passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out through the ventral root; and an effector, a muscle or gland, responds. The loop closes inside the cord, so the response starts before the message reaches the brain, which is why you feel the pain a fraction of a second after you have already moved. The knee-jerk (patellar) reflex is even simpler: it is monosynaptic, with the sensory neuron synapsing straight onto the motor neuron and no interneuron in between. Example: Kabir steps on a thorn while walking barefoot in the garden, and his leg lifts before he shouts; his spinal cord acted first and informed his brain afterwards. Option b is right because the classic limb and body reflexes are organised at the level of the spinal cord, without needing the brain. Exam trap: the medulla (c) does control some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting, as well as the vital rhythms of breathing and heart rate, so it looks attractive; but the word 'usually' points to the spinal cord, the centre for most reflexes in the body.", "topic": "Reflex arc", "fig": "Charles Sherrington"}, {"q": "What is a functional hallucination?", "options": {"a": "Stimuli in one sensory modality causing a hallucination in a different sensory modality", "b": "Stimuli in one sensory modality causing a hallucination in the same sensory modality", "c": "Both a & b", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a functional hallucination is one in which a stimulus in one sensory modality causes a hallucination in the same modality. A hallucination is normally a perception without any external object, experienced as real. In a functional hallucination, however, a real stimulus is present, and the hallucination occurs only while that stimulus lasts. The person perceives both at once: the normal perception and the false one, side by side. Frank Fish (1967), in Clinical Psychopathology, gives the classic example of a patient who hears voices only when the water tap is running; he hears the water as well as the voices, and the voices stop when the tap is turned off. Example: Meera, who has schizophrenia, reports that whenever the ceiling fan whirs she hears a man muttering insults. She can hear the fan clearly too, and the muttering vanishes when the fan is switched off. Both the trigger and the voice are auditory, so this is a functional hallucination. Compare it with an illusion, in which the real stimulus itself is misperceived (the fan's hum is heard as words); in a functional hallucination the real sound stays a normal sound and the voice is added on top. Option b is right because the trigger and the hallucination share the same sense, and both are experienced together. Exam trap: the reflex hallucination (option a) also needs a real stimulus, but the hallucination appears in a different modality, for example feeling a touch on the skin every time a door bangs, a kind of morbid synaesthesia. Examiners swap the two definitions, so remember: functional, same sense; reflex, another sense.", "topic": "Functional hallucination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is NOT correct in respect of speed tests?", "options": {"a": "A speed test evaluates the individual on the basis of the time taken to answer the items correctly", "b": "In a speed test, all the items are of different degree of difficulty", "c": "In a speed test, the test taker is required to answer all the items", "d": "In a speed test, all the items are of the same degree of difficulty"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the statement that all the items in a speed test are of different degrees of difficulty is NOT correct. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, classes tests by how they use time. A pure speed test has items that are uniformly easy, all of about the same low difficulty, so that anyone given enough time would get nearly all of them right. What separates people is how many items they finish correctly within a strict time limit, set so short that hardly anyone completes the test. A clerical checking test, where you mark which pairs of names are identical, or a letter-cancellation task is a typical example. A pure power test is the opposite: the items climb steadily in difficulty until some are too hard for anyone, and the time is generous or unlimited, so the score reflects the highest level a person can reach. Most real tests mix the two. Example: Arjun and Kabir take a symbol-matching test with 200 easy items in 3 minutes. Arjun finishes 140 and Kabir 110, and both make almost no errors, so the difference in their scores is speed, not knowledge. Option b is right because varied, rising difficulty is the mark of a power test, not a speed test. Exam trap: option c ('the test taker is required to answer all the items') sounds false because few people finish a speed test; but the instruction to attempt every item as fast as possible is part of how a speed test is given, so it is a stated feature, whereas b reverses the defining feature. Note too that split-half reliability is falsely inflated on speed tests, so test-retest or parallel forms are preferred.", "topic": "Speed versus power tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following would a client-centred therapist use?", "options": {"a": "Empathy", "b": "Desensitization", "c": "Racket system", "d": "Ego states"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a client-centred therapist would use empathy. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, built his approach (later called person-centred therapy) on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, which gets blocked when love and approval are given only on conditions of worth. In a famous 1957 paper Rogers set out the 'necessary and sufficient conditions' for therapeutic change, and three of them became the core conditions the therapist offers: congruence (being genuine), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance without judgement) and accurate empathic understanding (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding back). The therapist is non-directive: no interpretation, no advice, no homework. The main skill is reflection of feeling. Example: Riya says, 'Everyone at home thinks I'm lazy, but I'm just so tired all the time.' A client-centred counsellor replies, 'It sounds exhausting, and hurtful, to feel so drained and then be judged for it.' She is not diagnosing or teaching; she is mirroring Riya's experience so that Riya can hear herself more clearly and move towards her own solutions. Option a is right because empathy is one of the three core conditions at the heart of Rogers's therapy. Exam trap: the other options are named techniques or concepts from directive therapies, used as look-alikes: desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's behaviour therapy, while rackets and ego states belong to Eric Berne's transactional analysis. If an option is something the therapist does to the client, it is not client-centred.", "topic": "Rogers's core conditions", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Which hormone is responsible for sending signals to the brain that you need food?", "options": {"a": "Glucagon", "b": "Ghrelin", "c": "Oxytocin", "d": "Leptin"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: ghrelin is the hormone that sends the brain the signal that you need food. Ghrelin was discovered by Masayasu Kojima and colleagues (1999). It is made mainly by cells in the lining of the stomach; its blood level rises before meals, when the stomach is empty, and falls after eating. It travels to the hypothalamus, where it acts on the arcuate nucleus, exciting neurons that release neuropeptide Y and agouti-related peptide, which start eating. For this reason it is called the 'hunger hormone', and injected ghrelin makes people and animals eat more. Its partner is leptin, discovered by Jeffrey Friedman's team (1994): leptin is released by fat cells in proportion to body fat and tells the hypothalamus that energy stores are full, so it reduces appetite over the long term. Mice that cannot make leptin (ob/ob mice) become hugely obese. Example: Arjun skips lunch during exam revision, and by 4 pm his ghrelin has climbed and he cannot stop thinking about samosas. After he eats, ghrelin drops, and gut signals such as cholecystokinin and stomach stretch help him feel full. Option b is right because ghrelin is the circulating hunger signal sent from the empty stomach to the brain. Exam trap: leptin (d) is also an appetite hormone, so students mix the two up; but leptin is the satiety signal from fat, the opposite message. A memory aid: ghrelin makes the stomach 'growl'; leptin helps you stay 'lean'.", "topic": "Hunger hormones", "fig": ""}, {"q": "'Multiple intelligence theory' was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Terman", "b": "Gardner", "c": "Binet", "d": "Guilford"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: multiple intelligence theory was proposed by Howard Gardner (1983) in his book Frames of Mind. Gardner argued that intelligence is not one general ability that a single IQ score can capture, but a set of relatively independent abilities, each with its own brain systems. He used eight criteria to decide what counts as an intelligence, including that it can be selectively destroyed by brain damage, that it shows up in prodigies and savants, and that it has its own developmental history. His original list had seven intelligences: linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal (understanding other people) and intrapersonal (understanding oneself). In Intelligence Reframed (1999) he added an eighth, naturalist (recognising and classifying plants, animals and patterns in nature), and discussed a possible existential intelligence. Example: Kabir struggles with algebra, but he can play a raga on the flute after hearing it once and is the team captain everyone trusts. Gardner would say Kabir is high in musical and interpersonal intelligence, strengths that an ordinary IQ test would miss. Option b is right because Gardner is the author of the theory of multiple intelligences. Exam trap: Joy Paul Guilford (d) also rejected a single general factor and proposed many abilities in his Structure of Intellect model (1967), so his name is the near miss; but 'multiple intelligences' is Gardner's term. Lewis Terman and Alfred Binet built intelligence tests that give a single overall score.", "topic": "Multiple intelligences", "fig": "Howard Gardner"}, {"q": "Language deficits are among the most common features of neurological disorders, and are collectively known as:", "options": {"a": "Dysphasia", "b": "Alogias", "c": "Anomias", "d": "Aphasias"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: language deficits caused by neurological disorders are collectively known as aphasias. Aphasia is an acquired disorder of language, the ability to produce or understand spoken and written words, caused by damage to the language areas of the brain, usually in the left hemisphere after a stroke, head injury, tumour or dementia. It is not a weakness of the speech muscles (that is dysarthria), and it is not a lifelong developmental delay. Paul Broca (1861) described patients with damage to the left inferior frontal lobe who spoke slowly, with great effort and few words, but understood well: Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia. Carl Wernicke (1874) described damage to the left posterior superior temporal lobe giving fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Other types include conduction aphasia (poor repetition), global aphasia (loss of almost all language) and anomic aphasia (mainly word-finding difficulty). Example: after a stroke, Meera's grandfather understands everything said to him but can only manage 'tea... want... now'; this is Broca's aphasia. Option d is right because 'aphasias' is the umbrella term for the whole family of language disorders that follow neurological damage. Exam trap: dysphasia (a) means a partial or milder loss of language and is used as a synonym in some British texts, so it is the near miss; but the standard collective term in neuropsychology is aphasia. Anomia (c) is only one type, and alogia (b) is a negative symptom of schizophrenia.", "topic": "Aphasia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ is NOT a common data collection method in qualitative research.", "options": {"a": "Participant observation", "b": "Structured interview", "c": "Content Analysis", "d": "Focus groups"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the structured interview is NOT a common data collection method in qualitative research. Qualitative research aims to understand meanings, experiences and context in depth, in participants' own words, and its data are words, images and observations rather than numbers. Its usual methods are flexible and open: participant observation, where the researcher joins a group and records what happens (as in ethnography); in-depth unstructured or semi-structured interviews, which follow a loose guide and let participants lead; focus groups, guided discussions among about 6 to 10 people; and the analysis of documents, diaries, media and other texts. A structured interview, by contrast, asks every respondent the same fixed questions in the same order, often with fixed response options, so that answers can be coded and counted. It is really a spoken questionnaire, the tool of surveys and epidemiological studies, and it belongs to quantitative research. Example: Kabir is studying how new mothers in Pune experience postpartum depression. Sitting in support groups, holding open-ended interviews and running a focus group all fit his qualitative aim; reading out a fixed checklist with yes or no answers would give him numbers, not stories. Option b is right because fixed questions and fixed answers suit measurement, not the open exploration that qualitative work needs. Exam trap: content analysis (c) can be quantitative (counting words or themes) as well as qualitative, so students think it is the odd one out; but it is a standard way to analyse qualitative text and documents, so it is a common qualitative method.", "topic": "Qualitative data collection", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The 16PF test is based on:", "options": {"a": "Eysenck's theory", "b": "Cattell's theory", "c": "Allport's theory", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the 16PF is based on Raymond Cattell's theory. Raymond Cattell, a British-born psychologist who worked in the United States, built his trait theory by factor analysis, a statistical method that finds the few underlying factors that explain the correlations among many measures. He started from the trait words Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, merged synonyms, and factor-analysed three kinds of data: L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). He separated surface traits, clusters of behaviour that go together, from source traits, the deeper underlying causes. He found 16 source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire, the 16PF (1949), such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance and liveliness, each scored as a bipolar dimension. Factor-analysing the 16 again gives broader second-order (global) factors such as extraversion and anxiety. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and liveliness but low emotional stability, which helps her counsellor see a sociable young woman who is easily upset under pressure. Option b is right because Cattell designed the 16PF to measure the source traits of his own factor-analytic theory. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (a) also used factor analysis, but he arrived at only three broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); Gordon Allport (c) preferred the idiographic study of the individual and did not build the 16PF.", "topic": "16PF and Cattell", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Sleep spindles and K-complexes can be seen in which stage of sleep?", "options": {"a": "Stage 1", "b": "Stage 2", "c": "Stage 3 & 4", "d": "REM Sleep"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: sleep spindles and K-complexes are the signature of stage 2 sleep. Sleep is staged from the EEG (electroencephalogram), the record of the brain's electrical waves. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep, and the manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968) set out stages 1 to 4 plus REM; the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007) now calls them N1, N2, N3 and REM, merging stages 3 and 4 into N3. Awake and relaxed with eyes closed, the EEG shows alpha waves. Stage 1 (N1) is light, drowsy sleep with theta waves, vertex sharp waves and often hypnic jerks. Stage 2 (N2) is true sleep with a theta background plus two hallmarks: sleep spindles, brief bursts of 12 to 14 Hz waves lasting about half a second or more, and K-complexes, single large, sharp waves that are often set off by a sound. Stage 2 fills about half of a night's sleep. Stages 3 and 4 (N3) are deep, slow-wave sleep with large delta waves. REM sleep shows a fast, low-amplitude EEG like waking, rapid eye movements, vivid dreams and loss of muscle tone. Example: in a sleep laboratory, Arjun's trace shows a burst of spindles and then a large K-complex when a corridor door clicks shut; the technician scores that stretch of the record as stage 2. Option b is right because spindles and K-complexes define stage 2. Exam trap: spindles can also appear briefly in deeper sleep, so c tempts some students; but stages 3 and 4 are defined by delta waves, and stage 1 (theta, vertex waves) has neither feature.", "topic": "Sleep stages and EEG", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who is the person behind the rise of the 'mental hygiene movement'?", "options": {"a": "Dorothea Dix", "b": "Pinel", "c": "Hippocrates", "d": "Plato"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: among these options, Dorothea Dix is the person behind the rise of the mental hygiene movement. Dorothea Dix (1802 to 1887) was a Boston schoolteacher who, in 1841, went to teach a Sunday class at a jail in East Cambridge, Massachusetts, and found mentally ill people kept in cold, filthy cells alongside criminals. Over the following decades she toured jails and almshouses, recorded the conditions in detail and presented the evidence to state legislatures, beginning with her Memorial to the Legislature of Massachusetts (1843). Her lobbying led to the founding or enlargement of dozens of state mental hospitals in the United States, and she carried the campaign to Canada and Scotland. Abnormal psychology textbooks teach her work as part of the mental hygiene movement: the push for humane, public, medical care of the mentally ill instead of neglect and punishment. In hindsight, many of the large hospitals she helped build later became overcrowded custodial institutions. Option a is right because Dix is the reformer among the options whose campaigns are taught under the mental hygiene movement. Exam trap: Philippe Pinel (b) is the other famous reformer, but he belongs to moral treatment in France, when he removed the chains from patients at Bicetre in 1793; examiners rely on students merging the two reform movements. Key note: the organised mental hygiene movement itself was founded by Clifford Beers, a former patient whose book A Mind That Found Itself (1908) led to the Connecticut Society for Mental Hygiene (1908) and the National Committee for Mental Hygiene (1909), with Adolf Meyer suggesting the name 'mental hygiene'. Beers is not an option, and standard textbooks (for example James Butcher, Susan Mineka and Jill Hooley) teach Dix's campaigning under the same movement, so a stands.", "topic": "Mental hygiene movement", "fig": "Dorothea Dix"}, {"q": "X believes there is one general intelligence that overlaps with many abilities. Which of the following theorists is most likely to agree with X?", "options": {"a": "Edward L. Thorndike", "b": "Charles Spearman", "c": "Alfred Binet", "d": "Lewis Terman"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Charles Spearman is the theorist most likely to agree with X. Charles Spearman (1904) noticed that schoolchildren's scores on very different tasks, such as classics, French, mathematics and pitch discrimination, were all positively correlated: a child who did well on one tended to do well on the others. To explain this pattern he devised an early form of factor analysis and proposed his two-factor theory, set out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Every mental task draws on g, a general intelligence common to all tasks, which he likened to a kind of mental energy, plus s, a specific factor unique to that task. Tasks such as reasoning and analogies load heavily on g, while simple sensory tasks depend mostly on s. This is exactly X's view: one general ability that overlaps with, and runs beneath, many separate abilities. Example: Meera tops her class in mathematics, Hindi and science, and also picks up chess quickly. A Spearman-style explanation is that her high g feeds all these performances, while her particular flair for chess adds a specific s. Option b is right because the idea of a single general intelligence running through all abilities is Spearman's g. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike (a) is the classic opponent of g; his multifactor theory held that intelligence is many separate abilities (he described abstract, mechanical and social intelligence in 1920) with no single general factor, so he would disagree with X. Alfred Binet and Lewis Terman built tests that give one overall score, which tempts students, but the theory of g itself is Spearman's.", "topic": "Spearman's g factor", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Who first attempted to describe personality traits in terms of lexical descriptors?", "options": {"a": "Ross Stagner", "b": "Allport and Odbert", "c": "R. B. Cattell", "d": "Costa and McCrae"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) first attempted to describe personality traits through lexical descriptors. Their starting point was the lexical hypothesis: the differences between people that matter in daily life will have been noticed and named in ordinary language, so the dictionary is a store of trait terms, and the more important a trait, the more words a language has for it. Francis Galton (1884) had sketched the idea by estimating the number of character words in a thesaurus, but Allport and Odbert carried out the first systematic search. In their monograph Trait-Names: A Psycho-Lexical Study, they went through Webster's New International Dictionary and culled 17,953 words that distinguish one person's behaviour from another's. They sorted these into four lists: only about 4,500 named stable, consistent traits (sociable, honest); the rest described temporary states (flustered), evaluations (admirable) or physical features and abilities. Raymond Cattell then reduced the stable-trait list to 171 terms and, by factor analysis, to 16 source traits, and later lexical research led to the Big Five. Example: when Kabir's friends describe him as 'generous', 'reliable' and 'shy', each word is a lexical descriptor, the raw material Allport and Odbert collected. Option b is right because their 1936 dictionary study was the first major lexical description of traits. Exam trap: Raymond Cattell (c) is closely linked with the lexical approach, but he worked from Allport and Odbert's list afterwards; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (d) came decades later with the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and the five-factor model.", "topic": "Lexical hypothesis", "fig": "Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert"}, {"q": "What is the name of the conceptual framework within which research is carried out?", "options": {"a": "Research hypothesis", "b": "Synopsis of research", "c": "Research paradigm", "d": "Research design"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the conceptual framework within which research is carried out is called a research paradigm. The term comes from Thomas Kuhn (1962), The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, who used it for the shared set of assumptions, accepted theories, methods and standard problems that a scientific community works within during a period of 'normal science'. When unexplained findings pile up, a scientific revolution brings a 'paradigm shift'. In research methods, a paradigm is the researcher's basic worldview, made up of beliefs about what reality is (ontology), how it can be known (epistemology) and which methods suit it (methodology). The main paradigms are positivism and post-positivism (an objective reality measured by experiments and statistics), interpretivism or constructivism (reality as the meanings people construct, studied through interviews and observation) and the critical paradigm (research aimed at exposing power and changing society); pragmatism underlies mixed methods. Example: Riya wants to study exam stress in nursing students. Working in the positivist paradigm, she would measure cortisol levels and stress-scale scores; working in the interpretivist paradigm, she would interview students about what exams mean to them. The paradigm frames every later choice she makes. Option c is right because a paradigm is the overarching conceptual framework that guides the questions, design and methods of research. Exam trap: research design (d) sounds similar, but it is the specific plan of a single study (experimental, correlational, cross-sectional), chosen within a paradigm, not the framework itself.", "topic": "Research paradigm", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A three-year-old girl calls every dog she encounters \"Rover\" (the name of the family's pet dog). This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Over-emphasis", "b": "Over-extension", "c": "Over-stimulation", "d": "Babbling"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: calling every dog 'Rover' is an example of over-extension. Over-extension is an early error in word meaning in which a child applies a word to a wider set of things than adults do, usually on the basis of shared features such as shape, size, movement or sound. Eve Clark (1973) explained it with her semantic feature hypothesis: the child's first meaning for a word holds only one or two features (four legs, furry), so anything with those features gets the word. Over-extensions are most common in the second year and the early part of the third, when vocabulary is small; children call all men 'daddy', all round things 'ball', or the moon a 'ball'. The opposite error is under-extension, using a word too narrowly, such as using 'dog' only for the family pet and refusing it for other dogs, or 'car' only for the family car. Both fade as children learn more words and the contrasts between them, for instance once they learn 'dog' as a category name and 'Rover' as one dog's name. Option b is right because the girl stretches a name learned for one dog to cover all dogs, using the word more broadly than its adult meaning. Exam trap: over-regularisation, saying 'goed' or 'mouses', is a different error, applying a grammatical rule too widely, and examiners sometimes offer it in place of over-extension. Here what is stretched is a word's meaning, not a grammar rule.", "topic": "Over-extension in language", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to the trichromatic theory of colour vision, which of the following are the three types of cones?", "options": {"a": "Red, yellow and blue", "b": "Red, blue and green", "c": "Yellow, blue and green", "d": "Yellow, red and blue"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: according to the trichromatic theory, the three types of cones are red, blue and green. Thomas Young (1802) proposed that the eye needs only three kinds of colour receptor, and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) developed the idea through colour-matching experiments, showing that a person with normal vision can match any colour by mixing three lights of different wavelengths; hence the name Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory. Modern studies of the retina confirmed it: there are three types of cone, each with a different photopigment, most sensitive to short wavelengths (around 420 nanometres, seen as blue), medium wavelengths (around 530, green) or long wavelengths (around 560, towards red). Every colour we see is coded by the ratio of activity across the three cone types. The theory explains colour blindness well: a person missing the long or medium cones (protanopia or deuteranopia) confuses reds and greens. Example: Arjun's phone screen shows a yellow banana using only its red and green pixels lit together; his long and medium cones fire together, and his brain reads the mixture as yellow. Option b is right because the three cone types correspond to red, green and blue. Exam trap: red, yellow and blue (a and d) are the painter's primaries, based on subtractive mixing of pigments, not the eye's receptors. Yellow also appears in Ewald Hering's opponent-process theory (1878), which pairs red with green, blue with yellow and black with white; that pairing describes later neural processing beyond the cones, not the cone types themselves.", "topic": "Trichromatic theory", "fig": "Thomas Young and Hermann von Helmholtz"}, {"q": "A researcher presents his participant with a series of flashing lights at varying intervals. After each presentation of a light, he asks the participant to fully describe her internal experiences. This method is known as:", "options": {"a": "Functionalism", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Behaviourism", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: asking a participant to describe her inner experience after each flash of light is the method of introspection. Introspection means 'looking inward': a person observes and reports the contents of her own conscious experience. Wilhelm Wundt, who founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, used a controlled form of it that he called internal perception: trained observers received a precisely timed stimulus, such as a light, a tone or a metronome beat, and immediately reported their sensations and feelings, often alongside reaction-time measures. Edward Titchener (1898), who built structuralism at Cornell, made the method stricter still: observers trained over many trials described only the raw elements of experience (quality, intensity, duration, clearness) and avoided the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead of the experience. The aim was to break consciousness down into its basic elements, much as a chemist analyses compounds. Example: Meera, a trained observer, sees a flash and reports 'a small bright patch, slightly yellowish, fading quickly, with a brief feeling of tension'. She does not say 'a bulb', which would be the stimulus error. Option b is right because the researcher is collecting the participant's own reports of her internal experience under controlled stimulation, which is introspection. Exam trap: functionalism (a) sometimes used introspection too, but it is a school (William James, John Dewey) defined by asking what the mind is for, not a method; behaviourism (c), launched by John Watson (1913), rejected introspection, and psychoanalysis (d) used free association to reach the unconscious.", "topic": "Introspection", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The laws of perceptual organisation were proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Max Wertheimer", "b": "Wolfgang Kohler", "c": "Kurt Koffka", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the laws of perceptual organisation came from all three founders of Gestalt psychology, Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka. Max Wertheimer (1912) launched the school with his study of the phi phenomenon, the apparent motion seen when two lights flash one after the other, with Kohler and Koffka as his observers. The Gestalt message was that the whole is different from the sum of its parts: we perceive organised forms, not a mosaic of separate sensations, a direct challenge to the elements of Wilhelm Wundt and Edward Titchener. Wertheimer set out the grouping laws in a 1923 paper: proximity (near things group together), similarity (alike things group), closure (gaps are filled to make a complete figure), good continuation (smooth lines are followed), common fate (things moving together group) and the master law of Pragnanz, that we see the simplest, most stable organisation the conditions allow. Kurt Koffka spread these ideas to English readers and wrote Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935); Wolfgang Kohler added the field theory of the brain and his insight studies with chimpanzees (The Mentality of Apes, German edition 1917). Example: Kabir looks at a row of six dots and sees three pairs, because the dots in each pair sit closer together; that is the law of proximity. Option d is right because the Gestalt laws are taught as the joint work of the founding trio, with Wertheimer as the leader. Exam trap: examiners offer Wertheimer alone (a), since he wrote the 1923 laws paper; but with all three founders listed, 'all of the above' is the expected answer.", "topic": "Gestalt laws of organisation", "fig": "Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka"}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders is commonly associated with an \"overvalued idea\"?", "options": {"a": "Anorexia Nervosa", "b": "Gender Identity disorder", "c": "Borderline-Personality disorder", "d": "Histrionic Personality disorder"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: anorexia nervosa is the disorder most commonly associated with an overvalued idea. An overvalued idea, a term introduced by Carl Wernicke (1892), is an understandable, socially acceptable belief that comes to dominate a person's life. It is held with intense emotion and pursued far beyond reason, but, unlike a delusion, it is not held with absolute, unshakeable certainty, and it is not culturally strange: others can see why the person might think it. Unlike an obsession, it is not felt as senseless or resisted; the person agrees with it. In anorexia nervosa, the dread of fatness and the belief that one must be thin (Gerald Russell's 'morbid fear of becoming fat', 1970) is the classic overvalued idea: slimness is valued in the culture, but the patient pursues it to the point of starvation. Other examples are the preoccupation with a minor bodily flaw in body dysmorphic disorder, morbid jealousy and some hypochondriacal beliefs. Example: Riya, 17, weighs far less than is healthy for her height, yet says, 'If I eat a full meal I will become fat, and being fat would make me worthless.' She can discuss other views and is not deluded about the world, but the idea of thinness governs her days. Option a is right because the fear of fatness in anorexia is the standard textbook example of an overvalued idea. Exam trap: overvalued ideas, delusions and obsessions are easily confused; the differences are conviction (less fixed than a delusion) and ownership without resistance (unlike an obsession).", "topic": "Overvalued idea", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The trait approach to personality asserts that traits are:", "options": {"a": "Adjectives describing behaviour", "b": "Discrete and discontinuous categories of people", "c": "Generalised systems which ensure consistency in an individual's behaviour", "d": "Psychological categories of temperament associated with specific body physique"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the trait approach holds that traits are generalised systems that make a person's behaviour consistent. Gordon Allport (1937), the founder of trait psychology, defined a trait as a 'generalized and focalized neuropsychic system', peculiar to the individual, with the capacity to render many stimuli functionally equivalent and to initiate and guide consistent forms of behaviour. Put simply, a trait is a real, lasting disposition inside the person that makes many different situations mean the same thing to her, so she responds to them in similar ways. Traits are continuous dimensions on which everyone can be placed, from low to high, rather than boxes. Allport grouped them as cardinal (one ruling trait that shapes almost everything), central (the 5 to 10 traits that describe someone well) and secondary (narrow preferences that appear only in some settings). Later trait theorists, such as Raymond Cattell, Hans Eysenck and the Big Five researchers, kept the same core idea: stable, general dispositions explain consistency across time and situations. Example: Meera is friendly at college, at a wedding and on a crowded train; her trait of sociability turns all three settings into 'chances to talk', so her behaviour is consistent across them. Option c is right because Allport's definition says exactly that traits are generalised systems guiding consistent behaviour. Exam trap: option b describes the type approach (for example, introvert and extravert as separate kinds of people), which uses discrete categories; trait theory instead uses continuous dimensions. Option d describes body-build theories, a form of type theory.", "topic": "Allport's definition of trait", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "A developmental psychologist is investigating movement abnormalities as an early symptom of autism. Her research team is collecting data about crawling techniques from videotapes of 9-month-olds. To increase standardisation of data collection, she should:", "options": {"a": "Provide clear definitions of each crawling style with videotaped examples", "b": "Expand her study to include 12-month-olds", "c": "Collect data only on children with clinically significant crawling deficiencies", "d": "Have her research team collect data in person instead of watching videotapes"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: to increase standardisation, she should provide clear definitions of each crawling style with videotaped examples. In observational research, several coders watch behaviour and sort it into categories. The danger is that each coder applies a private idea of what, say, 'commando crawling' or 'bottom shuffling' means, so the same baby is coded differently by different people. The remedy is a coding scheme built on operational definitions, which state each category in terms of exactly what can be seen (for example, 'belly on the floor, arms pull the body forward, legs trail'), backed by a coding manual with model video clips and by training sessions until coders agree. Agreement is then checked as inter-rater (inter-observer) reliability: two coders rate the same tapes independently, and their agreement is calculated as a percentage or as Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects for agreement expected by chance. Periodic re-checks guard against observer drift, the slow shift of coders away from the definitions over time. Example: two research assistants, Arjun and Kabir, disagree on a third of the clips at first; after training with the definition sheet and example videos, they agree on over 90 percent, and the data become standardised. Option a is right because precise definitions with examples make every coder apply the same rules, which is what standardising data collection means. Exam trap: option d (live observation) sounds more natural, but it removes the chance to replay and recheck behaviour and adds reactivity, so it lowers, not raises, standardisation. Options b and c change the sample, not the measurement procedure.", "topic": "Observer reliability and coding", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who discovered that not all mental processes are accompanied by mental imagery?", "options": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt", "b": "E. B. Titchener", "c": "J. B. Watson", "d": "Oswald Kulpe"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Oswald Kulpe discovered that not all mental processes are accompanied by mental imagery. Oswald Kulpe (1862 to 1915), once Wilhelm Wundt's assistant at Leipzig, moved to Wurzburg in 1894 and built the Wurzburg school there. With students such as Karl Marbe, Narziss Ach and Karl Buhler, he pushed introspection into areas Wundt had ruled out of the laboratory: judgement, thinking and will. In their experiments (about 1901 to 1909) observers solved a task, such as judging which of two weights was heavier or giving an associated word, and then described exactly what had passed through their minds. Many reported that the decisive step of the thought came without any image or sensation, only vague 'conscious attitudes' such as doubt or certainty. This was imageless thought. Ach showed that the instruction, or task (Aufgabe), sets up a determining tendency that steers thinking before the stimulus even appears. Example: when Riya is asked 'Is a mango a fruit?' and answers 'yes', she can find no picture or word in her mind that produced the judgement; the answer simply arrived. The Wurzburg school would call this imageless thought. Option d is right because Kulpe and his Wurzburg group were the ones who reported thought without imagery. Exam trap: Edward Titchener (b) is the near miss because he was part of the famous debate, but on the other side: defending structuralism, he insisted every thought has sensory or imaginal content and argued that the Wurzburg observers had simply missed faint images. The unresolved quarrel helped discredit introspection as a method.", "topic": "Imageless thought, Wurzburg school", "fig": "Oswald Kulpe"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a finding from Harlow's monkey experiments?", "options": {"a": "Attachment was not primarily about hunger or thirst; it could not be reduced to nursing", "b": "Contact comfort aided normal development and was a protective factor against stress reactions", "c": "Impact of early maternal deprivation could be reversed in the equivalent of six months for humans", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of the above, because all three statements are findings from Harry Harlow's monkey experiments. Harry Harlow (1958), in The Nature of Love, raised infant rhesus monkeys with two surrogate 'mothers': one made of bare wire that held a milk bottle and one covered in soft terry cloth that gave no milk. The infants fed from the wire mother but spent most of their time clinging to the cloth one, so attachment could not be reduced to feeding. This contradicted the drive-reduction view that a baby loves its mother because she satisfies hunger (statement a). When a frightening object, such as a mechanical drumming bear, was introduced, or the infants were placed in a strange open room, they ran to the cloth mother, calmed down and then explored; without her they froze, cried and crouched (Harlow and Robert Zimmermann, 1959). Contact comfort worked as a secure base and a buffer against stress (statement b). Later isolation studies showed that monkeys kept alone for about the first three months, roughly the equivalent of six months of human infancy, recovered fairly well once placed with peers, whereas 6 to 12 months of isolation caused lasting social, sexual and maternal damage (statement c). Option d is right because a, b and c are all reported findings, so none of them is 'not a finding'. Exam trap: students pick c because they remember that early deprivation caused permanent harm. Key note: the reversibility in c holds only for short isolation (about 90 days); longer isolates were later only partly rehabilitated by Stephen Suomi and Harlow (1972) using younger 'therapist' monkeys. Textbooks summarise the short-isolation result as c states, so d stands.", "topic": "Harlow's surrogate mothers", "fig": "Harry Harlow"}, {"q": "A mental disorder is conceptualised as a ______ significant behavioural or psychological syndrome.", "options": {"a": "Physically", "b": "Psychologically", "c": "Clinically", "d": "Systematically"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: a mental disorder is conceptualised as a clinically significant behavioural or psychological syndrome. This wording comes from the American Psychiatric Association's Diagnostic and Statistical Manual. DSM-IV (1994) defined a mental disorder as 'a clinically significant behavioral or psychological syndrome or pattern' associated with present distress, disability, or a significantly increased risk of suffering death, pain, disability or an important loss of freedom. DSM-5 (2013) updated it to a syndrome characterised by clinically significant disturbance in an individual's cognition, emotion regulation or behaviour that reflects a dysfunction in the psychological, biological or developmental processes underlying mental functioning, usually associated with significant distress or disability in social, occupational or other important activities. Two exclusions follow: an expectable or culturally approved response to a common stressor or loss, such as grief after a death, is not a disorder; and socially deviant behaviour (political, religious or sexual) or conflict with society is not a disorder unless it results from a dysfunction in the individual. 'Clinically significant' means severe enough to cause real distress or impairment, the threshold that separates a disorder from ordinary sadness, worry or eccentricity. Example: Kabir feels low for two days after failing a test and then recovers; that is not clinically significant. If his low mood lasted weeks and he stopped attending college, it would be. Option c is right because 'clinically significant' is the phrase the DSM uses. Exam trap: 'psychologically' (b) feels natural because the syndrome is psychological, but the definition already says 'behavioural or psychological syndrome'; the qualifier being tested is 'clinically'.", "topic": "DSM definition of mental disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A procedure that establishes a sequence of responses, which lead to a reward following the final response, by working backwards from the final response is called:", "options": {"a": "Shaping", "b": "Stimulus Control", "c": "Chaining", "d": "Stimulus generalization"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: building a sequence of responses by working backwards from the final, rewarded response is called chaining, here backward chaining. A behaviour chain is a series of responses in which each step produces the cue (discriminative stimulus) for the next, and only the last step earns the main reward. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, described how such chains hold together: each stimulus in the chain is both a conditioned reinforcer for the response before it and a signal for the response after it. In backward chaining the trainer first teaches the final link, which is followed at once by the reward; then adds the second-to-last link, whose completion leads into the already learned final step; and so on back to the first link. Because the learner always finishes with success and reward, the method suits young children and people with intellectual disability. Forward chaining starts from the first step instead, and total task presentation practises all the steps every time. Example: to teach Aarav, a child with autism, to tie his shoelaces, the therapist does every step except the final pull, lets Aarav complete it and praises him. Once that is easy, Aarav does the last two steps, then three, until he ties the whole lace himself. Option c is right because chaining links responses into a sequence, and the stem describes its backward form. Exam trap: shaping (a) also builds new behaviour step by step, but it reinforces successive approximations to a single target response, not a sequence of different responses linked together.", "topic": "Backward chaining", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the use of factorial analysis?", "options": {"a": "For setting the hypothesis", "b": "To understand the difference between two variables", "c": "To understand the relationship between two variables", "d": "To understand the difference between various variables"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: factor analysis is used to understand the relationship between variables. Factor analysis is a statistical method that starts from the correlations among many measured variables and asks whether a smaller number of hidden dimensions, called factors, can account for them. Variables that correlate highly with one another are grouped onto the same factor, and each variable's correlation with a factor is its factor loading. Charles Spearman (1904) invented the method when he noticed that children's marks in quite different school subjects all correlated positively; he explained the pattern with one general factor, g. Louis Thurstone (1931 to 1947) developed multiple factor analysis and found several primary mental abilities instead of one g, and Raymond Cattell (1949) used it to reduce thousands of trait words to the 16 source traits of the 16PF. Exploratory factor analysis looks for the structure; confirmatory factor analysis tests whether a structure proposed in advance fits the data. Suppose Meera gives 200 students ten tests: vocabulary, reading, analogies, arithmetic, number series and so on. If the verbal tests correlate strongly with each other and the number tests with each other, factor analysis will reveal a verbal factor and a numerical factor. Option c is right because factor analysis works on relationships (correlations) among variables, not on differences between group means. Exam trap: the word \"factorial\" pulls students towards d, which describes a factorial ANOVA, a design that tests mean differences across two or more independent variables (factors) and their interaction, built on Ronald Fisher's (1925) analysis of variance. Key note: factor analysis studies the pattern of relationships among many variables at once, so \"two variables\" is narrower than the method really is; c is still the only option about relationships, which is what factor analysis analyses, and c stands.", "topic": "Purpose of factor analysis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Piaget's stage for infancy is:", "options": {"a": "Formal operation", "b": "Preoperational thought", "c": "Sensorimotor thought", "d": "Concrete operations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, infancy is the stage of sensorimotor thought. Jean Piaget (1936), a Swiss psychologist who observed his own three children in close detail and described their first two years in The Origins of Intelligence in Children, proposed four stages of cognitive development, each a qualitatively different way of thinking: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (about 11 onwards). In the sensorimotor stage the infant knows the world through the senses and through actions such as looking, sucking, grasping and moving. Thinking happens in action, not yet in words or mental symbols. Piaget divided the stage into six substages, from simple reflexes to the first mental representations near age 2. Its great achievement is object permanence, the understanding that objects go on existing when out of sight, which begins at about 8 to 12 months and is complete by about 18 to 24 months. When baby Kabir, at 10 months, pulls a cloth off a hidden rattle, he shows that the rattle still exists for him even unseen. Option c is right because sensorimotor thought is the stage that covers infancy, from birth to about 2 years. Exam trap: preoperational thought begins with language and pretend play at about 2, in the toddler and preschool years, so it is the near miss for \"infancy\".", "topic": "Piaget's sensorimotor stage", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "What is a dependent variable in research?", "options": {"a": "The variable that is manipulated by the researcher", "b": "The variable that remains constant throughout the research", "c": "The variable that is not relevant to the research question", "d": "The variable that is measured and observed"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the dependent variable is the variable that is measured and observed. In an experiment the researcher changes one thing and watches what happens to another. The factor deliberately changed is the independent variable (IV); the outcome recorded to see whether it changed is the dependent variable (DV), so called because its value is expected to depend on the IV. Everything else that might affect the DV is held constant; these are controlled variables. Any outside factor that might influence the DV is an extraneous variable, and one that varies along with the IV, so that its effect cannot be separated from the IV's, is a confounding variable, the main threat to internal validity. Take Arjun, who tests whether caffeine improves reaction time. He gives one group coffee and another decaffeinated coffee (caffeine is the IV, with two levels), keeps the time of day and the task identical (controlled variables), and records reaction time in milliseconds (the DV). If the caffeine group responds faster, the change in the DV is put down to the IV. A handy check is the phrase \"the effect of the IV on the DV\": the effect of caffeine on reaction time. In non-experimental research the same idea appears as predictor and outcome (criterion) variables. Option d is right because the DV is the outcome the researcher measures, not the one manipulated. Exam trap: examiners swap the two definitions, so option a, \"manipulated by the researcher\", is the independent variable, the most common reversal on this topic.", "topic": "Dependent variable", "fig": ""}, {"q": "People with ______ avoid specific public places or situations because they anticipate having anxiety or a panic attack and being unable to escape or get help.", "options": {"a": "Social phobia", "b": "Agoraphobia", "c": "Panic xenophobia", "d": "Acrophobia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: this describes agoraphobia. The word comes from the Greek agora, the marketplace, so it literally means fear of the marketplace or of public places. In DSM-5 (2013), agoraphobia is marked fear or anxiety about at least two of five kinds of situation: using public transport, being in open spaces such as car parks or bridges, being in enclosed places such as shops or cinemas, standing in a queue or a crowd, and being outside the home alone. The core of the fear is not the place itself but the thought that escape might be difficult, or help unavailable, if panic-like or embarrassing symptoms struck. The situations are avoided, endured with intense dread or faced only with a companion, and the problem typically lasts 6 months or more. Since DSM-5 it is diagnosed separately from panic disorder, though the two often occur together and a person can receive both. Alan Goldstein and Dianne Chambless (1978) described the mechanism as \"fear of fear\": after a few panic attacks, people come to dread the bodily sensations themselves and the places where attacks happened. Picture Riya, who had a panic attack on a crowded Delhi Metro train: she now travels only by autorickshaw, avoids malls and will not go to the market without her sister. Option b is right because the stem describes avoiding public places for fear of being unable to escape or get help during panic. Exam trap: social phobia also leads to avoidance of public situations, but there the feared outcome is being judged or embarrassed by others, not being trapped without help.", "topic": "Agoraphobia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Conformity was first systematically studied by:", "options": {"a": "Albert Ellis", "b": "Solomon Asch", "c": "Carl Rogers", "d": "Alfred Adler"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: conformity was first studied systematically by Solomon Asch. Conformity means changing one's behaviour or judgement to match a group, in response to real or imagined group pressure. In his line-judgement studies, Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) seated one real participant among about seven confederates who had been told what to say. On each trial everyone saw a standard line and three comparison lines and announced, in turn, which comparison matched the standard. The answer was obvious: tested alone, people erred less than 1 percent of the time. But on 12 of the 18 trials (the critical ones) the confederates unanimously gave the same wrong answer. The real participant, who answered last or next to last, conformed on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about 75 percent conformed at least once, though a quarter never did. Conformity rose with group size up to about three confederates and fell sharply when one ally broke the unanimity. Interviews showed most yielders knew the group was wrong but went along to avoid ridicule, which is normative influence, distinguished from informational influence by Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955). If Kabir hears six classmates confidently say line C, he may say C too, even though he can see that line B matches. Option b is right because Asch's experiments are the classic systematic study of conformity to a clear majority. Exam trap: Albert Ellis, Carl Rogers and Alfred Adler are clinicians and personality theorists, so the social psychologist is the only fit. Key note: Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic effect study (1935) on norm formation came earlier and is sometimes called the first conformity experiment, but Sherif is not an option, and Asch's studies are the classic systematic study of conformity to a clear majority, so b stands.", "topic": "Asch conformity experiments", "fig": "Solomon Asch"}, {"q": "Who is the father of neuropsychology?", "options": {"a": "Donald Hebb", "b": "Dr. Raphael", "c": "Shepherd Ivory Franz", "d": "Alfred Binet"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: among these options, Donald Hebb is the one called the father of neuropsychology. Neuropsychology studies how the brain produces behaviour and mental processes, largely by examining what changes when particular parts of the brain are damaged and by measuring those changes with tests. Donald Hebb (1949), a Canadian psychologist at McGill University, wrote The Organization of Behavior, the first full theory of how thought and memory could arise from the activity of networks of neurons. He proposed the cell assembly, a group of neurons that become linked because they fire together during an experience, and the Hebbian synapse: when one neuron repeatedly helps to fire another, the connection between them strengthens (later summed up as \"cells that fire together wire together\"). Long-term potentiation, found by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973), later supported the idea. Before that, Hebb had tested patients after brain surgery with Wilder Penfield in Montreal and found that removing large parts of the frontal lobes left IQ scores surprisingly intact, and at McGill he trained a generation of neuropsychologists, including Brenda Milner. When Meera learns a new raga by practising daily, Hebb's rule says the neurons involved fire together and so wire together. Option a is right because Hebb's 1949 book laid the foundation of modern neuropsychology, and he is the only founder figure among the options. Exam trap: Shepherd Ivory Franz sounds plausible because he did early brain-lesion research, but he was a pioneer, not the founder. Key note: Alexander Luria (Higher Cortical Functions in Man, 1962) is most often called the father of neuropsychology, and Hebb is also widely given the title for founding modern neuropsychology and neural network theory; Luria is not an option, so a stands.", "topic": "Father of neuropsychology", "fig": "Donald Hebb"}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is:", "options": {"a": "Left of the median", "b": "Right of the median", "c": "Same as median", "d": "Same as mode"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: in a negatively skewed distribution the mean lies to the left of the median. Skewness is the lack of symmetry in a distribution. In a negative (left) skew most scores bunch at the high end and a long tail stretches toward the low scores on the left. The three measures of central tendency then separate. The mode stays at the peak, where scores are most frequent. The median, the middle score by rank, moves a little toward the tail. The mean, which uses the value of every score, is pulled furthest toward the extreme low scores. So from left to right the order is mean, median, mode: mean less than median less than mode. Positive (right) skew is the mirror image, with mode less than median less than mean. Karl Pearson (1895) built his coefficients of skewness on this gap; for example, 3 times (mean minus median) divided by the standard deviation, which comes out negative in a left skew. Suppose most students in Arjun's class score 80 to 95 on an easy test but three score below 20: those few low scores drag the mean below the median. Option a is right because the long low tail pulls the mean below, that is to the left of, the median. Exam trap: students link \"negative\" with the wrong side or forget which measure moves most; remember that the mean always chases the tail.", "topic": "Negative skew and central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "State the correct sequence of Tuckman's stages of group development:", "options": {"a": "Storming, norming, forming, performing and adjourning", "b": "Norming, forming, storming, performing and adjourning", "c": "Forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning", "d": "Forming, norming, storming, performing and adjourning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Bruce Tuckman's sequence is forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning. Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed about 50 studies of therapy, training and laboratory groups and proposed that small groups pass through predictable stages. In forming, members are polite, uncertain and dependent on the leader as they learn about the task and one another. In storming, conflict breaks out over roles, goals and leadership, and members resist the demands of the group. In norming, the group settles these conflicts, builds cohesion and agrees on rules and roles. In performing, energy goes into the task itself, with flexible roles and mutual support. Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977) added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the work ends and the group disbands, sometimes with a sense of loss (some writers call it mourning). Think of Meera's college project team: awkward introductions, then an argument over who leads, then agreed deadlines and duties, then smooth work, and finally a farewell after submission. Option c is right because it is the only option in Tuckman's order, with storming before norming. Exam trap: option d swaps storming and norming, but conflict comes before the group agrees its norms, which is why the rhyme runs forming, storming, norming, performing.", "topic": "Tuckman's group stages", "fig": "Bruce Tuckman"}, {"q": "The 'cocktail party effect' describes a phenomenon in which:", "options": {"a": "You remember details of events to which you are paying attention", "b": "It is more likely that you will hear your name called if you are not looking at the person calling it", "c": "You can selectively attend to a particular conversation out of the many conversations taking place", "d": "It is easier to hear the person you are talking to when it is quieter in the room"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the cocktail party effect is the ability to attend selectively to one conversation out of the many taking place. Colin Cherry (1953), a British engineer studying speech, described the problem: at a noisy party we follow one speaker and shut out the rest. He tested it with dichotic listening, in which a different message is played to each ear through headphones, and shadowing, in which the listener repeats one message aloud as it is heard. People shadowed the attended ear well but could report almost nothing about the content of the unattended ear; they did not notice when it switched from English to German, though they did notice a change from a male to a female voice, a physical feature. Donald Broadbent (1958) explained this with his filter model: messages are selected early, on physical features, before their meaning is processed. Neville Moray (1959) then found that about a third of listeners heard their own name in the unattended ear, so some meaning leaks through, and Anne Treisman (1964) proposed that the filter only attenuates (turns down) unattended messages rather than blocking them. Imagine Kabir at a crowded wedding, chatting with a cousin while the band plays: he follows one voice, yet turns at once when someone across the room says \"Kabir\". Option c is right because the effect names selective attention to one conversation among many. Exam trap: hearing your own name from an unattended conversation is Moray's breakthrough finding within the cocktail party phenomenon, not its definition, and option b's clause about not looking at the caller is irrelevant.", "topic": "Cocktail party effect", "fig": "Colin Cherry"}, {"q": "Clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was:", "options": {"a": "The creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits", "b": "Treatment of returning veterans with 'shell shock'", "c": "Development of effective interrogation techniques", "d": "Coaching of newly trained fighter pilots"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was the creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits. When the United States entered the war in 1917, the army had to sort well over a million men quickly into jobs, officer training or discharge. Robert Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, chaired a committee, with Lewis Terman, Henry Goddard and others, that produced two group intelligence tests. The Army Alpha was a written test for literate recruits who could read English, with items on arithmetic, analogies, number series and general information; its multiple-choice format came from Arthur Otis, Terman's student. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for men who were illiterate or did not speak English, using mazes, picture completion and cube counting, with instructions given by demonstration. About 1.75 million recruits were tested. At the same time Robert Woodworth (1917) wrote the Personal Data Sheet to screen soldiers likely to break down under fire, the first personality inventory. The war proved that tests could be given to large groups at once, group testing boomed in schools and industry in the 1920s, and assessment remained the clinical psychologist's main job for decades. A recruit like Arjun, who had never been to school, would have taken the pictorial Beta rather than the Alpha. Option a is right because the army's group intelligence tests were psychology's signature contribution to the First World War. Exam trap: shell shock sounds like the obvious clinical task, but in World War I it was treated mainly by army doctors and psychiatrists; psychologists became therapists on a large scale only after World War II, when the Veterans Administration began training them in 1946.", "topic": "Army Alpha and Beta tests", "fig": "Robert Yerkes"}, {"q": "As described in textbooks of abnormal psychology, this is a syndrome of schizophrenia occurring in the presence of intellectual disability. It differs from schizophrenia only in that there is often a poverty of ideation, and delusions are not usually very well systematised.", "options": {"a": "Late paraphrenia", "b": "Pfropf schizophrenia", "c": "Pseudoneurotic schizophrenia", "d": "Oneiroid schizophrenia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: this is Pfropf schizophrenia, schizophrenia grafted onto intellectual disability. Pfropf is German for a graft, as when a gardener grafts a shoot onto an existing plant. German psychiatry in the tradition of Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926), who described dementia praecox in the 1890s, used Pfropfschizophrenie for a psychosis grafted onto a pre-existing mental handicap (then called oligophrenia or mental retardation). The illness looks much like ordinary schizophrenia, with hallucinations, delusions, odd behaviour and a decline from the person's previous level of functioning, but the limited intellect colours it: ideas are fewer and simpler (poverty of ideation), and delusions are poorly systematised, often fragmentary or naive, because building an elaborate delusional system needs cognitive resources. Catatonic features and repetitive behaviour are said to be common. Diagnosis is hard, because a person with intellectual disability may lack the language to describe hallucinations, and stereotyped behaviour can be mistaken for psychosis. Today it would be coded as schizophrenia co-occurring with intellectual disability. Suppose Arjun, 24, with moderate intellectual disability, begins talking to unseen people and insists, without any further detail, that a neighbour is \"doing magic\" on him: this is the grafted picture. Option b is right because Pfropf schizophrenia is the named syndrome of schizophrenia in the presence of intellectual disability. Exam trap: the other three are also named variants of schizophrenia, each defined by something else (late onset, neurotic-looking symptoms or a dreamlike state), so learn each by its defining feature.", "topic": "Pfropf schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Mr X, a 35-year-old man presents with suicidal ideation, depressed mood, loss of interest and enjoyment, and increased fatigue for the past 4 weeks. The symptoms had an insidious onset and a deteriorating course. There was a history of chronic feelings of emptiness, multiple episodes of non-suicidal self-harm, and fear of abandonment in relationships. He is the first-born child and oldest of 3 siblings. His father struggled with outbursts of aggression and had completed suicide when he was 14 years old, and his mother was pregnant with his youngest brother (now, 21). He struggles with alcohol overconsumption occasionally and is dependent on alcohol use. He confided that he has a tendency to eat when emotional. He got involved romantically and became sexually active at 19 years old and has had intense and volatile relationships. The current depressive episode was precipitated by a break up. What can be his diagnosis?", "options": {"a": "Complex post-traumatic stress disorder", "b": "Alcohol dependence", "c": "Borderline personality disorder", "d": "Major depressive disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the picture fits borderline personality disorder. A personality disorder is an enduring, pervasive and inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that begins by adolescence or early adulthood and causes distress or impairment. In DSM-5 (2013), borderline personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotion, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and shown by at least 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substance use, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and transient, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Mr X shows at least five: fear of abandonment, intense and volatile relationships, impulsivity in two areas (alcohol and emotional eating), repeated self-harm and chronic emptiness. The pattern runs from early adulthood, and the current episode followed a break-up, the kind of perceived rejection that typically destabilises people with this disorder. His father's aggression and suicide fit the known risk factors of early loss and family instability. Marsha Linehan (1993) developed dialectical behaviour therapy for this disorder. Option c is right because the long-standing pattern across relationships, mood and behaviour meets the borderline criteria. Exam trap: the stem opens with depressive symptoms to pull you towards major depressive disorder, but the question asks for the diagnosis that explains the whole history. Key note: the current 4-week depressive episode would also be recorded, but only four depressive symptoms are described, and a DSM-5 major depressive episode needs five; the long-standing pattern fits borderline personality disorder, the diagnosis the case is built to test, and c stands.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a practical consideration in evaluating a psychological test? 1. If reading is required by the examinee, does his or her ability match the level required by the test? 2. How appropriate is the length of the test? 3. Will the test produce accurate measurements in the context and for the purpose for which you would like to use it? 4. Have specialised subgroup norms been established?", "options": {"a": "4", "b": "3", "c": "3 and 4", "d": "2"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: items 3 and 4 are not practical considerations. Gary Groth-Marnat, in the Handbook of Psychological Assessment (first edition 1984), sets out the questions a clinician should ask when choosing a test under five headings: theoretical orientation, practical considerations, standardisation, reliability and validity. Practical considerations ask whether the test suits the examinee and the setting in everyday terms: whether the reading level matches the examinee's ability, whether the length suits the person's attention and the time available, and how much training the examiner needs to give and score it. Standardisation asks whether the norm group resembles the person being tested, whether adequate specialised subgroup norms exist (by age, sex, education or ethnicity, for example), and whether the instructions for administration are clear. Validity asks whether the test measures what it claims and will produce accurate measurements in the context and for the purpose intended. So item 1 (reading level) and item 2 (length) are practical; item 3 (accurate measurement for your purpose) is a validity question; and item 4 (subgroup norms) is a standardisation question. Suppose Meera, a clinical psychologist, must assess a farmer with five years of schooling: she checks that the inventory's reading level and length suit him (practical), that rural Indian norms exist (standardisation) and that the scores predict what she needs to know (validity). Option c is right because both 3 and 4 belong to other headings, so neither is a practical consideration. Exam trap: choosing only one of them (a or b) is a half answer, since both items fall outside the practical heading.", "topic": "Evaluating a psychological test", "fig": "Gary Groth-Marnat"}, {"q": "In retrograde amnesia, which of the following kinds of problem is seen?", "options": {"a": "Problems with recalling previously learned material", "b": "Problems with face recognition", "c": "Difficulty finding routes", "d": "Poor encoding of verbal material"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: retrograde amnesia is a problem with recalling material learned before the onset of the amnesia. Amnesia is a loss of memory, usually after brain injury, disease or psychological trauma, and it is described by its direction in time from the moment of onset. Retrograde amnesia (retro, backwards) is the loss of memories formed before the injury. It often follows a temporal gradient: the memories closest to the injury are lost first and most completely, while older ones survive, a pattern called Ribot's law after Theodule Ribot (1881). A footballer concussed in a tackle may remember the morning but not the minutes before the collision, because those memories had not yet been consolidated. Anterograde amnesia (antero, forwards) is the inability to form new lasting memories after the onset. The classic case is patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), whose medial temporal lobes, including both hippocampi, were removed in 1953 to control his epilepsy: William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported that he could no longer form new declarative memories, yet he could still learn motor skills such as mirror drawing. When Riya wakes after a road accident unable to remember the drive but recalls her childhood clearly, she shows retrograde amnesia with Ribot's gradient. Option a is right because retrograde amnesia means losing previously learned material. Exam trap: poor encoding of new verbal material also sounds like memory loss, but failing to lay down new memories is anterograde amnesia, the opposite direction in time.", "topic": "Retrograde amnesia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term 'linguistic relativity' refers to the idea that:", "options": {"a": "Some languages are better than others", "b": "Language influences how people think", "c": "It is easier to talk to family members than to friends", "d": "There are different languages around the world"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: linguistic relativity is the idea that language influences how people think. It comes from the anthropologist Edward Sapir (1929) and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf, whose essays were collected in Language, Thought and Reality (1956); together the claim is called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Whorf argued, partly from his study of the Hopi language, that the categories built into a language shape the way its speakers carve up experience. The hypothesis has two versions. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language determines thought, so speakers of different languages think in ways that cannot be translated. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says language influences habitual thought, perception and memory without fixing them. Research has largely rejected the strong form: Eleanor Rosch (1972), then publishing as Eleanor Heider, found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour terms, still remembered focal colours better than non-focal ones, just as English speakers did. Weaker effects are well supported; for example, Russian has separate basic words for light blue and dark blue, and its speakers tell those shades apart faster. Imagine Arjun, a Hindi speaker, whose words chacha, tau, mama and fufa distinguish uncles that English lumps together: he may notice and remember which side of the family a relative belongs to more readily than an English speaker. Option b is right because relativity means that thought is influenced by the language one speaks. Exam trap: do not confuse the weak version (relativity, influence) with the strong version (determinism, control), and note that the idea says nothing about some languages being better than others.", "topic": "Sapir-Whorf hypothesis", "fig": "Edward Sapir and Benjamin Lee Whorf"}, {"q": "Who introduced the term \"intelligence quotient\" (IQ)?", "options": {"a": "A.R. Jensen", "b": "E.L. Thorndike", "c": "R. B. Cattell", "d": "William Stern"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: William Stern introduced the term intelligence quotient. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence test in 1905, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's performance as a mental age (MA), the age level of the hardest items the child could pass. Mental age on its own is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. William Stern (1912), a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), a ratio he called the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove decimals and popularised the abbreviation IQ: IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. So if Kabir, aged 8, has a mental age of 10, his ratio IQ is (10 / 8) x 100 = 125. Because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, the ratio IQ breaks down for adults, and David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Option d is right because Stern coined the quotient in 1912. Exam trap: Terman popularised IQ and added the 100, which tempts students to credit him; he is absent here, and the listed names (Jensen, Thorndike, Cattell) are famous for other work on intelligence.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "According to the ______ model of motivation, a physiological need such as thirst stimulates an appropriate response.", "options": {"a": "Drive reduction", "b": "Yerkes-Dodson", "c": "Hierarchy of needs", "d": "James-Lange"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the drive-reduction model of motivation, a physiological need such as thirst stimulates a response that reduces it. Clark Hull (1943), in Principles of Behavior, built the most formal version. A need is a biological deficit, such as a lack of water or food. The need creates a drive, an aroused, unpleasant state of tension that energises the organism and pushes it to act. Behaviour that reduces the drive (drinking, eating) is reinforced, so the response becomes more likely the next time the need arises; Hull saw drive reduction as the basis of reinforcement and learning. The idea rests on homeostasis, the body's tendency to keep its internal conditions steady, which Walter Cannon popularised in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Primary drives come from biological needs; secondary (acquired) drives, such as a desire for money, are learned through association with primary ones. When Meera finishes a long badminton match, her body is short of water, she feels thirst (the drive), and drinking a glass of nimbu pani reduces it, so reaching for a drink after games becomes a habit. The theory struggles with behaviour that raises arousal, such as curiosity, thrill-seeking or exploring when no need is pressing, which led Donald Hebb (1955) and others to propose optimal arousal theories. Option a is right because the stem describes a need producing a drive that prompts a response to reduce it. Exam trap: Yerkes-Dodson also concerns arousal, but it links arousal to performance, not need to response.", "topic": "Drive-reduction theory", "fig": "Clark Hull"}, {"q": "The group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Brown", "b": "Thorndike", "c": "Spearman", "d": "Thurstone"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone proposed the group factor theory of intelligence. Using multiple factor analysis, a method he developed to extract several independent factors from a set of correlations, Louis Thurstone (1938) gave 56 tests to University of Chicago students and published Primary Mental Abilities. He found not one general factor but seven group factors, each shared by a cluster of tests but not by all: verbal comprehension (V), word fluency (W), number (N), space (S), associative memory (M), perceptual speed (P) and reasoning (R). He called them primary mental abilities and built tests to measure each, so that every person gets a profile rather than a single IQ. They are \"group\" factors because they sit between Charles Spearman's (1904) one general factor, common to every test, and the specific factors unique to one test. Later Thurstone found that his primary abilities were themselves correlated, suggesting a second-order general factor, so the two views moved closer. Cyril Burt and Philip Vernon (1950) then placed group factors in a hierarchy under g, with major group factors (verbal-educational and practical-mechanical) and minor ones below them. If Riya scores high on space and number but only average on verbal comprehension and word fluency, Thurstone's profile shows that pattern, which one IQ number would hide. Option d is right because group factor theory is Thurstone's primary mental abilities model. Exam trap: Spearman is the usual distractor, but his is the two-factor theory (g plus s), the very view Thurstone opposed.", "topic": "Thurstone's primary mental abilities", "fig": "Louis Thurstone"}, {"q": "______ is concerned with the skills and underlying abilities which we have acquired during life.", "options": {"a": "Procedural Memory", "b": "Propositional memory", "c": "Declarative Memory", "d": "Semantic Memory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: procedural memory is concerned with the skills and underlying abilities we acquire during life. It is memory for how to do things: riding a bicycle, typing, tying a knot, playing the tabla. It is learned gradually through practice, shown in performance rather than recalled in words, and works largely outside awareness, so it is classed as nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) showed that amnesic patients could learn a mirror-reading skill they could not remember practising, and Squire's later taxonomy of long-term memory divides it into declarative memory, knowing that, which can be consciously stated, and nondeclarative memory, which includes procedural skills, priming and classical conditioning. Declarative memory itself splits, following Endel Tulving (1972), into episodic memory for personal events with a time and place, and semantic memory for general facts and word meanings. Procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (the striatum) and the cerebellum, while declarative memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobes, which is why patient H.M. got better at mirror drawing day after day without remembering the task. When Kabir sits at the harmonium after years away, his fingers find the scales even though he could not explain the finger pattern in words. Option a is right because skills and abilities built up through practice are the content of procedural memory. Exam trap: declarative and semantic memory are also long-term stores, but they hold knowing that (facts and events), not knowing how.", "topic": "Procedural memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Bandura's social cognitive theory is based on which perspective?", "options": {"a": "Ageing", "b": "Learning", "c": "Behavioural", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: social cognitive theory, which Albert Bandura (1986) named in Social Foundations of Thought and Action, is based on the learning perspective. Bandura first built social learning theory, showing in the Bobo doll studies (Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961) that children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the aggression without ever being reinforced themselves. In Social Learning Theory (1977) and then the 1986 book, he argued that people learn mainly by observing others (observational learning, or modelling). Observational learning needs four processes: attention to the model, retention of what was seen, reproduction of the act and motivation to perform it. He added reciprocal determinism, the idea that the person (thoughts and expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another, and self-efficacy (1977), the belief that one can carry out an action successfully, which shapes what people attempt and how long they persist. Personality textbooks therefore place Bandura under the learning (social learning or social-cognitive) perspective. When Riya watches her elder brother speak confidently at a school debate and be praised, she may start to volunteer in class herself: she learned the behaviour vicariously, and her growing self-efficacy keeps it going. Option b is right because the theory explains behaviour through learning, by observation and by cognitive processing of experience. Exam trap: \"ageing\" is a developmental topic, not a perspective in personality theory, and \"none of the above\" lures anyone unsure whether learning and behaviourism differ. Key note: the theory grew out of behaviourism, so c is the near miss, but Bandura rejected radical behaviourism by putting cognition (expectations, self-efficacy) at the centre, so it is classed under the learning perspective rather than behaviourism, and b stands.", "topic": "Bandura's social cognitive theory", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "An increase in the value of one variable with a decrease in the value of the other variable implies:", "options": {"a": "Zero correlation", "b": "Positive correlation", "c": "Negative correlation", "d": "No correlation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: when one variable increases as the other decreases, the correlation is negative. Correlation describes how two variables vary together, and the most widely used index is the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, of Karl Pearson (1896), which runs from +1 through 0 to -1. Its sign gives the direction and its size gives the strength. In a positive correlation (r between 0 and +1) the two variables rise and fall together, like height and weight. In a negative or inverse correlation (r between 0 and -1) high scores on one go with low scores on the other: as hours of practice rise, errors fall. A zero correlation (r close to 0) means no linear relationship. On a scatterplot a negative correlation slopes downward from left to right. A correlation of -0.80 is just as strong as one of +0.80; the minus sign shows only the direction. If Arjun finds that the more time students spend on their phones at night, the lower their morning test scores, he has found a negative correlation, though correlation alone cannot show that one causes the other. Option c is right because opposite movement of two variables is the definition of a negative correlation. Exam trap: zero correlation and no correlation (a and d) mean the same thing, and a minus sign shows inverse direction, not a weak relationship.", "topic": "Negative correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A disruption of the ability to speak is known as:", "options": {"a": "Wernicke's Aphasia", "b": "Broca's Aphasia", "c": "Bedecker's aphasia", "d": "Warnick's aphasia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: a disruption of the ability to speak is Broca's aphasia. Aphasia is a disorder of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere. Paul Broca (1861), a French surgeon, studied a patient named Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because that was almost the only syllable he could say, although he understood much of what was said to him. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area, just in front of the motor cortex that controls the face and mouth. Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia is slow, effortful, halting speech in short, telegraphic phrases that drop small grammatical words (\"want... tea... now\"), while comprehension of everyday speech is relatively preserved; patients usually know their speech is wrong and are frustrated by it. Carl Wernicke (1874) described the opposite pattern after damage to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia, with fluent, well-articulated but empty speech full of wrong and invented words, and poor comprehension. The arcuate fasciculus links the two areas, and damage to it produces conduction aphasia, marked by poor repetition. After a stroke, Kabir's grandfather knows exactly what he wants to say but can only get out \"water... glass\", a picture of Broca's aphasia. Option b is right because Broca's aphasia is the loss of fluent speech production. Exam trap: Wernicke's aphasia also disturbs speech, but there the speech flows easily; it is meaning and comprehension that break down.", "topic": "Broca's aphasia", "fig": "Paul Broca"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "options": {"a": "Token economy", "b": "Aversive therapy", "c": "Flooding", "d": "Systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the token economy is not a classical conditioning treatment; it comes from operant conditioning. Classical (respondent) conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflexive response comes to trigger a similar response itself. Therapies built on it change the emotional response attached to a stimulus. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs deep relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared situations, so relaxation gradually replaces anxiety (reciprocal inhibition, or counterconditioning). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape prevented, until the conditioned fear extinguishes because nothing bad happens. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus, such as a nausea-producing drug, so that the behaviour itself comes to evoke discomfort. The token economy works through consequences instead. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran one on a psychiatric ward: patients earned tokens, which are generalised secondary reinforcers, for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, just as the operant principles of B. F. Skinner (1938) predict. In a children's hostel, Meera might give stars for making the bed and finishing homework, to be traded for a Sunday outing. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour by reinforcement after a response, not by pairing stimuli. Exam trap: ask whether a technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical); aversion therapy feels like punishment, which makes it look operant, but its standard textbook basis is classical pairing.", "topic": "Token economy as operant", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a true indicator of the validity of items during test construction?", "options": {"a": "Face Validity", "b": "Content Validity", "c": "Construct Validity", "d": "Criterion Validity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: face validity is not a true indicator of validity. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure, and evidence for it is gathered during test construction. Face validity is only whether a test looks, to the people taking it, as if it measures what it says. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, first edition 1954) stressed that face validity is not validity in the technical sense: it concerns appearance and acceptability, not what the test actually measures. A test can look right and measure nothing useful, or look odd and predict well; many MMPI items have little face validity yet separate clinical groups empirically. It still matters in practice, because test takers cooperate better with items that seem relevant. The true indicators are three. Content validity asks whether the items adequately sample the whole domain, judged systematically by experts against a test blueprint. Criterion validity asks whether scores correlate with an outside criterion measured at the same time (concurrent) or later (predictive). Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether scores behave as the theory of the construct predicts, including convergent and discriminant evidence (Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959). Suppose Kabir writes an anxiety scale with items like \"I often feel anxious\": it has high face validity, but only correlations with clinical ratings and other anxiety measures can show that it is valid. Option a is right because face validity is a surface impression, not an empirical index. Exam trap: content validity also relies on judgement, which makes it look like face validity, but it is a systematic expert check against the domain, not a first impression.", "topic": "Face validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is one of the most influential theories of depression?", "options": {"a": "Freud's psychodynamic theory", "b": "Beck's cognitive theory", "c": "Seligman's learned helplessness theory", "d": "Berne's humanistic theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck's cognitive theory is one of the most influential theories of depression. Aaron Beck (1967), an American psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' thinking was persistently and systematically negative, and proposed that depression is driven by how people think. At its core is the cognitive triad: negative views of the self (\"I am worthless\"), the world (\"nothing goes right\") and the future (\"it will never get better\"). These views come from negative schemas, stable beliefs formed in early experience, often around loss or rejection, that lie dormant until a stressful event activates them. Once active, they produce automatic negative thoughts and systematic cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, all-or-nothing thinking and personalisation. The theory led directly to cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979), in which patients test their negative thoughts against evidence; it is among the most researched psychological treatments for depression, and the Beck Depression Inventory (1961) is one of the most used depression scales. If Meera fails one exam and thinks \"I am stupid, my teachers despise me and I will never get a job\", she shows all three parts of the triad. Option b is right because Beck's model is the theory of depression that textbooks single out as most influential. Exam trap: \"Berne's humanistic theory\" borrows a real name, but Eric Berne is linked to transactional analysis, not to any theory of depression. Key note: Martin Seligman's learned helplessness (1975) and its attributional (1978) and hopelessness (1989) reformulations are also major theories, so c is the near miss; Beck's cognitive theory is the one textbooks single out as the most influential, and b stands.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive theory of depression", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"pgibams-2023": [{"q": "The five Ps of case formulation include:", "concept": "The answer is a: the five Ps of case formulation are the presenting problem, predisposing factors, precipitating factors, perpetuating factors and protective factors. A case formulation is the clinician's working explanation of why this particular person has this problem at this particular time, and it guides the treatment plan. The five Ps framework, taught widely in CBT and psychiatry training (for example by Craig Macneil and colleagues, 2012), sorts that explanation into five boxes. The presenting problem is what brings the person in: the symptoms, their severity and their effect on daily life. Predisposing factors are vulnerabilities present before onset, such as family history, temperament, early adversity or perfectionism. Precipitating factors are the triggers that set the problem off now, such as a loss, an exam failure or a move. Perpetuating factors keep it going once it has started: avoidance, rumination, substance use, an unsupportive family. Protective factors are the strengths and resources treatment can build on, such as a supportive friend, good insight or a steady job. Each box can be filled with biological, psychological and social entries, which turns it into a biopsychosocial formulation (George Engel, 1977). Example: Arjun, 21, has low mood and poor sleep (presenting); his mother had depression and he is a perfectionist (predisposing); he failed his third attempt at an entrance exam (precipitating); he now stays in his room and ruminates (perpetuating); he is close to his sister and willing to attend weekly sessions (protective). Option a is right because it lists exactly the five standard Ps and nothing else. Exam trap: each wrong option swaps one or two real Ps for invented ones ('persisting', 'personality', 'positive', 'pre-morbid'), so check all five words, not just the first two.", "traps": {"b": "This list drops the presenting problem and adds 'persisting factors', which is not one of the five Ps; what keeps a problem going is already covered by perpetuating factors.", "c": "Perpetuating, precipitating and presenting are real Ps, but 'personality factors' and 'positive factors' are invented; personality traits belong under predisposing factors and strengths under protective factors, and both predisposing and protective factors are missing.", "d": "'Pre-morbid factors' and 'positive factors' are not Ps of the model; pre-morbid vulnerabilities are what predisposing factors cover and the strengths box is called protective, so both of those real Ps are missing."}}, {"q": "Match the following. 1. Learned helplessness, 2. Flashbulb memory, 3. Freeze, 4. Shock. i. A state of extreme alertness where one is also unable to move or take action against the danger, ii. A highly vivid and detailed \"snapshot\" of a moment in which a consequential, surprising and emotionally arousing piece of news was learned, iii. A state that occurs after a person has experienced a stressful situation repeatedly, iv. A state of dissociation, where your body and mind feel disconnected and nothing feels real", "concept": "The answer is c: learned helplessness matches iii, flashbulb memory ii, freeze i and shock iv (1-iii, 2-ii, 3-i, 4-iv). Learned helplessness was found by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967). Dogs that had received shocks they could not escape later lay down and took shocks in a shuttle box, even when jumping a low barrier would have ended them, while dogs that had been able to switch the shock off learned to escape. Repeated uncontrollable stress, not the shock itself, taught them that action is useless, which matches iii; Seligman (1975) later used it as a model of depression. Flashbulb memory, named by Roger Brown and James Kulik (1977), is a vivid, detailed 'snapshot' of where you were when you heard surprising, consequential and emotional news, such as the assassination of John F. Kennedy; that is ii. Such memories feel certain, but Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) showed with the Challenger explosion that they change a great deal over time. Freeze is the third member of the fight, flight or freeze response: the person is highly alert, heart racing, yet cannot move or act, sometimes called tonic immobility; that is i. Shock, in the psychological sense used here, is the stunned, dissociated state just after a traumatic event, when body and mind feel disconnected and nothing feels real; that is iv. Example: when Meera hears that her cousin has been in an accident, she first freezes, fully alert but unable to move; for hours afterwards she feels numb and unreal; years later she still remembers exactly where she stood when the call came. Option c is right because it is the only code that pairs all four terms correctly. Exam trap: 'repeatedly' in iii is the clue to learned helplessness, and 'extreme alertness but unable to move' is freeze, not shock; once 1 and 3 are fixed, only c fits.", "traps": {"a": "This code gives learned helplessness the freeze description and freeze the 'repeated stress' description, reversing items 1 and 3; only 2-ii and 4-iv are right.", "b": "This code gets every pair wrong: it gives learned helplessness the dissociation description, flashbulb memory the repeated-stress description and freeze the flashbulb snapshot.", "d": "This code gives learned helplessness the flashbulb description and flashbulb memory the repeated-stress description, which belongs to learned helplessness (Seligman and Maier, 1967); no pair is correct."}}, {"q": "Injury to the hypothalamus may cause a person to sleep abnormally for a:", "concept": "The answer is b: injury to the hypothalamus can make a person sleep abnormally for a long period. The hypothalamus is a small region at the base of the brain, below the thalamus, that regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, hormones and the sleep-wake cycle. The classic evidence came from Constantin von Economo (1917), a Viennese neurologist who studied the epidemic of encephalitis lethargica, the 'sleeping sickness' that swept Europe during and after the First World War. Most of his patients slept for days or weeks, waking only briefly to eat, and at post-mortem their damage lay in the posterior hypothalamus and upper brainstem. A smaller group had the opposite problem, severe insomnia, and their lesions lay in the anterior hypothalamus near the preoptic area. Von Economo concluded that the posterior hypothalamus holds a waking centre and the anterior hypothalamus a sleep centre, and later lesion experiments in animals confirmed this. Today we know that the posterior and lateral hypothalamus contain orexin (hypocretin) and histamine neurons that keep us awake, and loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy, while the ventrolateral preoptic area switches sleep on. A third hypothalamic region, the suprachiasmatic nucleus just above the optic chiasm, is the circadian clock that times sleep across the 24-hour day. Example: after a viral brain infection damages his posterior hypothalamus, Rahul sleeps 16 to 18 hours a day and is hard to rouse. Option b is right because the classic result of hypothalamic injury, seen in von Economo's patients, is prolonged sleep (somnolence). Exam trap: 'abnormal pattern' (d) sounds safe and general, but the stem asks how long the person sleeps, and the textbook answer is a long period. Key note: damage to the anterior hypothalamus gives the opposite, sleeplessness, so the stem is a simplification; the classic textbook statement is prolonged sleep after hypothalamic injury, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "A short period of sleep (insomnia) is what von Economo saw in the smaller group with damage to the anterior hypothalamus near the preoptic area; the classic textbook outcome of hypothalamic injury is prolonged sleep.", "c": "A normal pattern contradicts the stem, which says the person sleeps abnormally; the hypothalamus is a key sleep-wake regulator, so damage disturbs sleep rather than leaving it normal.", "d": "'Abnormal pattern' only repeats the stem's word 'abnormally' without saying how; the stem ('sleep abnormally for a ...') asks about duration. Damage to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the hypothalamus's own circadian clock, does scramble the timing of sleep, which makes d the near miss, but the classic result of hypothalamic injury is prolonged sleep."}}, {"q": "MMPI was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the MMPI was developed by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built at the University of Minnesota Hospitals by Hathaway, a psychologist, and McKinley, a neuropsychiatrist; work began in the late 1930s and the test was published in 1943. Its key innovation was empirical criterion keying. Instead of writing items that merely looked as if they measured depression, the authors wrote a large pool of true-false statements and kept only those that patients with a given diagnosis (for example, depressed inpatients) answered differently from normal controls, mainly visitors to the hospital. An item stayed in the Depression scale because depressed patients endorsed it more often, whatever its content. This produced ten clinical scales, such as Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia and Schizophrenia, plus validity scales (L, F and later K) to detect lying, careless or odd responding, and defensiveness. The MMPI-2 (1989) restandardised it on a broader sample with 567 items, and the MMPI-A serves adolescents. Example: if Kavita answers 'true' to an item that depressed patients endorsed far more often than controls, it adds to her Depression score even if the item seems unrelated to mood. Option a is right because Hathaway and McKinley are the authors of the original MMPI. Exam trap: Theodore Millon did build a well-known clinical inventory, the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI), so pairing him with Alfred Binet sounds plausible, but neither man had any part in the MMPI.", "traps": {"b": "Theodore Millon (1977) built the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory for personality disorders, and Alfred Binet with Theodore Simon built the first intelligence scale (1905); they never worked together and neither built the MMPI.", "c": "Eduard Glanzmann was a Swiss paediatrician whose name is on a bleeding disorder (Glanzmann's thrombasthenia); Glanzmann and Riniker are names from medicine, not authors of a personality test.", "d": "'None' is wrong because option a correctly names the MMPI's two authors."}}, {"q": "Analysis of avoidance learning suggests that many phobias are acquired through ______ conditioning:", "concept": "The answer is a: analysis of avoidance learning suggests that many phobias are acquired through classical conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927), a neutral stimulus paired with one that naturally causes a reaction comes to cause that reaction by itself. John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) applied it to fear with an 11-month-old boy, Little Albert. Albert at first played happily with a white rat. Each time he reached for it, a steel bar was struck behind his head, and the loud noise frightened him. After a few pairings the rat alone made him cry and crawl away, and the fear generalised to a rabbit, a dog and a fur coat. But why do phobias last, when a conditioned response should extinguish once the feared object is met without harm? Orval Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory (1947) answers this. Factor one: fear is acquired by classical conditioning. Factor two: the person then avoids the feared object, and the drop in fear that avoidance brings is negative reinforcement, an operant process. Because the person never stays long enough to learn that the object is safe, the fear never extinguishes. Example: Kabir is bitten by a dog at age 7, so dogs become paired with pain (classical); afterwards he crosses the road whenever he sees one, and the relief he feels each time strengthens the avoidance (operant). Option a is right because the fear itself, the core of the phobia, is learned by classical conditioning; operant reinforcement only maintains the avoidance. Exam trap: the stem's words 'avoidance learning' pull you toward operant (b), but in the two-factor theory operant conditioning keeps the phobia going, while classical conditioning acquires it.", "traps": {"b": "Operant conditioning (Edward Thorndike, 1898; B. F. Skinner, 1938) learns from consequences; in Mowrer's two-factor theory it maintains the avoidance through negative reinforcement (relief), but it does not create the original fear.", "c": "Intermittent (partial) reinforcement is a schedule of reinforcement within operant conditioning that makes responses resistant to extinction; it is not a type of conditioning that acquires fear.", "d": "Reinforcement is the operant process that strengthens a response, here the relief that keeps avoidance going; it is a mechanism within conditioning, not a kind of conditioning, and it maintains rather than acquires the fear."}}, {"q": "One of the concepts of the humanistic-existential approach is:", "concept": "The answer is d: unconditional positive regard is a concept of the humanistic-existential approach. This approach, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, sees people as free, responsible and naturally growing toward their potential; its leading figures include Abraham Maslow (1954), Rollo May (1958) and above all Carl Rogers. Carl Rogers (1957), in his paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change, named three core therapist conditions: congruence (being genuine), accurate empathic understanding and unconditional positive regard. Unconditional positive regard means warmly accepting and valuing the client as a person, without conditions, even when you do not approve of what he has done. Rogers argued that problems grow from conditions of worth: a child learns that he is lovable only if he behaves in certain ways, so he denies parts of his experience and his self-concept becomes incongruent with what he actually feels. A therapist who offers acceptance without conditions lets the client drop that defensiveness, look honestly at his feelings and resume growth. Example: Rohan tells his counsellor that he cheated in an exam and hates himself. A person-centred counsellor neither lectures nor reassures; she accepts Rohan as a worthwhile person, listens closely and reflects his feelings, so he can explore why he felt such pressure. Option d is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions, the heart of humanistic therapy. Exam trap: 'rationalism' sounds like a philosophical, existential word, but it points to Albert Ellis's (1955) rational emotive behaviour therapy, a cognitive approach; and unconditional positive regard means accepting the person, not approving every behaviour.", "traps": {"a": "Rationalism is the philosophical view that knowledge comes from reason; in therapy it points to Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy, a cognitive approach that disputes irrational beliefs, not a humanistic concept.", "b": "Transference, the client's redirection onto the therapist of feelings from early relationships, is a central concept of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, not of the humanistic-existential approach.", "c": "Deviation from the statistical norm is one criterion for defining abnormality (behaviour that is statistically rare), not a concept of any therapeutic approach."}}, {"q": "The link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is due to the presence of?", "concept": "The answer is d: the hypothalamus links the nervous system and the endocrine system. The nervous system sends fast electrical signals along neurons; the endocrine system sends slower chemical messages, hormones, through the blood. The hypothalamus, a small structure at the base of the brain below the thalamus, belongs to both. It sits just above the pituitary gland, the 'master gland', and is joined to it by a stalk, the infundibulum. It controls the pituitary in two ways. First, neurons in its supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons to the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, it secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal cortex, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary, which is why the hypothalamus is called a neuroendocrine organ. Example: before her viva, Ananya worries. Her hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol. A worry has become a hormone through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Option d is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones by ruling the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is called the master gland, but it takes its orders from the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus; the other three options are purely neural structures with no hormonal role.", "traps": {"a": "The corpus callosum is the thick band of fibres joining the left and right cerebral hemispheres (cut in Roger Sperry's split-brain patients); it links the two halves of the brain, not the brain and the glands.", "b": "The reticular formation, a network of neurons in the core of the brainstem, controls arousal, alertness and the sleep-wake cycle (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949); it does not release or control hormones.", "c": "The cerebellum at the back of the brain coordinates balance, posture and smooth, well-timed movement and supports motor learning; it plays no part in the neuroendocrine link."}}, {"q": "Excessive emotional attachment of a daughter to her father is called the:", "concept": "The answer is a: excessive emotional attachment of a daughter to her father is called the Electra complex. In Sigmund Freud's (1905) psychosexual theory, the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals and on a family triangle. A boy desires his mother and sees his father as a rival, fearing castration: the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek king who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother. For girls, Freud described a parallel sequence: the girl discovers she lacks a penis, blames her mother, turns her love to her father, and resolves the conflict, less completely in Freud's view, by identifying with her mother. Carl Jung (1913) named this the Electra complex, after the Greek heroine Electra, who helped her brother avenge their father by killing their mother. Freud himself rejected the term and preferred to speak of the female Oedipus complex, or 'feminine Oedipus attitude', but exams pair Electra with girls and Oedipus with boys. In classical theory an unresolved complex can leave lasting traces, such as a woman who keeps choosing older, father-like partners. Example: four-year-old Anjali announces that she will marry Papa when she grows up and sulks when he hugs her mother; in Freudian terms she is in the Electra phase. Option a is right because the Electra complex is the name for a girl's attachment to her father and rivalry with her mother. Exam trap: the Oedipus complex is the boy's version (son to mother), and examiners rely on students mixing up the two Greek names; the inferiority and superiority complexes belong to a different theorist altogether.", "traps": {"b": "The Oedipus complex, Sigmund Freud's term, is the boy's version: desire for the mother and rivalry with the father during the phallic stage.", "c": "The inferiority complex is Alfred Adler's term in Individual Psychology for an exaggerated, disabling sense of inadequacy; it has nothing to do with attachment to a parent.", "d": "The superiority complex, also Alfred Adler's, is an exaggerated show of superiority that masks underlying feelings of inferiority; it is not about a daughter's attachment to her father."}}, {"q": "In Sternberg's triarchic theory, the type of intelligence that involves the ability to think critically and analytically is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory, the ability to think critically and analytically is componential intelligence. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, argued that IQ tests capture only one part of intelligence and proposed three sub-theories, hence 'triarchic'. Componential (analytical) intelligence is the ability to analyse, compare, judge and solve well-defined problems. It runs on three kinds of mental components: metacomponents, which plan, monitor and evaluate problem solving; performance components, which carry out the strategy; and knowledge-acquisition components, which learn new information. This is the intelligence that school and IQ tests reward. Experiential (creative) intelligence is the ability to deal with novel situations and to make familiar tasks automatic, combining experiences in original ways. Contextual (practical) intelligence, often called street smarts, is the ability to adapt to the environment, shape it or select a better one. Sternberg later folded the three into his theory of successful intelligence, the balanced use of analytical, creative and practical abilities to reach one's goals. Example: Priya tops her class by dissecting arguments in a logic paper (componential); her brother Kabir invents a new game from scrap materials (experiential); their cousin Dev, with poorer marks, runs a busy shop and handles every customer well (contextual). Option a is right because critical, analytical thinking is the defining feature of componential intelligence. Exam trap: crystallised intelligence (c) belongs to a different theory, Raymond Cattell (1963) and John Horn's fluid and crystallised abilities; examiners slip it in because it sounds like a fourth 'c' word beside componential and contextual.", "traps": {"b": "Contextual intelligence is Sternberg's practical intelligence: adapting to, shaping or selecting one's environment ('street smarts'); it concerns everyday success, not critical analysis.", "c": "Crystallised intelligence, accumulated knowledge and vocabulary, comes from Raymond Cattell and John Horn's theory of fluid and crystallised intelligence, not from Sternberg's triarchic theory.", "d": "Experiential intelligence is Sternberg's creative intelligence: handling novelty and automatising familiar tasks; it is insight and originality, not critical, analytical judgement."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following types of love does not belong to Rollo May's classification of love?", "concept": "The answer is d: narcissism is not one of Rollo May's types of love. Rollo May (1969), the American existential psychologist, described four kinds of love in his book Love and Will. Sex, or libido, is the biological drive, satisfied by the release of tension. Eros is the drive to procreate or create, a longing for union with another person and for a lasting, deepening relationship; May thought modern culture had split sex from eros. Philia is friendship or brotherly love: an easy, relaxed liking of the other person as they are. Agape is selfless love devoted to the welfare of the other with no expectation of return, like a parent's love or the love of God for humanity. May held that mature, authentic love blends all four, and that love cannot be separated from will, the capacity to organise oneself to move toward a goal. Narcissism, love directed at oneself, is a different idea, from Sigmund Freud's paper On Narcissism (1914); it describes self-absorption, and in its extreme form narcissistic personality disorder, not a kind of love for another person. Example: Rhea's bond with her school friend Tara is philia; her devotion to caring for her ill grandmother, wanting nothing back, is agape. Option d is right because narcissism is absent from May's list of sex, eros, philia and agape. Exam trap: 'eros' sounds like Freud's life instinct, so students strike it out; but in May's scheme eros is a valid type of love, and only narcissism is foreign to it.", "traps": {"a": "Eros is one of May's four kinds of love: the longing to create and to unite with another person in a lasting relationship, beyond the mere release of sex.", "b": "Philia is one of May's four kinds of love: friendship or brotherly love, an easy liking of the other as they are.", "c": "Agape is one of May's four kinds of love: selfless love given for the other's welfare with no expectation of return."}}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "concept": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus controls homeostasis (hunger, thirst, body temperature) and the pituitary gland; it regulates drives and hormones, not the fast reflex arc.", "c": "The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex and plans voluntary, deliberate movement; reflexes by definition bypass this conscious control.", "d": "The medulla does run a few brainstem reflexes (coughing, sneezing, gagging, swallowing) and the vital rhythms of breathing and heart rate, but most reflexes in the body close their loop in the spinal cord."}}, {"q": "Cattell's personality theory is also known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: Raymond Cattell's personality theory is known as factor-analytic theory. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes the correlations among many measures and groups them into a few underlying dimensions, called factors. Raymond Cattell (1946, 1950), a British-born psychologist working in the United States, used it to find the basic structure of personality. He started from the trait words that Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, cut the list down to about 171 terms and then to a few dozen clusters, and gathered three kinds of data: L-data (life records, such as school reports and ratings by people who know the person), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests, where the person cannot easily tell what is being measured). Factor analysis of these data yielded 16 source traits, the deep underlying causes of behaviour, such as warmth, emotional stability, dominance and sensitivity; clusters of visible behaviour that merely go together he called surface traits. He measured the source traits with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). Cattell also divided traits into ability, temperament and dynamic traits, and his split of intelligence into fluid and crystallised came from the same method. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and high dominance but low emotional stability; these scores place her on dimensions the statistics discovered, not on categories a theorist imagined. Option b is right because Cattell derived his traits from factor analysis, so his approach is called factor-analytic. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (1947) also used factor analysis but arrived at a few broad superfactors; 16 source traits and the 16PF identify Cattell.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic theory is Sigmund Freud's account of unconscious conflict among id, ego and superego, built from clinical case material, not from statistics.", "c": "Behaviouristic theory (John Watson, 1913; B. F. Skinner) explains personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement, with no inner traits; Cattell's traits are statistical dimensions inside the person.", "d": "There is no 'projective theory' of personality; projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT are assessment methods based on the projective hypothesis, while Cattell relied on objective L, Q and T data."}}, {"q": "The Children's Apperception Test (CAT) method of personality assessment was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Children's Apperception Test (CAT) was given by Bellak, that is, Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak (1949). The CAT is a projective test adapted from the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), which Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray introduced in 1935. In the TAT, adults tell stories about ambiguous pictures of people, and the themes are thought to reveal their needs, conflicts and relationships. Leopold Bellak reasoned that young children identify more readily with animals than with adult human figures, so the CAT shows 10 pictures of animals in human situations, such as chicks around a bowl of food, bears in a tug of war and a lion with a pipe. It is meant for children of about 3 to 10. Each picture is chosen to pull for typical childhood themes: feeding, sibling rivalry, attitudes to parents, fear of aggression, being alone at night and toilet training. The child tells what is happening, what led up to it and how it ends, and the examiner looks for recurring themes, the hero the child identifies with, and how conflicts are resolved. A human-figure version, the CAT-H (1965), followed, and the Bellaks also built the Senior Apperception Technique (1973) for older adults. Example: shown the bears pulling on a rope, six-year-old Aarav says the baby bear is losing and will be sent away; repeated themes of losing and rejection would be explored further. Option b is right because the CAT is Bellak's test. Exam trap: Henry Murray (d) made the TAT, the parent test, so his name is the trap; the children's animal version is Bellak's.", "traps": {"a": "B. F. Skinner (1938) developed operant conditioning and the Skinner box; he never built a picture-story projective test.", "c": "John Holland is known for the RIASEC theory of vocational interests (realistic, investigative, artistic, social, enterprising, conventional) and the Self-Directed Search, not for a children's projective test.", "d": "Henry Murray, with Christiana Morgan, created the Thematic Apperception Test (1935) for adolescents and adults; the CAT was adapted from it but is Bellak's."}}, {"q": "Which part of the nervous system is called the centre of Emotions?", "concept": "The answer is a: the hypothalamus is called the centre of emotions. An emotion has three parts: a feeling, an appraisal of the situation, and a bodily reaction such as a racing heart, sweating or a flushed face. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, organises the bodily side. It controls the autonomic nervous system, which speeds or slows the heart and breathing, and through the pituitary it controls stress hormones such as cortisol. The classic evidence came from Philip Bard (1928), who removed the cerebral cortex of cats and found that they flew into 'sham rage' at the slightest touch: hissing, clawing, arching the back and raising the fur, rage with no real target. The rage disappeared when the posterior hypothalamus was removed as well, so the hypothalamus was needed to produce the emotional response, while the cortex normally held it in check. Walter Hess then stimulated parts of the hypothalamus in awake cats through fine electrodes and produced coordinated rage or fear on demand, work that won him a Nobel Prize (1949). Example: when Vikram hears a loud bang at night, his hypothalamus sets off the fight-or-flight reaction; his heart pounds and his palms sweat before he has worked out that it was a firecracker. Option a is right because the hypothalamus drives the bodily expression of emotion and is the structure standard textbooks call the centre of emotion. Exam trap: the thalamus (b) is the relay in the Cannon-Bard theory, and the two similar words are easy to swap. Key note: modern work places emotion in the limbic system, especially the amygdala (James Papez's circuit, 1937; Paul MacLean's 'limbic system', 1952), and the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, 1927) gave the thalamus a central role; among these options the hypothalamus, called the centre of emotion in standard Indian textbooks, is the expected answer, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The thalamus is the sensory relay station; in the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, 1927) it sends signals at the same moment to the cortex (felt emotion) and to the body (arousal), but it is not the structure called the centre of emotion.", "c": "The cerebral cortex appraises and labels emotions and, as Bard's decorticate cats showed, normally holds emotional reactions in check; it is not the centre that produces them.", "d": "The hippocampus, part of the limbic system and Papez's circuit, is chiefly the structure for forming new memories; it gives emotions their context but is not the centre of emotion."}}, {"q": "What is the term used to denote the long-term increase in IQ scores over the past few decades?", "concept": "The answer is a: the long-term increase in IQ scores is called the Flynn effect. James Flynn (1984), a political scientist in New Zealand, compared how American samples scored on old and new versions of IQ tests. Each time a test is restandardised, the new norm group must answer more items correctly to earn the average score of 100, so the same performance gives a lower IQ against new norms than against old ones. Flynn (1987) then gathered data from 14 nations and found the same pattern everywhere: average scores rose about 3 points per decade across the twentieth century, with the largest gains on tests of fluid reasoning such as Raven's Progressive Matrices rather than on vocabulary. Richard Herrnstein and Charles Murray (1994) gave it the name Flynn effect in The Bell Curve. The gains are far too fast to be genetic. Suggested causes include better nutrition and health, more and better schooling, smaller families, a more cognitively complex environment and greater familiarity with abstract, test-like thinking. In some countries, such as Norway and Denmark, the rise has slowed or reversed in recent decades. The practical lesson is that tests must be re-normed regularly, because an old test inflates IQ. Example: if Arjun takes an IQ test whose norms are 20 years old, his score may be about 6 points too high, which matters when a cut-off around 70 decides a diagnosis of intellectual disability. Option a is right because the generational rise in IQ is named after Flynn, who documented it. Exam trap: the 'Wechsler effect' and the 'IQ effect' sound technical but do not exist; only the Flynn effect is a real term.", "traps": {"b": "'Two generation gap' is not a psychological term for IQ change; the generation gap is a loose phrase for differences in values and attitudes between parents and children.", "c": "'The IQ effect' is not a named phenomenon; the documented rise in IQ scores carries the name of James Flynn, who reported it.", "d": "There is no 'Wechsler effect'; David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and introduced the deviation IQ, but the rise in scores across generations is the Flynn effect."}}, {"q": "Which of the following types of discipline developed as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline many adults had been subjected to during their childhood?", "concept": "The answer is d: permissive discipline developed as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline many adults had been subjected to as children. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), in Child Development, a textbook widely followed in Indian courses, described three types of discipline used by parents and teachers. Authoritarian discipline is the traditional kind: strict rules, harsh punishment for breaking them, little or no explanation, and no say for the child. Permissive discipline means little or no discipline: the child is seldom taught rules, rarely punished and largely left to make his own decisions. Hurlock explains that it grew up as a revolt against the authoritarian discipline that many adults had suffered in their own childhood; resolving not to repeat it, they swung to the other extreme. The result, she warns, is often a child who is confused and insecure, unsure what is expected of him. Democratic discipline uses explanation, discussion and reasoning to help the child understand why certain behaviour is expected; it stresses learning rather than punishment and rewards good behaviour. The scheme parallels Diana Baumrind's (1966, 1971) parenting styles, authoritarian, permissive and authoritative, with democratic discipline close to authoritative parenting. Example: Kavya's father was beaten for every small mistake as a boy, so he vows never to punish her; he sets no rules for bedtime or homework, and Kavya grows up unsure of limits. Option d is right because permissive discipline is the type Hurlock traces to adults rebelling against their own strict upbringing. Exam trap: democratic discipline (b) also departs from authoritarian methods and sounds like the reformed choice, but it is reasoned guidance with rules; the revolt that throws rules away is permissive.", "traps": {"a": "Authoritarian discipline is the very style being rebelled against: strict rules, harsh punishment and no explanation; it cannot be a revolt against itself.", "b": "Democratic discipline, in Hurlock's scheme, uses explanation, discussion and reasoning, with rules and rewards; it is the balanced, recommended method, not the reaction of adults rejecting their own strict upbringing.", "c": "'Motivational discipline' is not one of Hurlock's three types (authoritarian, permissive, democratic); it is a distractor built from the general idea of motivating a child."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a projective test of personality?", "concept": "The answer is c: the word association test is a projective test of personality. A projective test presents an ambiguous or open stimulus and lets the person answer freely; because there is no fixed set of answers, the response is thought to reveal hidden needs, conflicts and feelings, the projective hypothesis set out by Lawrence Frank (1939), who coined the term projective methods. Francis Galton (1879) first tried word association on himself, noting the ideas each word called up. Carl Jung (1904 to 1910) made it a clinical tool: he read out a list of 100 stimulus words, asked the person to reply with the first word that came to mind, and timed each reply. Complex indicators, such as a long delay, no reply, repeating the stimulus word, an odd or very personal answer, or visible emotion, suggested that the word had touched an emotionally charged complex. Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff (1910) published a 100-word list with norms from 1,000 normal adults, so that each answer could be scored as common or unusual. Example: to most words Sanjay answers within a second, but when he hears 'father' he pauses for eight seconds and then says 'nothing'; that delay and odd answer would be explored as a possible complex about his father. Option c is right because the word association test uses free, unstructured responses to reveal unconscious material, the defining feature of projective methods. Exam trap: the MMPI and Bell's Adjustment Inventory are personality tests too, which tempts you, but they are structured self-report inventories with fixed answers, not projective tests.", "traps": {"a": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is a structured true-false self-report inventory scored by empirical keys; it is an objective test, not a projective one.", "b": "A sociometric test (Jacob Moreno, 1934) asks group members whom they would choose or reject as companions and maps the group's relationships in a sociogram; it measures social structure, not projected inner conflicts.", "d": "Bell's Adjustment Inventory (Hugh Bell, 1934) is a yes-no questionnaire covering home, health, social and emotional adjustment; it is a self-report inventory, not a projective test."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is a non-experimental research design?", "concept": "The answer is c: the case study is a non-experimental research design. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable and, ideally, assigns participants to conditions at random, so that a change in the dependent variable can be attributed to the manipulation. In non-experimental (descriptive or observational) research nothing is manipulated: the researcher observes, measures or describes things as they are. The case study is the classic example. It is an intensive, in-depth study of one person, family or small group, drawing on interviews, observation, records, tests and history, often over a long time. A famous case is H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed parts of both his medial temporal lobes in 1953 to stop his seizures, Brenda Milner (with Scoville, 1957) studied him for decades and showed that he could no longer form new long-term memories, which revealed the role of the hippocampus. Case studies are rich, suit rare conditions and generate hypotheses, but they cannot show cause and effect and may not generalise to other people. Example: a clinician writes up how Neha, a girl with a rare dissociative disorder, developed her symptoms and responded to therapy; she has manipulated nothing, only described. Option c is right because a case study describes a single case without manipulating any variable or using a control group. Exam trap: quasi-experimental sounds 'half non-experimental', but it does manipulate the independent variable, only without random assignment. Key note: a cross-sectional study is also non-experimental (observational: nothing is manipulated), so d is defensible; the case study is the textbook prototype of non-experimental, descriptive research, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A randomised controlled design (RCT) is a true experiment: participants are randomly assigned to treatment and control groups and the independent variable is manipulated, the gold standard for testing whether a treatment works.", "b": "A quasi-experimental design (Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, 1963) manipulates the independent variable but uses existing groups instead of random assignment; it is still a form of experiment.", "d": "A cross-sectional study compares different groups (for example, age groups) at one point in time; it is observational and so also non-experimental, but the case study is the standard textbook example expected here."}}, {"q": "Reliability and validity are two dimensions of:", "concept": "The answer is c: reliability and validity are two dimensions of test construction. Building a psychological test follows a fixed sequence, set out in standard texts such as Anne Anastasi's Psychological Testing (1954). First comes planning: the purpose, the population and a blueprint of content. Next the author writes many items, tries them out on a sample and runs item analysis, keeping items of suitable difficulty that discriminate well between high and low scorers. Then the test as a whole must be shown to work. Reliability is consistency: does the test give similar scores on retesting, across parallel forms, across its two halves or its items (Lee Cronbach's alpha, 1951), and across scorers? Validity is accuracy: does the test measure what it claims, shown through content, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive) and construct validity? Finally norms are built from a standardisation sample so that a raw score can be interpreted, and a manual reports it all. A test can be reliable without being valid (a bathroom scale that always reads 2 kg too heavy is consistent but wrong), but it cannot be valid without being reliable. Example: when Dr Sharma builds a Hindi anxiety scale, she must show that it gives Pooja nearly the same score two weeks apart (reliability) and that it correlates with clinicians' ratings of anxiety (validity) before anyone uses it. Option c is right because reliability and validity are the core psychometric properties every constructed test must establish. Exam trap: 'retest value' (d) echoes test-retest, which is only one way of estimating reliability, and so is far too narrow. Key note: 'test efficiency' (a) is a loose phrase for how well a test does its job overall and is the near miss; reliability and validity are the formal dimensions every constructed test must show, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Test efficiency' is a loose, non-technical phrase for how well a test works overall, sometimes used for the accuracy of decisions; reliability and validity are the formal psychometric dimensions established during test construction.", "b": "'Test procedure' refers to administration: instructions, timing and testing conditions; a standard procedure supports reliability, but reliability and validity are not dimensions of the procedure.", "d": "'Retest value' echoes test-retest reliability, one method of estimating reliability; it says nothing about validity and is too narrow to contain both."}}, {"q": "Community-based services that offer support in substance abuse include self-help services such as:", "concept": "The answer is a: Alcoholics Anonymous is a community-based self-help service for substance abuse. Self-help (mutual-aid) groups are run by members who share the same problem, not by professionals; they are free, rooted in the community and meet as often as members need. Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) was founded in Akron, Ohio, in 1935 by Bill Wilson and Bob Smith, a stockbroker and a surgeon who were both struggling with alcoholism. Its programme is the Twelve Steps, which begin with admitting powerlessness over alcohol and move through a moral inventory, making amends and carrying the message to other alcoholics. Members attend regular meetings, share their stories, keep each other's anonymity and are guided by a sponsor, a member with longer sobriety. The goal is total abstinence, taken one day at a time. The model spread to other problems: Narcotics Anonymous (1953) for drugs, Gamblers Anonymous for gambling, and Al-Anon for the families of drinkers. Research links regular AA attendance, and professional Twelve Step Facilitation that encourages it, with higher rates of continuous abstinence. Example: after detoxification, Suresh's psychologist suggests that he attend AA meetings twice a week; there he finds a sponsor who has been sober for ten years and whom he can phone whenever a craving strikes. Option a is right because AA is the best-known community-based self-help group for alcohol problems. Exam trap: the other three options borrow drug names (crack, cannabis, hashish) to sound like support groups, but none is a real organisation.", "traps": {"b": "'Crack Crack' is not a real organisation; crack is a smokable, highly addictive form of cocaine, so the name is only a play on the drug.", "c": "'Cannabis Collective' is not a recognised self-help service; cannabis (Cannabis sativa) is the plant that yields marijuana and hashish, and the name merely borrows it.", "d": "'Hashish Home' is not a real service; hashish is the resin of the cannabis plant, and the name is a distractor built from a drug name."}}, {"q": "Match the following. 1. Personality structure, 2. Personality process, 3. Personality development, 4. Psychopathology and change. (i) Psychological reactions and motives that change dynamically over relatively brief periods, (ii) Stable and enduring qualities that define the individual and distinguish individuals from one another, (iii) People sometimes change, and sometimes resist change or are unable to change, (iv) Everybody changes, and comes to be a unique individual by adulthood. Codes in the order 1, 2, 3, 4:", "concept": "The answer is c: structure matches (ii), process (i), development (iv), and psychopathology and change (iii). Daniel Cervone and Lawrence Pervin, in their textbook Personality: Theory and Research, argue that every complete personality theory must answer four questions, and they use these to compare the major theories. Structure refers to the stable, enduring units of personality, the relatively unchanging qualities that define a person and make people differ from one another, such as Sigmund Freud's (1923) id, ego and superego, or traits such as extraversion; that is (ii). Process refers to the dynamic side: the motives, emotions and psychological reactions that change over relatively brief periods, such as anxiety and defence or a person's goals at a given moment; that is (i). Growth and development asks how everybody changes over time and comes to be a unique individual by adulthood, through genes, parents, culture and experience; that is (iv). Psychopathology and behaviour change asks why some people develop problems and how they change, or resist change, or cannot change, especially in therapy; that is (iii). A memory aid: structure is the architecture of a house, process is the activity inside it, development is how it was built over the years, and psychopathology and change is what happens when it needs repair. Example: Aditi's high conscientiousness is structure; her anxiety before tomorrow's exam is process; the way her strict grandparents shaped her is development; whether therapy can ease her perfectionism is change. Option c is right because it is the only code that gives (ii), (i), (iv), (iii) in order. Exam trap: 'change' appears in both (iii) and (iv); 'everybody changes and becomes unique' is development, while 'sometimes resist change' is psychopathology and change.", "traps": {"a": "This code simply runs (i) to (iv) in order and gets every pair wrong: structure is the stable qualities (ii), not brief dynamic reactions, and resisting change belongs to psychopathology and change, not development.", "b": "This code gets structure right but gives process the 'resisting change' description and psychopathology the 'brief dynamic reactions' one; process means motives and reactions that shift over short periods (i).", "d": "This code gets process right but gives structure the 'resisting change' description and development the 'stable qualities' one; stable, enduring qualities are the definition of structure."}}, {"q": "Choose the correct sequence of development of the following methods for interpreting the Rorschach inkblot test:", "concept": "The answer is b: the expected sequence is Rapaport-Schafer's, Klopfer's, Piotrowski's, Beck's and finally Exner's Comprehensive System. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published his ten inkblots in Psychodiagnostik but died the next year without leaving a full scoring system. In the United States five separate systems grew up side by side from the late 1930s onward. Samuel Beck (1937, 1944) stayed close to Rorschach and stressed norms and statistics. Bruno Klopfer (1937, 1942) took a more clinical, phenomenological approach and founded the Rorschach Research Exchange. Zygmunt Piotrowski (1937, 1957) developed his 'perceptanalysis'. David Rapaport and Roy Schafer (1945 to 1946) built a psychoanalytic, ego-psychology system in Diagnostic Psychological Testing, and Marguerite Hertz added a fifth. Because the systems scored the same responses with different codes, research findings could not be compared, and clinicians argued over which was right. John Exner (1974) solved this with the Comprehensive System, which kept the empirically supported elements of all five, standardised administration and scoring, and summarised a record in a Structural Summary of ratios and indices with norms. It became the international standard, and the newer Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011) grew out of it. Option b is right because it is the only sequence that places Exner's Comprehensive System last, after the earlier American systems it integrated. Exam trap: options a and c put Exner near the start, and d puts Beck after Exner, but a synthesis can only come after the systems it combines. Key note: the strict order is Beck and Klopfer, then Piotrowski, then Rapaport-Schafer, with Exner last; b is the only option that ends with Exner's 1974 integration, the point the question tests, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "This sequence puts Exner's Comprehensive System first, but John Exner (1974) built it by integrating the earlier American systems of Beck, Klopfer, Hertz, Piotrowski and Rapaport-Schafer, so it must come last.", "c": "This sequence places Exner's Comprehensive System second, before Klopfer's and Piotrowski's; the Comprehensive System was the 1974 synthesis of those systems, so it cannot precede them.", "d": "This sequence puts Beck's system after Exner's, but Samuel Beck's system dates from 1937 to 1944 and was one of the sources Exner combined in 1974."}}, {"q": "Paraschemazia is:", "concept": "The answer is a: paraschemazia is distortion of the body image. The body image, or body schema, is the mental picture each of us has of our own body: its size, shape, position and how its parts are joined. Frank Fish (1967), in Clinical Psychopathology, the standard British text on descriptive psychopathology, groups its disturbances as disorders of the body image and names three by their Greek-based prefixes. In hyperschemazia a part of the body is felt as larger, heavier or more prominent than it is, as when a lip feels swollen after a dental injection. In hyposchemazia a part feels smaller or absent, as in the neglect of one side of the body after right parietal damage. In paraschemazia the body image is distorted: parts feel twisted, separated from the rest of the body, displaced or changed in shape. It is reported with hallucinogens such as LSD, in epileptic auras and with lesions of the parietal lobe, the region that builds the body map. Example: after taking a hallucinogen, Karan says his arms feel as if they have come away from his shoulders and his head is twisting round; he knows his body has not changed, but it feels distorted. Option a is right because 'para-' means beside or abnormal, and paraschemazia is the distorted body image in Fish's scheme. Exam trap: option c describes a nihilistic delusion, the belief that the body has been destroyed, is rotting or is dead, as in Cotard's syndrome (Jules Cotard, 1880); that is a disorder of belief, not a distortion of the felt body image.", "traps": {"b": "Weightlessness, as felt in space flight or a falling lift, is a bodily sensation, and sensory deprivation is a separate experimental condition; neither is the name of a body-image disorder.", "c": "The belief that one's body has been destroyed, is dead or is rotting is a nihilistic delusion, the core of Cotard's syndrome; it is a disorder of thought content, not a distortion of the body image.", "d": "'None' is wrong because option a gives the standard definition of paraschemazia."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques involves presenting target people with a small request and then following up with a larger request, the one desired all along?", "concept": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. It is one of the compliance tactics of social psychology, ways of getting people to say yes to a direct request. Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) demonstrated it with homeowners in California. When a researcher asked people cold to put a large, badly lettered 'Drive Carefully' sign on their front lawns, only about 17 per cent agreed. But among people who, two weeks earlier, had agreed to a tiny request, displaying a small 'Be a safe driver' sign, about 76 per cent agreed to the big one. Two explanations are offered. Consistency: having said yes once, a person feels pressure to stay consistent. Self-perception (Daryl Bem, 1967): watching themselves agree, people infer that they are the kind of person who supports such causes. The first request must be small enough that almost everyone accepts it, or the technique fails. Example: a volunteer asks Ishaan to sign a petition to save a neighbourhood park; a week later she asks him to spend a Sunday planting trees there, and he agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face (c) is the mirror image, a large request that is refused followed by a small one, and examiners count on students swapping the two; 'door in the foot' (d) is a scrambled name for no real technique.", "traps": {"a": "Low-ball (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) first wins agreement to an attractive deal and then raises its cost; people stick with their commitment, but there is no small request followed by a larger one.", "c": "Door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) runs in the reverse order: a large request that is refused, then the smaller real one, which feels like a concession to be returned.", "d": "'Door in the foot' is a scrambled blend of the two real names; no such compliance technique exists."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity means that scores on the test correlate highly with scores on another test that measures the same attribute, taken at about the same time. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. One family of evidence, criterion-related validity, asks how well test scores agree with an outside standard, the criterion. It comes in two forms, separated by timing. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later: an entrance test is valid if it predicts first-year marks. In concurrent validity the criterion is measured now, alongside the test: a new scale is given together with an established measure of the same trait, or with a current diagnosis, and the two sets of scores are correlated. A high correlation, reported as a validity coefficient, means the new test agrees with the accepted measure. Concurrent validity is especially useful when a shorter, cheaper or local-language test is meant to replace a longer one. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), following the American Psychological Association's technical recommendations (1954), listed concurrent validity beside predictive, content and construct validity. Example: a psychologist translates a depression scale into Marathi and gives it to 100 patients on the same day as Aaron Beck's Beck Depression Inventory (1961); if Sneha's scores and everyone else's on the two tests correlate around .80, the new scale shows good concurrent validity. Option d is right because concurrent validity is agreement with an established measure of the same attribute taken at the same time. Exam trap: option a takes 'concurrent' literally, as two tests done at the same time; timing alone proves nothing unless the scores also correlate with a valid criterion.", "traps": {"a": "Giving two tests at the same time is only the timing half of concurrent validity; without a high correlation with an established measure of the same attribute, simultaneous testing shows nothing about validity.", "b": "Agreement between two or more clinicians or scorers is inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability, a form of consistency, not validity.", "c": "Items that consistently relate to each other show internal consistency reliability, estimated by split-half methods or Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951); it is reliability, not validity."}}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow (1868), reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes.", "traps": {"a": "Parietal damage disturbs touch, body sense and spatial attention, producing astereognosis or neglect of one side of space, not impulsive personality change.", "c": "Occipital damage causes visual problems such as field defects, cortical blindness or visual agnosia, not changes in judgement or impulse control.", "d": "Temporal damage typically affects memory, hearing and language comprehension (Wernicke's aphasia), and bilateral lesions can cause Kluver-Bucy syndrome, but consequence-blind, irresponsible behaviour is the frontal picture."}}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of mental age?", "concept": "The answer is a: the concept of mental age was introduced by Alfred Binet. Asked by the French education ministry to pick out children who needed special schooling, Alfred Binet and the physician Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test: 30 items of rising difficulty that tapped judgement, comprehension and reasoning, rather than the simple sensory tasks Francis Galton (1884) had tried. In the 1908 revision they grouped the items by age level, placing each item at the age at which most ordinary children could pass it. A child's mental age is the age level of the items he or she passes. Binet himself spoke of the child's mental level; 'mental age' is the term that stuck once Henry Goddard translated the scale into English. For example, if eight-year-old Riya passes all the items normally passed by ten-year-olds, her mental age is 10 although her chronological age is 8: she is two years ahead. Mental age on its own is not a single index of brightness, because a two-year lag means much more at age 4 than at age 14. That is where the later steps came in. William Stern (1912) divided mental age by chronological age to give the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied it by 100 and popularised the label IQ. Option a is right because the age-graded scale and the idea of mental age came from Binet's work with Simon. Exam trap: examiners pair 'mental age' with Stern or Terman. Stern contributed the quotient (mental age divided by chronological age) and Terman the multiplication by 100 and the American revision; neither invented mental age itself.", "traps": {"b": "William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient by dividing mental age by chronological age; he used Binet's mental age, he did not create it.", "c": "Lewis Terman (1916) produced the Stanford-Binet, multiplied Stern's quotient by 100 and popularised 'IQ'; he built on mental age rather than inventing it.", "d": "Joy Paul Guilford proposed the Structure of Intellect model (operations, contents and products) and pioneered the study of divergent thinking; he had nothing to do with mental age."}}, {"q": "A 10-year-old boy has a mental age of 12 years. What will be his IQ?", "concept": "The answer is b: his IQ is 120. This is the ratio IQ, the original way of turning a mental age into a single number. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1908) gave each child a mental age (MA), the age level of the test items the child can pass. William Stern (1912) saw that the same lag means different things at different ages, so he divided mental age by chronological age (CA). Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the quotient by 100 to remove the decimal. The formula is IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. Working: MA = 12 years and CA = 10 years; 12 / 10 = 1.2; 1.2 x 100 = 120. So the boy performs like an average 12-year-old although he is 10, and his ratio IQ is 120. A quick check helps: when mental age is above chronological age the IQ must be above 100; when the two are equal it is exactly 100; when mental age is lower it is below 100. Arjun, aged 8 with a mental age of 6, would score (6 / 8) x 100 = 75. The ratio IQ has a flaw: mental age stops growing in the mid-teens, so the formula breaks down for adults (a 40-year-old would score absurdly low). David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which compares a person with others of the same age on a scale with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option b is right because (12 / 10) x 100 = 120. Exam trap: inverting the ratio, dividing CA by MA (10 / 12 x 100, about 83), is the classic slip; an IQ below 100 for a child who is ahead of his age should warn you that the division has been turned upside down.", "traps": {"a": "100 is the IQ only when mental age equals chronological age (for example MA 10 and CA 10); this boy is two years ahead, so his score must be above 100.", "c": "90 would need a mental age of 9 at age 10; these figures cannot produce it, and even the inverted ratio gives about 83, not 90.", "d": "The correct value, 120, is printed in option b, so 'none of the above' does not apply."}}, {"q": "Light passes through the eye in which of the following sequences?", "concept": "The answer is a: light passes through the cornea, then the pupil, then the lens, and finally reaches the retina. The full path is cornea, aqueous humour, pupil, lens, vitreous humour, retina. The cornea is the clear, curved window at the front of the eye. Light bends most where it passes from air into the eye, so the cornea does most of the focusing, roughly two-thirds of it. Behind the cornea lies the aqueous humour, a watery fluid. Light then passes through the pupil, which is not a structure but an opening in the iris, the coloured ring of muscle that widens the pupil in dim light and narrows it in bright light. Next comes the lens, a flexible, transparent disc attached to the ciliary muscle. It does the fine focusing: it thickens for near objects and flattens for distant ones, a process called accommodation. Light then crosses the jelly-like vitreous humour and lands on the retina, the inner lining at the back of the eye, where rods (dim light) and cones (colour and fine detail) turn light into neural signals that leave through the optic nerve. When Meera reads her phone in a dark room, her pupils widen to let in more light and her lenses thicken to focus the near text. Option a is right because it is the only sequence that follows the anatomy from front to back. Exam trap: the lens is the famous focusing part, so students put it first or in front of the pupil; in fact the lens sits behind the iris and pupil, and the cornea, not the lens, does most of the bending of light.", "traps": {"b": "Cornea, lens, pupil, retina puts the lens in front of the pupil; the lens actually lies just behind the iris, so light passes through the pupil first.", "c": "Lens, cornea, pupil, retina starts with the lens, but the cornea is the outermost transparent layer and is always the first structure light meets.", "d": "Lens, pupil, cornea, retina reverses the front of the eye entirely; the cornea is at the very front, then the pupil, and only then the lens."}}, {"q": "\"The quality of performance on any task is an inverted U-shaped function of arousal.\" This law is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the inverted-U relation between arousal and performance is the Yerkes-Dodson law. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose a white box over a black one, giving electric shocks of different strengths for mistakes. Learning was fastest at an intermediate shock, slower when the shock was weak or very strong, and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later textbooks turned this into a general law about arousal (alertness, anxiety, physiological activation). Performance improves as arousal rises from a low level, peaks at a moderate, optimal level, and then falls as arousal becomes excessive, which gives the inverted U. The optimum depends on the task: simple or well-practised tasks are done best at fairly high arousal, while complex or new tasks are done best at lower arousal. Donald Hebb (1955) linked the idea to his optimal arousal theory, the view that organisms seek a moderate level of stimulation. Kabir, preparing for an entrance exam, studies poorly when he is bored and sleepy, best when he is mildly keyed up, and freezes when panic sets in on the morning of the exam; because the exam is a complex task, his best level of arousal is moderate rather than high. Option c is right because Yerkes and Dodson are credited with the inverted-U function linking arousal and performance. Exam trap: the curve is often confused with Clark Hull's (1943) drive theory, in which more drive gives more performance in a rising line; the Yerkes-Dodson law says that too much arousal hurts, especially on difficult tasks.", "traps": {"a": "The law of effect, from Edward Thorndike's puzzle-box cats (1898, stated formally in 1911), says responses followed by satisfying consequences are strengthened; it concerns learning by consequences, not arousal.", "b": "Donald Broadbent's filter theory (1958) is an early-selection model of attention in which a filter lets one channel through on physical features; it says nothing about arousal and performance.", "d": "The principle of constancy, taken from Gustav Fechner by Sigmund Freud, holds that the mind keeps its level of excitation low and steady (perceptual constancy is a separate idea about stable perception of size, shape and colour); neither describes an inverted U."}}, {"q": "Who gave the theory of cognitive dissonance?", "concept": "The answer is c: Leon Festinger gave the theory of cognitive dissonance. In A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance, Leon Festinger (1957) proposed that holding two cognitions that contradict each other (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of one's own behaviour) creates an unpleasant state of tension, dissonance, which people are motivated to reduce. They can change the behaviour, change one of the beliefs, add new consonant beliefs, or play down the importance of the conflict. The classic test is the study by Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959). Students spent an hour on dull tasks such as turning pegs, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant that the tasks were enjoyable. Afterwards, those paid $1 rated the tasks as more enjoyable than those paid $20. Twenty dollars was enough to justify the lie, but one dollar was not, so the $1 group reduced their dissonance by changing their attitude: the effect of insufficient justification. Dissonance also explains effort justification (we value what we suffered to get) and post-decision dissonance (we upgrade the option we chose). For example, Vikram smokes but knows that smoking causes cancer. He may quit, or he may tell himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to 90', adding a consonant cognition that eases the tension. Option c is right because the theory, its name and its founding experiments are Festinger's. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 versus $20 result without any tension, by people inferring their attitudes from their own behaviour; Bem, or Fritz Heider's balance theory (1946), is the usual near miss for the founder of dissonance theory.", "traps": {"a": "Rensis Likert (1932) devised the summated rating attitude scale (strongly agree to strongly disagree); he measured attitudes rather than explaining dissonance.", "b": "Leonard Doob was one of the Yale group (John Dollard, Doob, Neal Miller, O. Hobart Mowrer and Robert Sears, 1939) behind the frustration-aggression hypothesis and wrote on attitudes and propaganda; dissonance theory is not his.", "d": "Option c correctly names Leon Festinger, so 'none of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of stages in creative thinking? 1. Illumination, 2. Preparation, 3. Evaluation, 4. Implementation, 5. Incubation", "concept": "The answer is a: the sequence is preparation (2), incubation (5), illumination (1), evaluation (3) and implementation (4). The classic model comes from Graham Wallas (1926), who in The Art of Thought drew on the accounts that scientists such as Hermann von Helmholtz and Henri Poincare gave of their own discoveries. He described four stages. Preparation: the person defines the problem, gathers information and works on it consciously, often without success. Incubation: the problem is set aside; conscious work stops, but processing goes on outside awareness, and rest or a change of activity helps. Illumination: the solution appears suddenly, the 'aha' or insight moment, often while the person is doing something else; Poincare described an idea arriving just as he stepped onto a bus. Verification: the idea is tested, checked and refined. Later versions of the model, like the one in this question, split verification into two steps: evaluation, judging whether the idea really works and is worth keeping, and implementation, putting it into practice. Imagine Ananya, a design student stuck on a poster for a water-saving campaign. She studies past campaigns for a week (preparation), gives up and goes home for a holiday (incubation), suddenly pictures a dripping tap shaped like a clock while on the train (illumination), checks the idea with her teacher and classmates (evaluation), and then produces and prints the poster (implementation). Option a is right because it is the only order that starts with preparation, places incubation before the flash of insight, and ends by evaluating and then applying the idea. Exam trap: students put illumination first because it is the most famous stage, or place incubation before preparation; incubation only helps after real preparatory work has been done.", "traps": {"b": "1, 5, 2, 4, 3 starts with illumination and puts preparation third; insight cannot come before the problem has been prepared, and implementation cannot come before evaluation.", "c": "5, 4, 1, 3, 2 begins with incubation and ends with preparation, which turns Graham Wallas's model upside down; there is nothing to incubate before preparation.", "d": "3, 5, 2, 4, 1 starts with evaluation and ends with illumination; evaluation can only judge an idea that insight has already produced."}}, {"q": "The clinical term for causal factors is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the clinical term for causal factors is etiology (British spelling aetiology). The word comes from the Greek aitia, meaning cause. Aetiology is the study of why a disorder develops: biological factors (genes, brain chemistry, infection, injury), psychological factors (learning, thinking styles, early experience) and social factors (poverty, trauma, family stress). Clinicians rarely find a single cause; the diathesis-stress model (Paul Meehl, 1962) and George Engel's biopsychosocial model (1977) treat causes as interacting. Aetiology belongs to a family of terms used in every case discussion. Epidemiology studies how often and in whom a disorder occurs in a population (prevalence, incidence, distribution). Prognosis is the predicted course and outcome. Relapse is the return of symptoms after a period of improvement. For example, when a psychiatrist discusses Sanjay's depression, the aetiology might be a family history of depression plus a recent job loss; the prognosis is good with treatment; and stopping medication too early raises the risk of relapse. Option b is right because etiology is the only term that names the causes of a disorder. Exam trap: epidemiology also talks about 'risk factors', so it tempts; but it describes how disorders are distributed across populations, not the causal explanation of the disorder itself.", "traps": {"a": "Epidemiology is the study of how frequently and in whom a disorder occurs in a population (prevalence, incidence, distribution); it counts cases rather than explaining causes.", "c": "Prognosis is the forecast of a disorder's likely course and outcome, for example 'good with early treatment'; it looks forward, not back to causes.", "d": "Relapse is the return of symptoms after a period of improvement or remission; it is a term about course, not about causes."}}, {"q": "The procedure used to assess the brain wave pattern in waking and sleep stages is:", "concept": "The answer is a: brain wave patterns in waking and in the stages of sleep are recorded with the EEG, the electroencephalogram. Hans Berger (1929) first recorded human brain waves through electrodes placed on the scalp. The EEG picks up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons and displays it as waves that differ in frequency and amplitude. Alert waking shows fast, low beta waves; relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows alpha waves (8 to 13 Hz); light sleep shows theta; deep slow-wave sleep shows large, slow delta waves; and REM sleep returns to fast, irregular, wake-like activity. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) used the EEG together with eye-movement recording to discover REM sleep. In a sleep laboratory the EEG is combined with the EOG (eye movements) and the EMG (muscle tone) in polysomnography. The EEG has excellent timing, in milliseconds, but poor spatial detail, and it is also the standard test for epilepsy. Option a is right because the EEG is the only listed procedure that records the brain's electrical activity. Exam trap: the ECG and EMG are also electrical recordings, and the EMG is even part of a sleep study, but they record the heart and the muscles, not brain waves.", "traps": {"b": "The ECG (electrocardiogram) records the electrical activity of the heart and is used to detect arrhythmias and heart attacks; it does not record brain waves.", "c": "The EMG (electromyogram) records the electrical activity of muscles; in sleep studies it shows the loss of muscle tone in REM sleep, but it does not show brain wave patterns.", "d": "ECT (electroconvulsive therapy, Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938) is a treatment that passes current through the brain to produce a controlled seizure, mainly for severe depression; it delivers electricity rather than recording it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is derived from the hemp plant \"Cannabis sativa\"?", "concept": "The answer is b: marijuana is derived from the hemp plant Cannabis sativa. Marijuana is the dried leaves and flowering tops of the plant, usually smoked or eaten. Its main psychoactive compound is delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), which acts on CB1 cannabinoid receptors in the brain. These receptors are dense in the hippocampus (hence the memory problems), the cerebellum and basal ganglia (hence the clumsiness) and the cortex. Hashish is the concentrated resin of the same plant, so it is stronger. In India the plant gives three familiar forms: bhang (leaves and seeds, often drunk at Holi), ganja (dried flowering tops) and charas (hand-rubbed resin, the Indian hashish). Effects include euphoria, relaxation, a slowed sense of time, increased appetite, red eyes and a fast heart rate; heavy use, especially in adolescence, is linked to poor memory, dependence and a raised risk of psychosis. DSM-5 treats cannabis as a substance class of its own, with cannabis use disorder and cannabis withdrawal. For example, Rohit, a college student who drank bhang at Holi, felt time stretching and laughed for hours; both effects come from THC. Option b is right because marijuana is the only option made from Cannabis sativa. Exam trap: opium and crack also come from plants, so 'derived from a plant' is not enough; you must match the plant: cannabis gives marijuana, hashish, bhang, ganja and charas; the opium poppy gives opium; the coca plant gives cocaine and crack.", "traps": {"a": "Opium is the dried latex (sap) of the unripe seed pods of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum; it yields morphine and codeine and is the parent of heroin, not of cannabis.", "c": "MDMA (ecstasy) is a synthetic amphetamine derivative made in laboratories; it is a stimulant with hallucinogenic effects and comes from no plant.", "d": "Crack is a smokable, freebase form of cocaine, which is extracted from the leaves of the coca plant (Erythroxylum coca), not from hemp."}}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to ______ with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to ______ with age.", "concept": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the pattern: fluid reasoning falls after early adulthood, and crystallised knowledge accumulates rather than declines.", "b": "Fluid intelligence does not increase across adulthood; it peaks early and then declines.", "d": "The first half is right, but crystallised intelligence keeps rising into late middle age rather than staying flat."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a method used to teach autistic children?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS) is a method used to teach autistic children. Andy Bondy and Lori Frost (1985) developed it at the Delaware Autistic Program for children with autism who had little or no functional speech. PECS rests on applied behaviour analysis: the child learns to hand a picture card to a communication partner in exchange for the item shown, and getting that item is the reward. It moves through six phases: (1) the physical exchange of a single picture; (2) distance and persistence (going to the partner and to the picture book); (3) discriminating between pictures; (4) building a sentence strip ('I want' plus a picture); (5) answering 'What do you want?'; and (6) commenting ('I see', 'I hear'). Because the child must approach another person, PECS teaches initiation and the social nature of communication, a core difficulty in autism, and many children go on to develop some speech. It is a form of augmentative and alternative communication (AAC). Aarav, a four-year-old autistic boy who used to scream when hungry, learns to pick up a picture of a biscuit and give it to his teacher; the request replaces the scream. Option a is right because PECS was designed specifically for autistic children with communication difficulties. Exam trap: Braille and the Taylor frame are also special education methods and appear in the same lists, but they serve children with visual impairment, not autism.", "traps": {"b": "Braille, devised by Louis Braille (1824), is a tactile reading and writing code of raised dots for people who are blind or visually impaired, not an autism intervention.", "c": "The Taylor frame is a metal slate with star-shaped holes into which shaped pegs are set to do arithmetic; it is a mathematics aid for blind pupils, not for autism.", "d": "PECS (option a) is a well-established method for autistic children, so 'none of these' is wrong."}}, {"q": "What is Spearman's rho?", "concept": "The answer is a: Spearman's rho is a measure of correlation. Charles Spearman (1904) introduced the rank-difference method, written with the Greek letter rho (ρ). It shows how closely two sets of ranks agree. Each person is ranked on both variables, the difference between each person's two ranks (D) is found, and ρ = 1 - 6ΣD² / N(N² - 1), where N is the number of pairs. Like Karl Pearson's r (1896), rho runs from -1 (perfect reversed agreement) through 0 (no relationship) to +1 (perfect agreement). Because it works on ranks, it suits ordinal data, small samples, relationships that rise or fall steadily but not in a straight line, and data with outliers; it is the usual non-parametric correlation. For example, if two teachers, Mr Sharma and Ms Iyer, rank the same ten essays and their orders nearly match, rho will be close to +1; if the essay one ranks first the other ranks last, and so on down the list, rho will be close to -1. Option a is right because rho is a correlation coefficient calculated on ranks. Exam trap: the formula contains squared differences, so students confuse it with variance or standard deviation; but those are measures of dispersion that describe the spread of one variable, while rho measures the association between two.", "traps": {"b": "Measures of dispersion (range, quartile deviation, mean deviation, standard deviation, variance) describe how spread out the scores of one variable are; rho describes the relationship between two ranked variables.", "c": "The normal probability curve (Abraham de Moivre, Carl Friedrich Gauss) and its z tables give the probability of scores falling in a region of the distribution; rho is not a probability measure.", "d": "Spearman's rho is a standard correlation coefficient, so 'none of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:", "concept": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport organised traits into cardinal, central and secondary traits. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make behaviour consistent across situations. He ranked a person's traits by how pervasive they are, and in Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he renamed these individual traits 'personal dispositions'. A cardinal trait dominates almost everything a person does, so much that the person becomes known by it, as in Machiavellian, narcissistic or Christlike; few people have one. Central traits are the 5 to 10 broad qualities that describe most people well, the words you would use in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious, anxious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent dispositions that appear only in particular situations, such as a food preference or irritability in traffic. For example, Neha's central traits might be friendly, hard-working and talkative, while a secondary trait is that she becomes nervous only when speaking on stage; she has no single cardinal trait. Allport studied individuals in depth (the idiographic approach), for instance in Letters from Jenny (1965). Option d is right because cardinal, central and secondary are Allport's own three levels. Exam trap: 'secondary' also appears in option a, which makes 'primary, secondary, tertiary' look familiar; but Allport never used primary or tertiary, and 'primary' belongs to Raymond Cattell's (1949) 16 primary (first-order) factors, a different model.", "traps": {"a": "Primary, secondary and tertiary is a generic ranking (used, for example, for levels of prevention); it is not Allport's terminology, and Cattell's 'primary' factors belong to a different model.", "b": "Cognitive, emotional and physiological describe the components of an emotional or anxiety response, not levels of personality traits.", "c": "Id, ego and superego are Sigmund Freud's structures of the psyche (The Ego and the Id, 1923), part of psychoanalytic theory, not a trait hierarchy."}}, {"q": "Match List I with List II. A. Carl Rogers, B. Albert Ellis, C. Eric Berne, D. Albert Bandura, E. Kurt Lewin. i. Token economy, ii. Phenomenological reality, iii. Disputing, iv. Life space, v. Rackets and games. Codes in the order A, B, C, D, E:", "concept": "The answer is a: the code ii, iii, v, iv, i is the intended matching. Learn each pair. Carl Rogers (1951) built person-centred therapy on phenomenology: each person reacts to the world as he or she perceives it, so the client's subjective perceptual field is his or her reality (phenomenological reality), and the therapist tries to see the world through the client's eyes. Albert Ellis (1955) founded rational emotive behaviour therapy on the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it and emotional Consequences; therapy adds D, Disputing irrational beliefs ('I must be perfect'), and E, the Effective new belief. Eric Berne (1964), in Games People Play, described transactional analysis: games are repeated ulterior transactions with a predictable bad payoff, and rackets are familiar substitute feelings a person collects to justify a life script. Kurt Lewin (1936) described the life space, the whole psychological field of person and environment at a given moment, in his formula B = f(P, E). The token economy, in which tokens earned for target behaviours are exchanged for rewards, was built by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) and belongs to the behaviour modification tradition that Albert Bandura (1969) surveyed in Principles of Behavior Modification. When a therapist challenges Riya's belief that one bad mark makes her a failure, that is Ellis's disputing. Option a is right because it is the only code that matches Rogers, Ellis and Berne correctly. Exam trap: check Rogers first; phenomenological reality is the hallmark of the humanistic approach, and that single pairing rules out b, c and d. Key note: the strictly correct matching is ii, iii, v, i, iv (Bandura with token economy, Lewin with life space), and no code prints it exactly; code a gets Rogers, Ellis and Berne right and only swaps the last two, while every other code gets Rogers wrong, so a is the intended answer and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "i, ii, iii, iv, v simply pairs the lists in printed order: Rogers with token economy, Ellis with phenomenological reality, Berne with disputing and Lewin with rackets and games, all wrong.", "c": "iv, iii, ii, i, v gives Rogers life space (Lewin's term), Berne phenomenological reality (Rogers's idea) and Lewin rackets and games (Berne's terms); only Ellis and Bandura are placed acceptably.", "d": "v, iv, iii, ii, i reverses the list: Rogers with rackets and games, Ellis with life space, Berne with disputing and Lewin with token economy, so no pairing is right."}}, {"q": "______ is the description of the basic biological response to sustained and unrelenting physical stress.", "concept": "The answer is a: the general adaptation syndrome (GAS) describes the body's basic biological response to sustained, unrelenting stress. Hans Selye (1936) noticed that rats injected with many different harmful substances, or exposed to cold, injury or forced exercise, all showed the same triad: enlarged adrenal glands, a shrunken thymus and lymph nodes, and bleeding stomach ulcers. The response was general (the same whatever the stressor), adaptive (it helps the body cope) and a syndrome (a set of signs that occur together). In The Stress of Life (1956) he described three stages. Alarm reaction: the body detects the threat, and the sympathetic nervous system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis release adrenaline and cortisol; resistance dips briefly, then rises. Resistance: if the stress continues, the body stays mobilised at a high level and seems to cope, but it is using up its reserves. Exhaustion: with prolonged stress the reserves run out, resistance collapses and 'diseases of adaptation' such as ulcers, high blood pressure and lowered immunity appear. For example, Arjun works night shifts for months while caring for his sick father: at first he feels wired and alert (alarm), then he manages for a while (resistance), then he falls ill again and again and feels drained (exhaustion). Option a is right because the GAS is exactly Selye's model of the physical response to prolonged stress. Exam trap: the GAS is purely biological and the same for every stressor; it ignores appraisal, which is why Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) proposed the transactional model, in which stress depends on how a person evaluates the demand.", "traps": {"b": "The diathesis-stress paradigm (Paul Meehl, 1962, for schizophrenia) says a disorder appears when an inherited or early vulnerability meets enough stress; it explains who becomes ill, not the body's stage-by-stage stress response.", "c": "Two-factor theory names Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) theory of emotion (arousal plus a cognitive label) or O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) theory of avoidance learning; neither describes the biological response to stress.", "d": "Stress inoculation training (Donald Meichenbaum, 1985) is a cognitive-behavioural treatment that prepares people to cope with stress in three phases; it is a therapy, not a description of the body's response."}}, {"q": "In which of the following cases has learned helplessness theory been applied to depression?", "concept": "The answer is a: learned helplessness theory was applied to depression in the battered woman syndrome. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) found that dogs given inescapable electric shocks later failed to escape shocks they could easily have avoided by jumping a low barrier; they simply lay down. Lack of control, not the shock itself, taught them that nothing they did mattered. Seligman (1975) then proposed learned helplessness as a model of depression: after repeated uncontrollable bad events, people show passivity, low motivation, sadness and an inability to see that their actions could work. Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later added attributions: helplessness turns into depression when people blame internal, stable and global causes. Lenore Walker (1979), in The Battered Woman, used the theory to explain why many abused women do not leave violent partners. Beatings that come whatever the woman does teach her that her actions cannot stop the violence, so she becomes passive, depressed and unable to see ways out; Walker called this pattern the battered woman syndrome. She also described a cycle of violence: tension building, an acute battering incident, then loving contrition. Consider Meera, beaten by her husband whether she cooks, stays silent or argues; after years she stops trying, believes leaving is impossible and becomes deeply depressed, which is learned helplessness in human form. Option a is right because the battered woman syndrome is the best-known application of learned helplessness to human depression. Exam trap: substance misuse and alcohol abuse tempt because depressed people may drink, but those problems are explained mainly by reinforcement, tension-reduction and expectancy models, not by learned helplessness.", "traps": {"b": "Personality disorders are explained mainly by developmental, trait and biosocial models (for example Marsha Linehan's biosocial theory of borderline personality disorder, 1993); they are not the classic application of learned helplessness.", "c": "Substance misuse is usually explained by reinforcement, reward-circuit and expectancy models; learned helplessness was not built as an account of drug use.", "d": "Alcohol abuse is explained by tension-reduction, expectancy and genetic models; it is a narrower form of option c and, like it, is not where learned helplessness was applied to depression."}}, {"q": "The physical attractiveness of a person, or any other trait, can influence multiple judgements and lead to a more favourable view of the whole personality. This tendency is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: when one attractive trait colours our judgement of the whole person, it is called the halo effect. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after studying how army officers rated their soldiers on physique, intelligence, leadership and character. The ratings correlated far too highly: a soldier judged handsome and well built was also rated more intelligent and more loyal, as if one good impression cast a glow over everything else. The halo is a general impression, or one striking trait, spreading to other, unrelated traits. Physical attractiveness is the strongest trigger. Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster (1972) showed students photographs of attractive, average and unattractive people; the attractive ones were judged kinder, more sociable, more successful and happier: 'what is beautiful is good'. The reverse, one bad trait dragging down the whole judgement, is sometimes called the horns effect. The halo also biases ratings by teachers, interviewers and clinicians, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. At a job interview, for example, Rohan is smart, confident and well dressed, and the panel also assumes he is honest and hard-working, although they have no evidence on those traits. Option c is right because the stem describes one trait shaping many unrelated judgements and a more favourable view of the whole personality. Exam trap: the Pygmalion effect also involves expectations about a person, but it concerns how those expectations change the person's actual performance; the halo effect is a bias in the perceiver's judgement only.", "traps": {"a": "The primacy effect, shown by Solomon Asch (1946) with trait lists read in different orders, is the greater weight given to information that comes first; it concerns the order of information, not one trait spreading to others.", "b": "The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is a self-fulfilling prophecy in which teachers' raised expectations led to real gains in pupils' performance; it changes the target's behaviour rather than biasing the observer's overall view.", "d": "Non-common effects belong to Edward Jones and Keith Davis's correspondent inference theory (1965): we infer a person's intentions from the unique outcomes of the option they chose; it is an attribution rule, not a rating bias."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT considered to be a risk factor for personality disorders?", "concept": "The answer is c: gender is not counted as a risk factor for personality disorders as a whole. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, begins by adolescence or early adulthood and causes distress or impairment (DSM-5, 2013). About one adult in ten in community surveys meets the criteria for at least one. Epidemiological studies agree on several correlates. Age: rates are highest in younger adults and fall with age, especially for the dramatic, impulsive cluster B disorders such as antisocial and borderline, which tend to soften by middle life. Place: rates are higher among people living in inner cities, where poverty, crime, family breakdown and weak social support cluster. Social class: low income, unemployment and less education are consistently linked with higher rates. Gender is different. Specific disorders do differ: antisocial personality disorder is diagnosed far more often in men, and borderline personality disorder is diagnosed more often in women in clinics, although community rates are closer to equal. When all personality disorders are counted together, however, studies show no consistent difference between men and women, so gender is not listed as a general risk factor. Consider Rahul, a 24-year-old unemployed man living in a crowded inner-city area: he carries three of the listed risk markers (youth, place and class), while being male raises only his chance of the antisocial type in particular. Option c is right because youth, inner-city living and low socioeconomic class are established risk markers, whereas overall prevalence does not differ consistently by gender. Exam trap: the well-known sex ratios for antisocial (men) and borderline (women) tempt students to call gender a risk factor; those ratios apply to single disorders, not to personality disorders overall.", "traps": {"a": "Living in inner cities is a recognised correlate: urban surveys find higher rates where poverty, crime, family breakdown and weak social networks cluster.", "b": "Low socioeconomic class (low income, unemployment, little education) is one of the most consistent correlates of personality disorder in community surveys.", "d": "Being a young adult is a risk marker: personality disorders, especially the cluster B types, are most common in early adulthood and become less common with age."}}, {"q": "Behavioural Self-Control Training (BSCT) is based on conditioning principles. These include which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: stimulus control, rewards for abstinence and self-monitoring are all parts of behavioural self-control training (BSCT). BSCT was developed in the 1970s by William Miller, with Ricardo Munoz (How to Control Your Drinking, 1976), for problem drinkers, often those aiming to cut down (controlled drinking) rather than stop, and it is used with other drugs too. It treats substance use as learned behaviour: situations become cues for drinking (classical conditioning) and the effects of the drink reinforce it (operant conditioning), so the client learns to rearrange the cues and the consequences. The main components are these. Goal setting: the client sets clear limits, such as no more than two drinks and none on weekdays, or chooses abstinence. Self-monitoring: a diary of when, where, with whom and how much drinking or drug use occurs, which makes patterns and triggers visible (option c). Stimulus control: changing the cues that set off use, for example avoiding certain bars, not keeping alcohol at home and drinking only with food (option a). Rate control: sipping, spacing drinks and alternating with soft drinks. Self-reinforcement: rewarding oneself for meeting the limits or staying abstinent (option b). Coping skills: refusal skills, other ways of handling stress, and relapse prevention. Kabir's diary shows that he drinks heavily every Friday with office friends; he starts meeting them at a cafe instead, keeps to two drinks on Saturdays, and buys himself a cricket ticket after a month within his limits. Option d is right because BSCT combines all three techniques in one package. Exam trap: each option is genuinely part of BSCT, so a candidate who recognises one component may pick it alone; 'all of the above' is correct when every statement is itself a component.", "traps": {"a": "Stimulus control (rearranging the cues that trigger drinking or drug use) is part of BSCT, but it is only one of several components.", "b": "Rewarding oneself for abstinence or for staying within limits (self-reinforcement, an operant principle) is part of BSCT, but not the whole of it.", "c": "Self-monitoring of when and how often drug use occurs is the first step of BSCT, but the programme also includes stimulus control and self-reinforcement."}}, {"q": "On what basis did Jean Piaget give his theory of cognitive development?", "concept": "The answer is b: Jean Piaget's theory of cognitive development rests on fundamental research. Fundamental (basic or pure) research seeks knowledge for its own sake: it aims to discover general principles and build theory, not to solve an immediate practical problem. Applied research, by contrast, uses knowledge to solve a specific practical problem. Jean Piaget (1936), trained as a biologist, wanted to answer a philosophical question: how does knowledge grow? He called this field genetic epistemology. While standardising reasoning tests in Paris around 1920, he became curious about the systematic wrong answers children gave. He then used the clinical method, a flexible questioning interview, with Swiss schoolchildren, and made detailed observations of his own three children, Jacqueline, Lucienne and Laurent, from infancy. From this he built a general theory: children actively construct knowledge through assimilation and accommodation and pass through four stages (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational). Nothing in this work aimed to fix a school, treat a child or judge a programme; it aimed to explain how thinking develops. Teachers later applied the theory (discovery learning, readiness), but that use was applied work built on Piaget's basic research. If Priya studies how children of different ages understand conservation of liquid, to learn how thinking develops, that is fundamental research; if she tests whether a new maths textbook improves Class 3 results in Pune, that is applied research. Option b is right because Piaget's aim was a general theory of how knowledge develops, the hallmark of fundamental research. Exam trap: because Piaget's ideas are used so widely in classrooms, students call his work applied research; the classroom use came later, while his own studies sought general understanding.", "traps": {"a": "Evaluation research judges whether a programme or policy works (formative and summative evaluation, Michael Scriven, 1967); Piaget was not assessing any programme.", "c": "Applied research tries to solve a specific practical problem, such as improving reading in a school; Piaget's theory was later applied in education, but his own research aimed at general understanding.", "d": "Action research, named by Kurt Lewin (1946), is carried out by practitioners in their own setting in cycles of planning, acting, observing and reflecting to change local practice; Piaget's work was not of this kind."}}, {"q": "A researcher plans to approach counselors as a participant seeking their services and to record their sessions without the counselors' knowledge to assess their competence. What ethical violations should the researcher safeguard against?", "concept": "The answer is c: the researcher must guard against both problems: the deception must be justifiable to the participants, and the counsellors must be protected from harm. The plan raises three ethical issues: the counsellors are deceived (the 'client' is really a researcher), they have not consented to take part, and their sessions are recorded without their knowledge. The American Psychological Association's Ethics Code (2002, standard 8.07) allows deception only under strict conditions: the study must have significant scientific, educational or applied value; no effective non-deceptive method can be available; the deception must never concern anything likely to cause physical pain or severe emotional distress; and participants must be told the truth (debriefed) as early as feasible, preferably at the end of their participation, and allowed to withdraw their data. That is why option a matters: at debriefing, the counsellors should be able to see that the deception was reasonably needed, since a counsellor who knows he or she is being tested behaves differently. Option b matters too: the counsellors could suffer real harm, such as embarrassment, damage to their reputation or even loss of their jobs, if recordings that judged them incompetent were shared. Protection from harm here means keeping the recordings confidential, anonymising the results, storing the data securely and never letting the findings affect anyone's employment; because recording normally needs consent, the counsellors' permission to keep and use the recordings must be sought at debriefing. Option c is right because the plan raises both a justified-deception issue and a risk of harm, so both safeguards apply. Exam trap: many candidates see the word 'deception' and choose a alone, forgetting that the people being deceived are themselves research participants entitled to protection from harm.", "traps": {"a": "Justifying the deception (and debriefing so that participants understand why it was needed) is a required safeguard, but on its own it ignores the risk of harm to the counsellors.", "b": "Protecting the counsellors from harm (confidential recordings, no effect on their jobs) is essential, but the study also relies on deception, which needs its own justification and debriefing.", "d": "The design clearly raises ethical problems (deception, no consent, covert recording), so 'none of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "A diagnostic formulation is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a diagnostic formulation is a descriptive statement about the nature and severity of the individual's psychiatric presentation. In the scheme of Len Sperry and colleagues (1992), a full case formulation has several parts. The diagnostic formulation is descriptive and cross-sectional: it answers 'what is going on, and how severe is it?' by summarising the presenting symptoms, the mental status findings and the resulting diagnosis (ICD or DSM). The clinical formulation is explanatory and longitudinal: it offers a rationale for how the symptoms and maladaptive life patterns developed and why they persist, often organised by the five Ps (presenting problem plus predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors). The treatment formulation follows from both and serves as an explicit blueprint for intervention: goals, methods and the order of treatment. Tracy Eells (1997), in the Handbook of Psychotherapy Case Formulation, defines a case formulation as a hypothesis about the causes, precipitants and maintaining influences of a person's problems that guides treatment. Consider Arjun, 30, with six weeks of low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest. 'Moderate depressive episode, no psychotic features' is the diagnostic formulation; the account linking his perfectionism, his father's criticism and a recent job loss is the clinical formulation; and the plan of twelve sessions of CBT plus a medication review is the treatment formulation. Option b is right because the diagnostic formulation describes and classifies the presentation rather than explaining it or planning treatment. Exam trap: options a and c are genuine descriptions of formulation, so 'all of the above' looks safe; but they describe the explanatory (clinical) and treatment parts of a case formulation, not the diagnostic part.", "traps": {"a": "Being explanatory and longitudinal, and giving a rationale for how symptoms develop and persist, defines the clinical (case) formulation, the step that follows the diagnostic one.", "c": "Serving as an explicit blueprint for treatment is the role of the treatment formulation, which builds on the diagnostic and clinical formulations.", "d": "Only b describes the diagnostic formulation; a and c describe other parts of a case formulation, so 'all of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 recognised the following about persons attempting suicide, in decriminalising attempted suicide:", "concept": "The answer is d: in decriminalising attempted suicide, the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 recognised all three ideas. Before the Act, section 309 of the Indian Penal Code (1860) made attempted suicide an offence punishable with up to one year of simple imprisonment, a fine or both, so survivors could be prosecuted at the very moment they most needed help. Section 115(1) of the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 says that, notwithstanding section 309, any person who attempts suicide shall be presumed, unless proved otherwise, to have severe stress and shall not be tried and punished under the Code. Section 115(2) places a duty on the appropriate government to provide care, treatment and rehabilitation to such a person to reduce the risk of another attempt. These words carry the three ideas in the options: recognition of the suffering the person is going through (a), care and consideration instead of criminal prosecution (b), and a legal presumption that protects the person unless the contrary is proved (c). The Indian Penal Code itself has since been replaced by the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. For example, when Pooja, 19, survives an overdose after failing her exams, the hospital treats her and refers her for counselling instead of reporting her to the police for a case to be registered. Option d is right because the Act's approach rests on the person's suffering, a duty of care rather than punishment, and a protective presumption. Exam trap: students remember 'decriminalisation' but forget the presumption clause and the government's duty of care. Key note: the Act's actual words presume 'severe stress', not 'mental illness', so option c is loosely worded; the question intends that the Act recognised all three ideas (the suffering, care instead of prosecution, and a presumption that protects the person), and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Recognising the suffering of a person who attempts suicide is part of the reasoning behind section 115, but it is only one of the three ideas the Act reflects.", "b": "Care instead of prosecution is written into section 115: subsection (1) bars trial and punishment, and subsection (2) gives the government a duty to provide care, treatment and rehabilitation; but it is not the only idea the Act recognised.", "c": "Section 115(1) does create a presumption in the person's favour, though its words are 'severe stress' rather than 'mental illness'; it is one part of the answer, not the whole."}}, {"q": "The definition of mental illness in the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act (2016) includes:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 defines mental illness as a disorder of thinking, mood, perception, orientation or memory. The Act replaced the Persons with Disabilities Act 1995, widened the recognised disabilities from 7 to 21, and lists mental illness as one of them. Its schedule defines mental illness as 'a substantial disorder of thinking, mood, perception, orientation or memory that grossly impairs judgment, behaviour, capacity to recognise reality or ability to meet the ordinary demands of life'. It then adds an exclusion: the term 'does not include retardation which is a condition of arrested or incomplete development of mind of a person, specially characterised by subnormality of intelligence'. Intellectual disability appears separately in the Act as a disability of its own, so it is not counted twice. A person with 40 percent or more of a specified disability has a 'benchmark disability', which opens reservations and other benefits. For example, Farhan, who has schizophrenia with hallucinations and poor judgement, falls under the RPwD category of mental illness, while his cousin with intellectual disability is covered under the separate category of intellectual disability. Option a is right because the five domains of thinking, mood, perception, orientation and memory form the core of the Act's definition. Exam trap: option b quotes the Act's own words, but they come from the exclusion clause, so a candidate who recognises the phrase may tick it. Key note: the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 definition adds 'mental conditions associated with the abuse of alcohol and drugs', which the RPwD definition does not; so c belongs to the MHCA, b is explicitly excluded, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Arrested or incomplete development of mind with subnormal intelligence is the Act's description of intellectual disability ('retardation'), which is expressly excluded from mental illness and listed as a separate disability.", "c": "Mental conditions associated with the abuse of alcohol and drugs appear in the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 definition of mental illness, not in the RPwD Act 2016 definition.", "d": "Option b is expressly excluded and option c belongs to a different Act, so 'all of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled from the:", "concept": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a rapid, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your foot off a sharp stone or the knee jerk when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the circuit behind it, the reflex arc. The arc has five links: a receptor picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal into the spinal cord through the dorsal root; an interneuron in the grey matter passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out through the ventral root; and an effector, a muscle or gland, responds. The loop closes inside the cord, so the response starts before the message reaches the brain, which is why you feel the pain a fraction of a second after you have already moved. The knee-jerk (patellar) reflex is even simpler: it is monosynaptic, with the sensory neuron synapsing straight onto the motor neuron and no interneuron in between. Example: Kabir steps on a thorn while walking barefoot in the garden, and his leg lifts before he shouts; his spinal cord acted first and informed his brain afterwards. Option b is right because the classic limb and body reflexes are organised at the level of the spinal cord, without needing the brain. Exam trap: the medulla (c) does control some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting, as well as the vital rhythms of breathing and heart rate, so it looks attractive; but the word 'usually' points to the spinal cord, the centre for most reflexes in the body.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex and the prefrontal areas that plan voluntary, deliberate action; reflexes bypass this conscious control, which is why they are automatic.", "c": "The medulla oblongata in the hindbrain runs a few brainstem reflexes (coughing, sneezing, swallowing, vomiting) and the vital centres for breathing and heart rate, but most reflexes in the body close their loop in the spinal cord.", "d": "The pons is the hindbrain 'bridge' that relays signals between the cerebrum and the cerebellum and helps regulate breathing rhythm and REM sleep; it carries a few cranial reflexes, such as the corneal blink, but it is not the usual centre for reflexes."}}, {"q": "What is a functional hallucination?", "concept": "The answer is b: a functional hallucination is one in which a stimulus in one sensory modality causes a hallucination in the same modality. A hallucination is normally a perception without any external object, experienced as real. In a functional hallucination, however, a real stimulus is present, and the hallucination occurs only while that stimulus lasts. The person perceives both at once: the normal perception and the false one, side by side. Frank Fish (1967), in Clinical Psychopathology, gives the classic example of a patient who hears voices only when the water tap is running; he hears the water as well as the voices, and the voices stop when the tap is turned off. Example: Meera, who has schizophrenia, reports that whenever the ceiling fan whirs she hears a man muttering insults. She can hear the fan clearly too, and the muttering vanishes when the fan is switched off. Both the trigger and the voice are auditory, so this is a functional hallucination. Compare it with an illusion, in which the real stimulus itself is misperceived (the fan's hum is heard as words); in a functional hallucination the real sound stays a normal sound and the voice is added on top. Option b is right because the trigger and the hallucination share the same sense, and both are experienced together. Exam trap: the reflex hallucination (option a) also needs a real stimulus, but the hallucination appears in a different modality, for example feeling a touch on the skin every time a door bangs, a kind of morbid synaesthesia. Examiners swap the two definitions, so remember: functional, same sense; reflex, another sense.", "traps": {"a": "This defines a reflex hallucination: a real stimulus in one sense produces a hallucination in a different sense (a sound triggers a felt touch), a form of morbid synaesthesia. The functional type keeps both in the same sense.", "c": "Both cannot describe one phenomenon, because the two definitions differ on the crucial point, same versus different modality; they name two separate types, functional and reflex hallucinations.", "d": "Option b gives the standard textbook definition of a functional hallucination, so 'none of the above' does not fit."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is NOT correct in respect of speed tests?", "concept": "The answer is b: the statement that all the items in a speed test are of different degrees of difficulty is NOT correct. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, classes tests by how they use time. A pure speed test has items that are uniformly easy, all of about the same low difficulty, so that anyone given enough time would get nearly all of them right. What separates people is how many items they finish correctly within a strict time limit, set so short that hardly anyone completes the test. A clerical checking test, where you mark which pairs of names are identical, or a letter-cancellation task is a typical example. A pure power test is the opposite: the items climb steadily in difficulty until some are too hard for anyone, and the time is generous or unlimited, so the score reflects the highest level a person can reach. Most real tests mix the two. Example: Arjun and Kabir take a symbol-matching test with 200 easy items in 3 minutes. Arjun finishes 140 and Kabir 110, and both make almost no errors, so the difference in their scores is speed, not knowledge. Option b is right because varied, rising difficulty is the mark of a power test, not a speed test. Exam trap: option c ('the test taker is required to answer all the items') sounds false because few people finish a speed test; but the instruction to attempt every item as fast as possible is part of how a speed test is given, so it is a stated feature, whereas b reverses the defining feature. Note too that split-half reliability is falsely inflated on speed tests, so test-retest or parallel forms are preferred.", "traps": {"a": "This is true of a speed test: because the items are easy, the score depends on how many are answered correctly in the time allowed, that is, on the speed of working.", "c": "This describes how a speed test is administered: examinees are told to try every item as quickly as possible, even though the time limit is set so that few actually finish. It is a stated feature, not the false statement.", "d": "This is the defining feature of a speed test: the items are homogeneous and of uniformly low difficulty, so differences in score come from speed rather than from the level of difficulty a person can handle."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following would a client-centred therapist use?", "concept": "The answer is a: a client-centred therapist would use empathy. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, built his approach (later called person-centred therapy) on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, which gets blocked when love and approval are given only on conditions of worth. In a famous 1957 paper Rogers set out the 'necessary and sufficient conditions' for therapeutic change, and three of them became the core conditions the therapist offers: congruence (being genuine), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance without judgement) and accurate empathic understanding (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding back). The therapist is non-directive: no interpretation, no advice, no homework. The main skill is reflection of feeling. Example: Riya says, 'Everyone at home thinks I'm lazy, but I'm just so tired all the time.' A client-centred counsellor replies, 'It sounds exhausting, and hurtful, to feel so drained and then be judged for it.' She is not diagnosing or teaching; she is mirroring Riya's experience so that Riya can hear herself more clearly and move towards her own solutions. Option a is right because empathy is one of the three core conditions at the heart of Rogers's therapy. Exam trap: the other options are named techniques or concepts from directive therapies, used as look-alikes: desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's behaviour therapy, while rackets and ego states belong to Eric Berne's transactional analysis. If an option is something the therapist does to the client, it is not client-centred.", "traps": {"b": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) behaviour therapy: relaxation paired with a graded anxiety hierarchy, based on reciprocal inhibition. It is directive and technique-led, the opposite of Rogers's non-directive style.", "c": "The racket system (Richard Erskine and Marilyn Zalcman, 1979) is a model within transactional analysis, the school founded by Eric Berne (Transactional Analysis in Psychotherapy, 1961): a self-reinforcing loop of script beliefs, feelings and behaviours. It is analysed by a TA therapist, not offered by a client-centred one.", "d": "Ego states (Parent, Adult, Child) are the basic units of Eric Berne's transactional analysis (Games People Play, 1964); the TA therapist analyses transactions between them, an interpretive, teaching approach unlike Rogers's reflection."}}, {"q": "Which hormone is responsible for sending signals to the brain that you need food?", "concept": "The answer is b: ghrelin is the hormone that sends the brain the signal that you need food. Ghrelin was discovered by Masayasu Kojima and colleagues (1999). It is made mainly by cells in the lining of the stomach; its blood level rises before meals, when the stomach is empty, and falls after eating. It travels to the hypothalamus, where it acts on the arcuate nucleus, exciting neurons that release neuropeptide Y and agouti-related peptide, which start eating. For this reason it is called the 'hunger hormone', and injected ghrelin makes people and animals eat more. Its partner is leptin, discovered by Jeffrey Friedman's team (1994): leptin is released by fat cells in proportion to body fat and tells the hypothalamus that energy stores are full, so it reduces appetite over the long term. Mice that cannot make leptin (ob/ob mice) become hugely obese. Example: Arjun skips lunch during exam revision, and by 4 pm his ghrelin has climbed and he cannot stop thinking about samosas. After he eats, ghrelin drops, and gut signals such as cholecystokinin and stomach stretch help him feel full. Option b is right because ghrelin is the circulating hunger signal sent from the empty stomach to the brain. Exam trap: leptin (d) is also an appetite hormone, so students mix the two up; but leptin is the satiety signal from fat, the opposite message. A memory aid: ghrelin makes the stomach 'growl'; leptin helps you stay 'lean'.", "traps": {"a": "Glucagon is made by the alpha cells of the pancreas and raises blood glucose by releasing stored glycogen from the liver between meals; it manages fuel supply, not the hunger message to the brain.", "c": "Oxytocin, made in the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary, triggers contractions in labour and milk let-down and is linked to bonding and trust; it is not a hunger signal.", "d": "Leptin (Jeffrey Friedman's team, 1994) comes from fat cells and tells the hypothalamus that fat stores are adequate, reducing appetite; it is the long-term satiety signal, the opposite of what the stem asks."}}, {"q": "'Multiple intelligence theory' was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: multiple intelligence theory was proposed by Howard Gardner (1983) in his book Frames of Mind. Gardner argued that intelligence is not one general ability that a single IQ score can capture, but a set of relatively independent abilities, each with its own brain systems. He used eight criteria to decide what counts as an intelligence, including that it can be selectively destroyed by brain damage, that it shows up in prodigies and savants, and that it has its own developmental history. His original list had seven intelligences: linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal (understanding other people) and intrapersonal (understanding oneself). In Intelligence Reframed (1999) he added an eighth, naturalist (recognising and classifying plants, animals and patterns in nature), and discussed a possible existential intelligence. Example: Kabir struggles with algebra, but he can play a raga on the flute after hearing it once and is the team captain everyone trusts. Gardner would say Kabir is high in musical and interpersonal intelligence, strengths that an ordinary IQ test would miss. Option b is right because Gardner is the author of the theory of multiple intelligences. Exam trap: Joy Paul Guilford (d) also rejected a single general factor and proposed many abilities in his Structure of Intellect model (1967), so his name is the near miss; but 'multiple intelligences' is Gardner's term. Lewis Terman and Alfred Binet built intelligence tests that give a single overall score.", "traps": {"a": "Lewis Terman (1916) revised the Binet-Simon scale as the Stanford-Binet and popularised the IQ; he treated intelligence as a general ability summed up in one score, not as multiple intelligences.", "c": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test to identify schoolchildren who needed special help; his scale gave one overall mental level, not separate intelligences.", "d": "Joy Paul Guilford's Structure of Intellect model (1967) crossed operations, contents and products to give many distinct abilities (120 at first, later 180); it is a factor-analytic model, not the multiple intelligences theory."}}, {"q": "Language deficits are among the most common features of neurological disorders, and are collectively known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: language deficits caused by neurological disorders are collectively known as aphasias. Aphasia is an acquired disorder of language, the ability to produce or understand spoken and written words, caused by damage to the language areas of the brain, usually in the left hemisphere after a stroke, head injury, tumour or dementia. It is not a weakness of the speech muscles (that is dysarthria), and it is not a lifelong developmental delay. Paul Broca (1861) described patients with damage to the left inferior frontal lobe who spoke slowly, with great effort and few words, but understood well: Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia. Carl Wernicke (1874) described damage to the left posterior superior temporal lobe giving fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Other types include conduction aphasia (poor repetition), global aphasia (loss of almost all language) and anomic aphasia (mainly word-finding difficulty). Example: after a stroke, Meera's grandfather understands everything said to him but can only manage 'tea... want... now'; this is Broca's aphasia. Option d is right because 'aphasias' is the umbrella term for the whole family of language disorders that follow neurological damage. Exam trap: dysphasia (a) means a partial or milder loss of language and is used as a synonym in some British texts, so it is the near miss; but the standard collective term in neuropsychology is aphasia. Anomia (c) is only one type, and alogia (b) is a negative symptom of schizophrenia.", "traps": {"a": "Dysphasia means a partial or milder impairment of language; some British texts use it interchangeably with aphasia, but the standard umbrella term for neurological language deficits is aphasia.", "b": "Alogia, poverty of speech (few words and empty replies), is a negative symptom of schizophrenia listed in Nancy Andreasen's Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (1982); it reflects impoverished thinking, not a neurological language disorder.", "c": "Anomia, difficulty finding the names of things, is one symptom and one type (anomic aphasia) within the aphasias, not the collective name for all of them."}}, {"q": "______ is NOT a common data collection method in qualitative research.", "concept": "The answer is b: the structured interview is NOT a common data collection method in qualitative research. Qualitative research aims to understand meanings, experiences and context in depth, in participants' own words, and its data are words, images and observations rather than numbers. Its usual methods are flexible and open: participant observation, where the researcher joins a group and records what happens (as in ethnography); in-depth unstructured or semi-structured interviews, which follow a loose guide and let participants lead; focus groups, guided discussions among about 6 to 10 people; and the analysis of documents, diaries, media and other texts. A structured interview, by contrast, asks every respondent the same fixed questions in the same order, often with fixed response options, so that answers can be coded and counted. It is really a spoken questionnaire, the tool of surveys and epidemiological studies, and it belongs to quantitative research. Example: Kabir is studying how new mothers in Pune experience postpartum depression. Sitting in support groups, holding open-ended interviews and running a focus group all fit his qualitative aim; reading out a fixed checklist with yes or no answers would give him numbers, not stories. Option b is right because fixed questions and fixed answers suit measurement, not the open exploration that qualitative work needs. Exam trap: content analysis (c) can be quantitative (counting words or themes) as well as qualitative, so students think it is the odd one out; but it is a standard way to analyse qualitative text and documents, so it is a common qualitative method.", "traps": {"a": "Participant observation, in which the researcher takes part in the group's daily life and records it, is the classic method of ethnography and a core qualitative technique.", "c": "Content analysis of documents, media and transcripts can count words quantitatively, but it is also a standard qualitative method for identifying themes and meanings in text.", "d": "Focus groups, moderated discussions among a small group, are widely used in qualitative research to explore shared views and the language people use about a topic."}}, {"q": "The 16PF test is based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: the 16PF is based on Raymond Cattell's theory. Raymond Cattell, a British-born psychologist who worked in the United States, built his trait theory by factor analysis, a statistical method that finds the few underlying factors that explain the correlations among many measures. He started from the trait words Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, merged synonyms, and factor-analysed three kinds of data: L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). He separated surface traits, clusters of behaviour that go together, from source traits, the deeper underlying causes. He found 16 source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire, the 16PF (1949), such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance and liveliness, each scored as a bipolar dimension. Factor-analysing the 16 again gives broader second-order (global) factors such as extraversion and anxiety. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and liveliness but low emotional stability, which helps her counsellor see a sociable young woman who is easily upset under pressure. Option b is right because Cattell designed the 16PF to measure the source traits of his own factor-analytic theory. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (a) also used factor analysis, but he arrived at only three broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); Gordon Allport (c) preferred the idiographic study of the individual and did not build the 16PF.", "traps": {"a": "Hans Eysenck's theory has three broad, biologically based dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); it is a rival factor model, not the basis of the 16PF.", "c": "Gordon Allport (1937) described cardinal, central and secondary traits and favoured the idiographic study of the unique individual; he did not use factor analysis to derive 16 source traits.", "d": "The 16PF has a clear theoretical source, Cattell's factor-analytic trait theory, so 'none of the above' does not fit."}}, {"q": "Sleep spindles and K-complexes can be seen in which stage of sleep?", "concept": "The answer is b: sleep spindles and K-complexes are the signature of stage 2 sleep. Sleep is staged from the EEG (electroencephalogram), the record of the brain's electrical waves. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep, and the manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968) set out stages 1 to 4 plus REM; the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007) now calls them N1, N2, N3 and REM, merging stages 3 and 4 into N3. Awake and relaxed with eyes closed, the EEG shows alpha waves. Stage 1 (N1) is light, drowsy sleep with theta waves, vertex sharp waves and often hypnic jerks. Stage 2 (N2) is true sleep with a theta background plus two hallmarks: sleep spindles, brief bursts of 12 to 14 Hz waves lasting about half a second or more, and K-complexes, single large, sharp waves that are often set off by a sound. Stage 2 fills about half of a night's sleep. Stages 3 and 4 (N3) are deep, slow-wave sleep with large delta waves. REM sleep shows a fast, low-amplitude EEG like waking, rapid eye movements, vivid dreams and loss of muscle tone. Example: in a sleep laboratory, Arjun's trace shows a burst of spindles and then a large K-complex when a corridor door clicks shut; the technician scores that stretch of the record as stage 2. Option b is right because spindles and K-complexes define stage 2. Exam trap: spindles can also appear briefly in deeper sleep, so c tempts some students; but stages 3 and 4 are defined by delta waves, and stage 1 (theta, vertex waves) has neither feature.", "traps": {"a": "Stage 1 (N1) is the drowsy transition from waking, with low-voltage theta waves, vertex sharp waves, slow rolling eye movements and hypnic jerks; spindles and K-complexes have not yet appeared.", "c": "Stages 3 and 4 (N3, slow-wave sleep) are defined by high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves; this is the deepest sleep, when sleepwalking and night terrors occur.", "d": "REM sleep shows a fast, low-voltage, desynchronised EEG resembling waking, with rapid eye movements, muscle atonia and vivid dreaming; spindles and K-complexes are absent."}}, {"q": "Who is the person behind the rise of the 'mental hygiene movement'?", "concept": "The answer is a: among these options, Dorothea Dix is the person behind the rise of the mental hygiene movement. Dorothea Dix (1802 to 1887) was a Boston schoolteacher who, in 1841, went to teach a Sunday class at a jail in East Cambridge, Massachusetts, and found mentally ill people kept in cold, filthy cells alongside criminals. Over the following decades she toured jails and almshouses, recorded the conditions in detail and presented the evidence to state legislatures, beginning with her Memorial to the Legislature of Massachusetts (1843). Her lobbying led to the founding or enlargement of dozens of state mental hospitals in the United States, and she carried the campaign to Canada and Scotland. Abnormal psychology textbooks teach her work as part of the mental hygiene movement: the push for humane, public, medical care of the mentally ill instead of neglect and punishment. In hindsight, many of the large hospitals she helped build later became overcrowded custodial institutions. Option a is right because Dix is the reformer among the options whose campaigns are taught under the mental hygiene movement. Exam trap: Philippe Pinel (b) is the other famous reformer, but he belongs to moral treatment in France, when he removed the chains from patients at Bicetre in 1793; examiners rely on students merging the two reform movements. Key note: the organised mental hygiene movement itself was founded by Clifford Beers, a former patient whose book A Mind That Found Itself (1908) led to the Connecticut Society for Mental Hygiene (1908) and the National Committee for Mental Hygiene (1909), with Adolf Meyer suggesting the name 'mental hygiene'. Beers is not an option, and standard textbooks (for example James Butcher, Susan Mineka and Jill Hooley) teach Dix's campaigning under the same movement, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Philippe Pinel removed the chains from patients at Bicetre in Paris (1793) and introduced moral treatment (kindness, routine and occupation); that is the earlier moral treatment movement, not the mental hygiene movement.", "c": "Hippocrates (about 460 to 370 BC) gave the first naturalistic account of mental illness as a brain disorder caused by an imbalance of the four humours; he lived more than two thousand years before any hygiene movement.", "d": "Plato (about 427 to 347 BC) saw madness partly as a disorder of the soul and urged that the mentally ill be cared for by their families rather than punished; he is a philosophical forerunner, not a reformer of asylum care."}}, {"q": "X believes there is one general intelligence that overlaps with many abilities. Which of the following theorists is most likely to agree with X?", "concept": "The answer is b: Charles Spearman is the theorist most likely to agree with X. Charles Spearman (1904) noticed that schoolchildren's scores on very different tasks, such as classics, French, mathematics and pitch discrimination, were all positively correlated: a child who did well on one tended to do well on the others. To explain this pattern he devised an early form of factor analysis and proposed his two-factor theory, set out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Every mental task draws on g, a general intelligence common to all tasks, which he likened to a kind of mental energy, plus s, a specific factor unique to that task. Tasks such as reasoning and analogies load heavily on g, while simple sensory tasks depend mostly on s. This is exactly X's view: one general ability that overlaps with, and runs beneath, many separate abilities. Example: Meera tops her class in mathematics, Hindi and science, and also picks up chess quickly. A Spearman-style explanation is that her high g feeds all these performances, while her particular flair for chess adds a specific s. Option b is right because the idea of a single general intelligence running through all abilities is Spearman's g. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike (a) is the classic opponent of g; his multifactor theory held that intelligence is many separate abilities (he described abstract, mechanical and social intelligence in 1920) with no single general factor, so he would disagree with X. Alfred Binet and Lewis Terman built tests that give one overall score, which tempts students, but the theory of g itself is Spearman's.", "traps": {"a": "Edward Thorndike held a multifactor view: intelligence is made of many specific abilities, grouped as abstract, mechanical and social (1920), with no single general factor, the opposite of X's belief.", "c": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence scale; he saw intelligence as a blend of judgement, comprehension and reasoning and was a test-maker, not the author of the g theory.", "d": "Lewis Terman (1916) adapted Binet's scale into the Stanford-Binet and popularised the IQ; his test gives one overall score, but the theory of general intelligence is Spearman's."}}, {"q": "Who first attempted to describe personality traits in terms of lexical descriptors?", "concept": "The answer is b: Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) first attempted to describe personality traits through lexical descriptors. Their starting point was the lexical hypothesis: the differences between people that matter in daily life will have been noticed and named in ordinary language, so the dictionary is a store of trait terms, and the more important a trait, the more words a language has for it. Francis Galton (1884) had sketched the idea by estimating the number of character words in a thesaurus, but Allport and Odbert carried out the first systematic search. In their monograph Trait-Names: A Psycho-Lexical Study, they went through Webster's New International Dictionary and culled 17,953 words that distinguish one person's behaviour from another's. They sorted these into four lists: only about 4,500 named stable, consistent traits (sociable, honest); the rest described temporary states (flustered), evaluations (admirable) or physical features and abilities. Raymond Cattell then reduced the stable-trait list to 171 terms and, by factor analysis, to 16 source traits, and later lexical research led to the Big Five. Example: when Kabir's friends describe him as 'generous', 'reliable' and 'shy', each word is a lexical descriptor, the raw material Allport and Odbert collected. Option b is right because their 1936 dictionary study was the first major lexical description of traits. Exam trap: Raymond Cattell (c) is closely linked with the lexical approach, but he worked from Allport and Odbert's list afterwards; Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (d) came decades later with the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and the five-factor model.", "traps": {"a": "Ross Stagner wrote one of the first personality textbooks, Psychology of Personality (1937), in the same year as Allport's own; he is not linked with the lexical search of the dictionary.", "c": "Raymond Cattell built on Allport and Odbert's list afterwards, merging synonyms to 171 terms and factor-analysing them down to 16 source traits (16PF, 1949); he refined the lexical approach rather than starting it.", "d": "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae developed the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and championed the five-factor model decades later, using questionnaire research that grew out of earlier lexical studies."}}, {"q": "What is the name of the conceptual framework within which research is carried out?", "concept": "The answer is c: the conceptual framework within which research is carried out is called a research paradigm. The term comes from Thomas Kuhn (1962), The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, who used it for the shared set of assumptions, accepted theories, methods and standard problems that a scientific community works within during a period of 'normal science'. When unexplained findings pile up, a scientific revolution brings a 'paradigm shift'. In research methods, a paradigm is the researcher's basic worldview, made up of beliefs about what reality is (ontology), how it can be known (epistemology) and which methods suit it (methodology). The main paradigms are positivism and post-positivism (an objective reality measured by experiments and statistics), interpretivism or constructivism (reality as the meanings people construct, studied through interviews and observation) and the critical paradigm (research aimed at exposing power and changing society); pragmatism underlies mixed methods. Example: Riya wants to study exam stress in nursing students. Working in the positivist paradigm, she would measure cortisol levels and stress-scale scores; working in the interpretivist paradigm, she would interview students about what exams mean to them. The paradigm frames every later choice she makes. Option c is right because a paradigm is the overarching conceptual framework that guides the questions, design and methods of research. Exam trap: research design (d) sounds similar, but it is the specific plan of a single study (experimental, correlational, cross-sectional), chosen within a paradigm, not the framework itself.", "traps": {"a": "A research hypothesis is a specific, testable prediction about the relationship between variables (for example, 'sleep loss lowers recall'); it is derived from theory within a paradigm, not the framework itself.", "b": "A synopsis is a short written summary of a proposed study (aims, method, expected results), usually submitted for approval; it is a document, not a conceptual framework.", "d": "Research design is the concrete blueprint for one study: how participants are selected, how variables are manipulated or measured and how data are analysed; it is chosen within a paradigm."}}, {"q": "A three-year-old girl calls every dog she encounters \"Rover\" (the name of the family's pet dog). This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is b: calling every dog 'Rover' is an example of over-extension. Over-extension is an early error in word meaning in which a child applies a word to a wider set of things than adults do, usually on the basis of shared features such as shape, size, movement or sound. Eve Clark (1973) explained it with her semantic feature hypothesis: the child's first meaning for a word holds only one or two features (four legs, furry), so anything with those features gets the word. Over-extensions are most common in the second year and the early part of the third, when vocabulary is small; children call all men 'daddy', all round things 'ball', or the moon a 'ball'. The opposite error is under-extension, using a word too narrowly, such as using 'dog' only for the family pet and refusing it for other dogs, or 'car' only for the family car. Both fade as children learn more words and the contrasts between them, for instance once they learn 'dog' as a category name and 'Rover' as one dog's name. Option b is right because the girl stretches a name learned for one dog to cover all dogs, using the word more broadly than its adult meaning. Exam trap: over-regularisation, saying 'goed' or 'mouses', is a different error, applying a grammatical rule too widely, and examiners sometimes offer it in place of over-extension. Here what is stretched is a word's meaning, not a grammar rule.", "traps": {"a": "'Over-emphasis' is not a term in language development; it is an everyday word for stressing something too much, so it does not describe a naming error.", "c": "Over-stimulation means receiving more sensory input than a child can comfortably process, leading to fussiness or withdrawal; it is about arousal, not about word meanings.", "d": "Babbling is the pre-word stage, from about 6 to 10 months, when infants repeat consonant-vowel syllables such as 'ba-ba' and 'da-da'; a three-year-old naming dogs is long past it."}}, {"q": "According to the trichromatic theory of colour vision, which of the following are the three types of cones?", "concept": "The answer is b: according to the trichromatic theory, the three types of cones are red, blue and green. Thomas Young (1802) proposed that the eye needs only three kinds of colour receptor, and Hermann von Helmholtz (1852) developed the idea through colour-matching experiments, showing that a person with normal vision can match any colour by mixing three lights of different wavelengths; hence the name Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory. Modern studies of the retina confirmed it: there are three types of cone, each with a different photopigment, most sensitive to short wavelengths (around 420 nanometres, seen as blue), medium wavelengths (around 530, green) or long wavelengths (around 560, towards red). Every colour we see is coded by the ratio of activity across the three cone types. The theory explains colour blindness well: a person missing the long or medium cones (protanopia or deuteranopia) confuses reds and greens. Example: Arjun's phone screen shows a yellow banana using only its red and green pixels lit together; his long and medium cones fire together, and his brain reads the mixture as yellow. Option b is right because the three cone types correspond to red, green and blue. Exam trap: red, yellow and blue (a and d) are the painter's primaries, based on subtractive mixing of pigments, not the eye's receptors. Yellow also appears in Ewald Hering's opponent-process theory (1878), which pairs red with green, blue with yellow and black with white; that pairing describes later neural processing beyond the cones, not the cone types themselves.", "traps": {"a": "Red, yellow and blue are the traditional painter's primaries for mixing pigments (subtractive mixing); the eye has no yellow cone, and this set leaves out green.", "c": "Yellow appears in Ewald Hering's opponent-process theory (1878) as the partner of blue, but there is no yellow cone; this set leaves out the long-wavelength 'red' cone.", "d": "This is the same set as option a, the subtractive pigment primaries, in another order; it leaves out green, which is a true cone type."}}, {"q": "A researcher presents his participant with a series of flashing lights at varying intervals. After each presentation of a light, he asks the participant to fully describe her internal experiences. This method is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: asking a participant to describe her inner experience after each flash of light is the method of introspection. Introspection means 'looking inward': a person observes and reports the contents of her own conscious experience. Wilhelm Wundt, who founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, used a controlled form of it that he called internal perception: trained observers received a precisely timed stimulus, such as a light, a tone or a metronome beat, and immediately reported their sensations and feelings, often alongside reaction-time measures. Edward Titchener (1898), who built structuralism at Cornell, made the method stricter still: observers trained over many trials described only the raw elements of experience (quality, intensity, duration, clearness) and avoided the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead of the experience. The aim was to break consciousness down into its basic elements, much as a chemist analyses compounds. Example: Meera, a trained observer, sees a flash and reports 'a small bright patch, slightly yellowish, fading quickly, with a brief feeling of tension'. She does not say 'a bulb', which would be the stimulus error. Option b is right because the researcher is collecting the participant's own reports of her internal experience under controlled stimulation, which is introspection. Exam trap: functionalism (a) sometimes used introspection too, but it is a school (William James, John Dewey) defined by asking what the mind is for, not a method; behaviourism (c), launched by John Watson (1913), rejected introspection, and psychoanalysis (d) used free association to reach the unconscious.", "traps": {"a": "Functionalism (William James, John Dewey, James Angell) is a school that studied what the mind does to help the organism adapt; it is a theoretical approach, not the name of this reporting method.", "c": "Behaviourism, launched by John Watson (1913), rejected introspection as unscientific and studied only observable behaviour; it would record a response to the light, not inner experience.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) explores unconscious conflict through free association, dreams and transference in therapy, not through structured reports of sensations after timed stimuli."}}, {"q": "The laws of perceptual organisation were proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the laws of perceptual organisation came from all three founders of Gestalt psychology, Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka. Max Wertheimer (1912) launched the school with his study of the phi phenomenon, the apparent motion seen when two lights flash one after the other, with Kohler and Koffka as his observers. The Gestalt message was that the whole is different from the sum of its parts: we perceive organised forms, not a mosaic of separate sensations, a direct challenge to the elements of Wilhelm Wundt and Edward Titchener. Wertheimer set out the grouping laws in a 1923 paper: proximity (near things group together), similarity (alike things group), closure (gaps are filled to make a complete figure), good continuation (smooth lines are followed), common fate (things moving together group) and the master law of Pragnanz, that we see the simplest, most stable organisation the conditions allow. Kurt Koffka spread these ideas to English readers and wrote Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935); Wolfgang Kohler added the field theory of the brain and his insight studies with chimpanzees (The Mentality of Apes, German edition 1917). Example: Kabir looks at a row of six dots and sees three pairs, because the dots in each pair sit closer together; that is the law of proximity. Option d is right because the Gestalt laws are taught as the joint work of the founding trio, with Wertheimer as the leader. Exam trap: examiners offer Wertheimer alone (a), since he wrote the 1923 laws paper; but with all three founders listed, 'all of the above' is the expected answer.", "traps": {"a": "Max Wertheimer founded Gestalt psychology (phi phenomenon, 1912) and wrote the 1923 paper on grouping laws, but the laws are taught as the shared contribution of all three founders, so naming him alone is incomplete here.", "b": "Wolfgang Kohler, a co-founder, is best known for insight learning in chimpanzees (Sultan joining two sticks to reach a banana) and for Gestalt field theory; he helped build the laws, but not alone.", "c": "Kurt Koffka, a co-founder, brought Gestalt psychology to English readers and wrote Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935); he was one of the three, not the sole author of the laws."}}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders is commonly associated with an \"overvalued idea\"?", "concept": "The answer is a: anorexia nervosa is the disorder most commonly associated with an overvalued idea. An overvalued idea, a term introduced by Carl Wernicke (1892), is an understandable, socially acceptable belief that comes to dominate a person's life. It is held with intense emotion and pursued far beyond reason, but, unlike a delusion, it is not held with absolute, unshakeable certainty, and it is not culturally strange: others can see why the person might think it. Unlike an obsession, it is not felt as senseless or resisted; the person agrees with it. In anorexia nervosa, the dread of fatness and the belief that one must be thin (Gerald Russell's 'morbid fear of becoming fat', 1970) is the classic overvalued idea: slimness is valued in the culture, but the patient pursues it to the point of starvation. Other examples are the preoccupation with a minor bodily flaw in body dysmorphic disorder, morbid jealousy and some hypochondriacal beliefs. Example: Riya, 17, weighs far less than is healthy for her height, yet says, 'If I eat a full meal I will become fat, and being fat would make me worthless.' She can discuss other views and is not deluded about the world, but the idea of thinness governs her days. Option a is right because the fear of fatness in anorexia is the standard textbook example of an overvalued idea. Exam trap: overvalued ideas, delusions and obsessions are easily confused; the differences are conviction (less fixed than a delusion) and ownership without resistance (unlike an obsession).", "traps": {"b": "Gender identity disorder (now gender dysphoria in DSM-5) involves a persistent experience of one's gender as different from the one assigned at birth; it is a matter of identity and distress, not the classic overvalued idea.", "c": "Borderline personality disorder involves unstable relationships, self-image and mood, with impulsivity and self-harm; its thinking problems are stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation, not an overvalued idea.", "d": "Histrionic personality disorder involves excessive emotionality and attention seeking, with shallow, impressionistic speech; it is not linked with a single dominating overvalued belief."}}, {"q": "The trait approach to personality asserts that traits are:", "concept": "The answer is c: the trait approach holds that traits are generalised systems that make a person's behaviour consistent. Gordon Allport (1937), the founder of trait psychology, defined a trait as a 'generalized and focalized neuropsychic system', peculiar to the individual, with the capacity to render many stimuli functionally equivalent and to initiate and guide consistent forms of behaviour. Put simply, a trait is a real, lasting disposition inside the person that makes many different situations mean the same thing to her, so she responds to them in similar ways. Traits are continuous dimensions on which everyone can be placed, from low to high, rather than boxes. Allport grouped them as cardinal (one ruling trait that shapes almost everything), central (the 5 to 10 traits that describe someone well) and secondary (narrow preferences that appear only in some settings). Later trait theorists, such as Raymond Cattell, Hans Eysenck and the Big Five researchers, kept the same core idea: stable, general dispositions explain consistency across time and situations. Example: Meera is friendly at college, at a wedding and on a crowded train; her trait of sociability turns all three settings into 'chances to talk', so her behaviour is consistent across them. Option c is right because Allport's definition says exactly that traits are generalised systems guiding consistent behaviour. Exam trap: option b describes the type approach (for example, introvert and extravert as separate kinds of people), which uses discrete categories; trait theory instead uses continuous dimensions. Option d describes body-build theories, a form of type theory.", "traps": {"a": "Adjectives describing behaviour are the raw material of the lexical approach (Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert's 1936 dictionary list); traits are the real dispositions the words point to, not the words themselves.", "b": "Discrete, discontinuous categories of people are the type approach, as in Galen's four temperaments (second century AD) or Carl Jung's introvert and extravert types (1921); trait theory places people on continuous dimensions instead.", "d": "Linking temperament to body build is the constitutional type approach of Ernst Kretschmer (1921) and of William Sheldon's somatotypes (endomorph, mesomorph, ectomorph, 1940s), not the trait approach."}}, {"q": "A developmental psychologist is investigating movement abnormalities as an early symptom of autism. Her research team is collecting data about crawling techniques from videotapes of 9-month-olds. To increase standardisation of data collection, she should:", "concept": "The answer is a: to increase standardisation, she should provide clear definitions of each crawling style with videotaped examples. In observational research, several coders watch behaviour and sort it into categories. The danger is that each coder applies a private idea of what, say, 'commando crawling' or 'bottom shuffling' means, so the same baby is coded differently by different people. The remedy is a coding scheme built on operational definitions, which state each category in terms of exactly what can be seen (for example, 'belly on the floor, arms pull the body forward, legs trail'), backed by a coding manual with model video clips and by training sessions until coders agree. Agreement is then checked as inter-rater (inter-observer) reliability: two coders rate the same tapes independently, and their agreement is calculated as a percentage or as Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects for agreement expected by chance. Periodic re-checks guard against observer drift, the slow shift of coders away from the definitions over time. Example: two research assistants, Arjun and Kabir, disagree on a third of the clips at first; after training with the definition sheet and example videos, they agree on over 90 percent, and the data become standardised. Option a is right because precise definitions with examples make every coder apply the same rules, which is what standardising data collection means. Exam trap: option d (live observation) sounds more natural, but it removes the chance to replay and recheck behaviour and adds reactivity, so it lowers, not raises, standardisation. Options b and c change the sample, not the measurement procedure.", "traps": {"b": "Adding 12-month-olds changes the sample and the age range of the question; it may help generality, but it does nothing to make the coding of crawling more consistent across observers.", "c": "Restricting the sample to children with clinically significant deficits creates selection bias and narrows the range of behaviour; it does not standardise how the data are coded, and it defeats the aim of finding early signs.", "d": "Live, in-person coding cannot be paused or replayed, leaves no permanent record for reliability checks and may make parents and babies behave differently (reactivity), so it reduces standardisation."}}, {"q": "Who discovered that not all mental processes are accompanied by mental imagery?", "concept": "The answer is d: Oswald Kulpe discovered that not all mental processes are accompanied by mental imagery. Oswald Kulpe (1862 to 1915), once Wilhelm Wundt's assistant at Leipzig, moved to Wurzburg in 1894 and built the Wurzburg school there. With students such as Karl Marbe, Narziss Ach and Karl Buhler, he pushed introspection into areas Wundt had ruled out of the laboratory: judgement, thinking and will. In their experiments (about 1901 to 1909) observers solved a task, such as judging which of two weights was heavier or giving an associated word, and then described exactly what had passed through their minds. Many reported that the decisive step of the thought came without any image or sensation, only vague 'conscious attitudes' such as doubt or certainty. This was imageless thought. Ach showed that the instruction, or task (Aufgabe), sets up a determining tendency that steers thinking before the stimulus even appears. Example: when Riya is asked 'Is a mango a fruit?' and answers 'yes', she can find no picture or word in her mind that produced the judgement; the answer simply arrived. The Wurzburg school would call this imageless thought. Option d is right because Kulpe and his Wurzburg group were the ones who reported thought without imagery. Exam trap: Edward Titchener (b) is the near miss because he was part of the famous debate, but on the other side: defending structuralism, he insisted every thought has sensory or imaginal content and argued that the Wurzburg observers had simply missed faint images. The unresolved quarrel helped discredit introspection as a method.", "traps": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt founded the Leipzig laboratory (1879) and used introspection for simple sensations and feelings; he held that higher processes such as thinking could not be studied by laboratory introspection and criticised the Wurzburg experiments.", "b": "Edward Titchener, defending structuralism at Cornell, held that every thought contains sensory or imaginal elements and argued that the Wurzburg observers had overlooked faint images; he opposed imageless thought.", "c": "John Watson (1913) founded behaviourism and rejected introspection altogether, treating thinking as subvocal speech; he used the imageless-thought dispute as evidence that introspection was unreliable."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a finding from Harlow's monkey experiments?", "concept": "The answer is d: none of the above, because all three statements are findings from Harry Harlow's monkey experiments. Harry Harlow (1958), in The Nature of Love, raised infant rhesus monkeys with two surrogate 'mothers': one made of bare wire that held a milk bottle and one covered in soft terry cloth that gave no milk. The infants fed from the wire mother but spent most of their time clinging to the cloth one, so attachment could not be reduced to feeding. This contradicted the drive-reduction view that a baby loves its mother because she satisfies hunger (statement a). When a frightening object, such as a mechanical drumming bear, was introduced, or the infants were placed in a strange open room, they ran to the cloth mother, calmed down and then explored; without her they froze, cried and crouched (Harlow and Robert Zimmermann, 1959). Contact comfort worked as a secure base and a buffer against stress (statement b). Later isolation studies showed that monkeys kept alone for about the first three months, roughly the equivalent of six months of human infancy, recovered fairly well once placed with peers, whereas 6 to 12 months of isolation caused lasting social, sexual and maternal damage (statement c). Option d is right because a, b and c are all reported findings, so none of them is 'not a finding'. Exam trap: students pick c because they remember that early deprivation caused permanent harm. Key note: the reversibility in c holds only for short isolation (about 90 days); longer isolates were later only partly rehabilitated by Stephen Suomi and Harlow (1972) using younger 'therapist' monkeys. Textbooks summarise the short-isolation result as c states, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is one of Harlow's central findings: infants fed from the wire mother but clung to the cloth one, showing that attachment is not reducible to nursing. Being a finding, it cannot be the answer to 'NOT a finding'.", "b": "This is also a reported finding: in fear and open-field tests the cloth mother acted as a secure base that calmed the infants and let them explore, so it is not the exception.", "c": "Harlow's isolation studies did find that about three months of isolation, roughly six months of human infancy, could be largely overcome once the monkey joined peers, while longer isolation did lasting harm. It is a finding, though limited to short deprivation."}}, {"q": "A mental disorder is conceptualised as a ______ significant behavioural or psychological syndrome.", "concept": "The answer is c: a mental disorder is conceptualised as a clinically significant behavioural or psychological syndrome. This wording comes from the American Psychiatric Association's Diagnostic and Statistical Manual. DSM-IV (1994) defined a mental disorder as 'a clinically significant behavioral or psychological syndrome or pattern' associated with present distress, disability, or a significantly increased risk of suffering death, pain, disability or an important loss of freedom. DSM-5 (2013) updated it to a syndrome characterised by clinically significant disturbance in an individual's cognition, emotion regulation or behaviour that reflects a dysfunction in the psychological, biological or developmental processes underlying mental functioning, usually associated with significant distress or disability in social, occupational or other important activities. Two exclusions follow: an expectable or culturally approved response to a common stressor or loss, such as grief after a death, is not a disorder; and socially deviant behaviour (political, religious or sexual) or conflict with society is not a disorder unless it results from a dysfunction in the individual. 'Clinically significant' means severe enough to cause real distress or impairment, the threshold that separates a disorder from ordinary sadness, worry or eccentricity. Example: Kabir feels low for two days after failing a test and then recovers; that is not clinically significant. If his low mood lasted weeks and he stopped attending college, it would be. Option c is right because 'clinically significant' is the phrase the DSM uses. Exam trap: 'psychologically' (b) feels natural because the syndrome is psychological, but the definition already says 'behavioural or psychological syndrome'; the qualifier being tested is 'clinically'.", "traps": {"a": "'Physically significant' is not part of any DSM or ICD definition; mental disorders are defined by psychological and behavioural disturbance, while physical illness is coded as a medical condition.", "b": "The syndrome is already described as 'behavioural or psychological', so 'psychologically significant' would be redundant; the DSM qualifier is 'clinically significant', which requires distress or impairment.", "d": "'Systematically significant' is not a diagnostic term; 'systematic' refers to an orderly method (as in systematic desensitisation), not to the severity threshold for a disorder."}}, {"q": "A procedure that establishes a sequence of responses, which lead to a reward following the final response, by working backwards from the final response is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: building a sequence of responses by working backwards from the final, rewarded response is called chaining, here backward chaining. A behaviour chain is a series of responses in which each step produces the cue (discriminative stimulus) for the next, and only the last step earns the main reward. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, described how such chains hold together: each stimulus in the chain is both a conditioned reinforcer for the response before it and a signal for the response after it. In backward chaining the trainer first teaches the final link, which is followed at once by the reward; then adds the second-to-last link, whose completion leads into the already learned final step; and so on back to the first link. Because the learner always finishes with success and reward, the method suits young children and people with intellectual disability. Forward chaining starts from the first step instead, and total task presentation practises all the steps every time. Example: to teach Aarav, a child with autism, to tie his shoelaces, the therapist does every step except the final pull, lets Aarav complete it and praises him. Once that is easy, Aarav does the last two steps, then three, until he ties the whole lace himself. Option c is right because chaining links responses into a sequence, and the stem describes its backward form. Exam trap: shaping (a) also builds new behaviour step by step, but it reinforces successive approximations to a single target response, not a sequence of different responses linked together.", "traps": {"a": "Shaping (B. F. Skinner) reinforces successive approximations to one new target response, for example rewarding a rat for facing the lever, then touching it, then pressing it; it refines a single behaviour rather than linking a sequence backwards.", "b": "Stimulus control is when a response occurs reliably in the presence of a particular discriminative stimulus and not in its absence (stopping at a red light); it concerns when a behaviour happens, not how a sequence is built.", "d": "Stimulus generalisation is responding to stimuli similar to the trained one, as when Little Albert's conditioned fear of a white rat spread to other furry objects (John Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920); it is the spread of a response, not a training procedure."}}, {"q": "What is the use of factorial analysis?", "concept": "The answer is c: factor analysis is used to understand the relationship between variables. Factor analysis is a statistical method that starts from the correlations among many measured variables and asks whether a smaller number of hidden dimensions, called factors, can account for them. Variables that correlate highly with one another are grouped onto the same factor, and each variable's correlation with a factor is its factor loading. Charles Spearman (1904) invented the method when he noticed that children's marks in quite different school subjects all correlated positively; he explained the pattern with one general factor, g. Louis Thurstone (1931 to 1947) developed multiple factor analysis and found several primary mental abilities instead of one g, and Raymond Cattell (1949) used it to reduce thousands of trait words to the 16 source traits of the 16PF. Exploratory factor analysis looks for the structure; confirmatory factor analysis tests whether a structure proposed in advance fits the data. Suppose Meera gives 200 students ten tests: vocabulary, reading, analogies, arithmetic, number series and so on. If the verbal tests correlate strongly with each other and the number tests with each other, factor analysis will reveal a verbal factor and a numerical factor. Option c is right because factor analysis works on relationships (correlations) among variables, not on differences between group means. Exam trap: the word \"factorial\" pulls students towards d, which describes a factorial ANOVA, a design that tests mean differences across two or more independent variables (factors) and their interaction, built on Ronald Fisher's (1925) analysis of variance. Key note: factor analysis studies the pattern of relationships among many variables at once, so \"two variables\" is narrower than the method really is; c is still the only option about relationships, which is what factor analysis analyses, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Setting a hypothesis is a planning step in which a testable prediction is stated before data are collected; factor analysis is a data-analysis technique, and in its exploratory form it is often run with no prior hypothesis at all.", "b": "Testing the difference between two groups or two conditions is the job of a t-test (William Gosset, writing as 'Student', 1908) or a non-parametric equivalent; factor analysis does not compare means.", "d": "Differences across several variables or groups are tested by analysis of variance; a factorial ANOVA crosses two or more independent variables, which is why the word 'factorial' misleads, but it compares means rather than grouping correlated variables."}}, {"q": "Piaget's stage for infancy is:", "concept": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, infancy is the stage of sensorimotor thought. Jean Piaget (1936), a Swiss psychologist who observed his own three children in close detail and described their first two years in The Origins of Intelligence in Children, proposed four stages of cognitive development, each a qualitatively different way of thinking: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (about 11 onwards). In the sensorimotor stage the infant knows the world through the senses and through actions such as looking, sucking, grasping and moving. Thinking happens in action, not yet in words or mental symbols. Piaget divided the stage into six substages, from simple reflexes to the first mental representations near age 2. Its great achievement is object permanence, the understanding that objects go on existing when out of sight, which begins at about 8 to 12 months and is complete by about 18 to 24 months. When baby Kabir, at 10 months, pulls a cloth off a hidden rattle, he shows that the rattle still exists for him even unseen. Option c is right because sensorimotor thought is the stage that covers infancy, from birth to about 2 years. Exam trap: preoperational thought begins with language and pretend play at about 2, in the toddler and preschool years, so it is the near miss for \"infancy\".", "traps": {"a": "Formal operations is Piaget's fourth stage, from about 11 or 12 years: abstract, hypothetical and systematic reasoning typical of adolescence, not infancy.", "b": "Preoperational thought is the second stage, about 2 to 7 years: symbols, language and pretend play appear, but thinking is egocentric and the child fails conservation tasks.", "d": "Concrete operations is the third stage, about 7 to 11 years: logical reasoning about real objects, with conservation, seriation and classification; it belongs to the school years."}}, {"q": "What is a dependent variable in research?", "concept": "The answer is d: the dependent variable is the variable that is measured and observed. In an experiment the researcher changes one thing and watches what happens to another. The factor deliberately changed is the independent variable (IV); the outcome recorded to see whether it changed is the dependent variable (DV), so called because its value is expected to depend on the IV. Everything else that might affect the DV is held constant; these are controlled variables. Any outside factor that might influence the DV is an extraneous variable, and one that varies along with the IV, so that its effect cannot be separated from the IV's, is a confounding variable, the main threat to internal validity. Take Arjun, who tests whether caffeine improves reaction time. He gives one group coffee and another decaffeinated coffee (caffeine is the IV, with two levels), keeps the time of day and the task identical (controlled variables), and records reaction time in milliseconds (the DV). If the caffeine group responds faster, the change in the DV is put down to the IV. A handy check is the phrase \"the effect of the IV on the DV\": the effect of caffeine on reaction time. In non-experimental research the same idea appears as predictor and outcome (criterion) variables. Option d is right because the DV is the outcome the researcher measures, not the one manipulated. Exam trap: examiners swap the two definitions, so option a, \"manipulated by the researcher\", is the independent variable, the most common reversal on this topic.", "traps": {"a": "A variable manipulated by the researcher is the independent variable, the presumed cause; this is the classic reversal of the dependent variable's definition.", "b": "A variable kept constant throughout the study is a controlled (constant) variable, held steady so that it cannot confound the effect of the independent variable on the dependent variable.", "c": "A variable outside the research question that could still affect the outcome is an extraneous variable, which the researcher tries to control; it is never the outcome being measured."}}, {"q": "People with ______ avoid specific public places or situations because they anticipate having anxiety or a panic attack and being unable to escape or get help.", "concept": "The answer is b: this describes agoraphobia. The word comes from the Greek agora, the marketplace, so it literally means fear of the marketplace or of public places. In DSM-5 (2013), agoraphobia is marked fear or anxiety about at least two of five kinds of situation: using public transport, being in open spaces such as car parks or bridges, being in enclosed places such as shops or cinemas, standing in a queue or a crowd, and being outside the home alone. The core of the fear is not the place itself but the thought that escape might be difficult, or help unavailable, if panic-like or embarrassing symptoms struck. The situations are avoided, endured with intense dread or faced only with a companion, and the problem typically lasts 6 months or more. Since DSM-5 it is diagnosed separately from panic disorder, though the two often occur together and a person can receive both. Alan Goldstein and Dianne Chambless (1978) described the mechanism as \"fear of fear\": after a few panic attacks, people come to dread the bodily sensations themselves and the places where attacks happened. Picture Riya, who had a panic attack on a crowded Delhi Metro train: she now travels only by autorickshaw, avoids malls and will not go to the market without her sister. Option b is right because the stem describes avoiding public places for fear of being unable to escape or get help during panic. Exam trap: social phobia also leads to avoidance of public situations, but there the feared outcome is being judged or embarrassed by others, not being trapped without help.", "traps": {"a": "Social phobia (social anxiety disorder) is fear of situations in which one may be watched and negatively evaluated, such as speaking or eating in public; the feared outcome is humiliation, not being unable to escape during panic.", "c": "'Panic xenophobia' is not a diagnosis; xenophobia means fear or dislike of strangers or foreigners, a social attitude rather than an anxiety disorder, attached to 'panic' to sound plausible.", "d": "Acrophobia is a specific phobia of heights; the fear is tied to one kind of stimulus, not to a range of public places from which escape might be hard."}}, {"q": "Conformity was first systematically studied by:", "concept": "The answer is b: conformity was first studied systematically by Solomon Asch. Conformity means changing one's behaviour or judgement to match a group, in response to real or imagined group pressure. In his line-judgement studies, Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) seated one real participant among about seven confederates who had been told what to say. On each trial everyone saw a standard line and three comparison lines and announced, in turn, which comparison matched the standard. The answer was obvious: tested alone, people erred less than 1 percent of the time. But on 12 of the 18 trials (the critical ones) the confederates unanimously gave the same wrong answer. The real participant, who answered last or next to last, conformed on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about 75 percent conformed at least once, though a quarter never did. Conformity rose with group size up to about three confederates and fell sharply when one ally broke the unanimity. Interviews showed most yielders knew the group was wrong but went along to avoid ridicule, which is normative influence, distinguished from informational influence by Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard (1955). If Kabir hears six classmates confidently say line C, he may say C too, even though he can see that line B matches. Option b is right because Asch's experiments are the classic systematic study of conformity to a clear majority. Exam trap: Albert Ellis, Carl Rogers and Alfred Adler are clinicians and personality theorists, so the social psychologist is the only fit. Key note: Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic effect study (1935) on norm formation came earlier and is sometimes called the first conformity experiment, but Sherif is not an option, and Asch's studies are the classic systematic study of conformity to a clear majority, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Albert Ellis founded rational emotive behaviour therapy (from 1955), a clinical method of disputing irrational beliefs; he did no experimental research on group conformity.", "c": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy and a self theory of personality built on the person's own experience; he did not study majority pressure on judgement.", "d": "Alfred Adler founded individual psychology (inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, social interest, birth order); social interest is a personality concept, not the experimental study of conformity."}}, {"q": "Who is the father of neuropsychology?", "concept": "The answer is a: among these options, Donald Hebb is the one called the father of neuropsychology. Neuropsychology studies how the brain produces behaviour and mental processes, largely by examining what changes when particular parts of the brain are damaged and by measuring those changes with tests. Donald Hebb (1949), a Canadian psychologist at McGill University, wrote The Organization of Behavior, the first full theory of how thought and memory could arise from the activity of networks of neurons. He proposed the cell assembly, a group of neurons that become linked because they fire together during an experience, and the Hebbian synapse: when one neuron repeatedly helps to fire another, the connection between them strengthens (later summed up as \"cells that fire together wire together\"). Long-term potentiation, found by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973), later supported the idea. Before that, Hebb had tested patients after brain surgery with Wilder Penfield in Montreal and found that removing large parts of the frontal lobes left IQ scores surprisingly intact, and at McGill he trained a generation of neuropsychologists, including Brenda Milner. When Meera learns a new raga by practising daily, Hebb's rule says the neurons involved fire together and so wire together. Option a is right because Hebb's 1949 book laid the foundation of modern neuropsychology, and he is the only founder figure among the options. Exam trap: Shepherd Ivory Franz sounds plausible because he did early brain-lesion research, but he was a pioneer, not the founder. Key note: Alexander Luria (Higher Cortical Functions in Man, 1962) is most often called the father of neuropsychology, and Hebb is also widely given the title for founding modern neuropsychology and neural network theory; Luria is not an option, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "'Dr. Raphael' is not a recognised figure in the history of neuropsychology; the name is a filler distractor.", "c": "Shepherd Ivory Franz (1874 to 1933) was an early American researcher on brain lesions and relearning who worked with Karl Lashley on how animals relearn habits after cortical damage; he was a pioneer of the field, not the figure called its father.", "d": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test; his contribution was mental testing, not the study of brain and behaviour."}}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is:", "concept": "The answer is a: in a negatively skewed distribution the mean lies to the left of the median. Skewness is the lack of symmetry in a distribution. In a negative (left) skew most scores bunch at the high end and a long tail stretches toward the low scores on the left. The three measures of central tendency then separate. The mode stays at the peak, where scores are most frequent. The median, the middle score by rank, moves a little toward the tail. The mean, which uses the value of every score, is pulled furthest toward the extreme low scores. So from left to right the order is mean, median, mode: mean less than median less than mode. Positive (right) skew is the mirror image, with mode less than median less than mean. Karl Pearson (1895) built his coefficients of skewness on this gap; for example, 3 times (mean minus median) divided by the standard deviation, which comes out negative in a left skew. Suppose most students in Arjun's class score 80 to 95 on an easy test but three score below 20: those few low scores drag the mean below the median. Option a is right because the long low tail pulls the mean below, that is to the left of, the median. Exam trap: students link \"negative\" with the wrong side or forget which measure moves most; remember that the mean always chases the tail.", "traps": {"b": "Right of the median is where the mean sits in a positively skewed distribution, in which the long tail runs toward high scores and pulls the mean upward.", "c": "The mean equals the median only in a symmetrical distribution, such as the normal curve, where mean, median and mode coincide.", "d": "In a negative skew the mode is the largest of the three values, at the peak on the right, so the mean cannot equal it; the three coincide only when the distribution is symmetrical."}}, {"q": "State the correct sequence of Tuckman's stages of group development:", "concept": "The answer is c: Bruce Tuckman's sequence is forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning. Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed about 50 studies of therapy, training and laboratory groups and proposed that small groups pass through predictable stages. In forming, members are polite, uncertain and dependent on the leader as they learn about the task and one another. In storming, conflict breaks out over roles, goals and leadership, and members resist the demands of the group. In norming, the group settles these conflicts, builds cohesion and agrees on rules and roles. In performing, energy goes into the task itself, with flexible roles and mutual support. Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977) added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the work ends and the group disbands, sometimes with a sense of loss (some writers call it mourning). Think of Meera's college project team: awkward introductions, then an argument over who leads, then agreed deadlines and duties, then smooth work, and finally a farewell after submission. Option c is right because it is the only option in Tuckman's order, with storming before norming. Exam trap: option d swaps storming and norming, but conflict comes before the group agrees its norms, which is why the rhyme runs forming, storming, norming, performing.", "traps": {"a": "This puts storming and norming before forming, but a group cannot quarrel or set norms before its members have come together and oriented themselves to the task.", "b": "This starts with norming, as if rules existed before members met and before any conflict; in Tuckman's model norms are built only after the storming stage.", "d": "This swaps storming and norming; in Tuckman's (1965) model conflict over roles and leadership comes first and is resolved by agreeing norms."}}, {"q": "The 'cocktail party effect' describes a phenomenon in which:", "concept": "The answer is c: the cocktail party effect is the ability to attend selectively to one conversation out of the many taking place. Colin Cherry (1953), a British engineer studying speech, described the problem: at a noisy party we follow one speaker and shut out the rest. He tested it with dichotic listening, in which a different message is played to each ear through headphones, and shadowing, in which the listener repeats one message aloud as it is heard. People shadowed the attended ear well but could report almost nothing about the content of the unattended ear; they did not notice when it switched from English to German, though they did notice a change from a male to a female voice, a physical feature. Donald Broadbent (1958) explained this with his filter model: messages are selected early, on physical features, before their meaning is processed. Neville Moray (1959) then found that about a third of listeners heard their own name in the unattended ear, so some meaning leaks through, and Anne Treisman (1964) proposed that the filter only attenuates (turns down) unattended messages rather than blocking them. Imagine Kabir at a crowded wedding, chatting with a cousin while the band plays: he follows one voice, yet turns at once when someone across the room says \"Kabir\". Option c is right because the effect names selective attention to one conversation among many. Exam trap: hearing your own name from an unattended conversation is Moray's breakthrough finding within the cocktail party phenomenon, not its definition, and option b's clause about not looking at the caller is irrelevant.", "traps": {"a": "Remembering details of attended events reflects the general link between attention and memory (attended information is encoded, unattended information largely lost); it is not the cocktail party effect.", "b": "Hearing your own name in an unattended conversation is Neville Moray's (1959) finding that meaningful material can break through the attentional filter; it is a related result, and the clause about not looking at the caller belongs to no definition.", "d": "Hearing more easily in a quiet room is simply a better signal-to-noise ratio (less masking), an acoustic fact rather than selective attention among competing conversations."}}, {"q": "Clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was:", "concept": "The answer is a: clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was the creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits. When the United States entered the war in 1917, the army had to sort well over a million men quickly into jobs, officer training or discharge. Robert Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, chaired a committee, with Lewis Terman, Henry Goddard and others, that produced two group intelligence tests. The Army Alpha was a written test for literate recruits who could read English, with items on arithmetic, analogies, number series and general information; its multiple-choice format came from Arthur Otis, Terman's student. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for men who were illiterate or did not speak English, using mazes, picture completion and cube counting, with instructions given by demonstration. About 1.75 million recruits were tested. At the same time Robert Woodworth (1917) wrote the Personal Data Sheet to screen soldiers likely to break down under fire, the first personality inventory. The war proved that tests could be given to large groups at once, group testing boomed in schools and industry in the 1920s, and assessment remained the clinical psychologist's main job for decades. A recruit like Arjun, who had never been to school, would have taken the pictorial Beta rather than the Alpha. Option a is right because the army's group intelligence tests were psychology's signature contribution to the First World War. Exam trap: shell shock sounds like the obvious clinical task, but in World War I it was treated mainly by army doctors and psychiatrists; psychologists became therapists on a large scale only after World War II, when the Veterans Administration began training them in 1946.", "traps": {"b": "Treating shell shock (war neurosis) in World War I fell mainly to army physicians and psychiatrists; clinical psychology's large move into therapy for veterans came after World War II through the Veterans Administration.", "c": "Interrogation techniques were not a contribution of clinical psychology in World War I; psychological work on interrogation belongs to later conflicts and the Cold War era and remains ethically disputed.", "d": "Selecting and training pilots became a major task of aviation psychology in World War II, not clinical psychology's main contribution to World War I."}}, {"q": "As described in textbooks of abnormal psychology, this is a syndrome of schizophrenia occurring in the presence of intellectual disability. It differs from schizophrenia only in that there is often a poverty of ideation, and delusions are not usually very well systematised.", "concept": "The answer is b: this is Pfropf schizophrenia, schizophrenia grafted onto intellectual disability. Pfropf is German for a graft, as when a gardener grafts a shoot onto an existing plant. German psychiatry in the tradition of Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926), who described dementia praecox in the 1890s, used Pfropfschizophrenie for a psychosis grafted onto a pre-existing mental handicap (then called oligophrenia or mental retardation). The illness looks much like ordinary schizophrenia, with hallucinations, delusions, odd behaviour and a decline from the person's previous level of functioning, but the limited intellect colours it: ideas are fewer and simpler (poverty of ideation), and delusions are poorly systematised, often fragmentary or naive, because building an elaborate delusional system needs cognitive resources. Catatonic features and repetitive behaviour are said to be common. Diagnosis is hard, because a person with intellectual disability may lack the language to describe hallucinations, and stereotyped behaviour can be mistaken for psychosis. Today it would be coded as schizophrenia co-occurring with intellectual disability. Suppose Arjun, 24, with moderate intellectual disability, begins talking to unseen people and insists, without any further detail, that a neighbour is \"doing magic\" on him: this is the grafted picture. Option b is right because Pfropf schizophrenia is the named syndrome of schizophrenia in the presence of intellectual disability. Exam trap: the other three are also named variants of schizophrenia, each defined by something else (late onset, neurotic-looking symptoms or a dreamlike state), so learn each by its defining feature.", "traps": {"a": "Late paraphrenia (Martin Roth, 1955) is a paranoid psychosis beginning in old age, usually after 60, often in socially isolated or hard-of-hearing women, with well-formed persecutory delusions and a preserved personality; it is defined by late onset, not intellectual disability.", "c": "Pseudoneurotic schizophrenia (Paul Hoch and Philip Polatin, 1949) is schizophrenia hidden behind florid neurotic symptoms such as pan-anxiety, pan-neurosis and chaotic sexuality; it is defined by its neurotic surface, not low intelligence.", "d": "Oneiroid schizophrenia (Wilhelm Mayer-Gross, 1924) is an acute, dream-like state with clouding of consciousness and vivid, scene-like hallucinations; it is defined by the dreamy disorientation, not by intellectual disability."}}, {"q": "Mr X, a 35-year-old man presents with suicidal ideation, depressed mood, loss of interest and enjoyment, and increased fatigue for the past 4 weeks. The symptoms had an insidious onset and a deteriorating course. There was a history of chronic feelings of emptiness, multiple episodes of non-suicidal self-harm, and fear of abandonment in relationships. He is the first-born child and oldest of 3 siblings. His father struggled with outbursts of aggression and had completed suicide when he was 14 years old, and his mother was pregnant with his youngest brother (now, 21). He struggles with alcohol overconsumption occasionally and is dependent on alcohol use. He confided that he has a tendency to eat when emotional. He got involved romantically and became sexually active at 19 years old and has had intense and volatile relationships. The current depressive episode was precipitated by a break up. What can be his diagnosis?", "concept": "The answer is c: the picture fits borderline personality disorder. A personality disorder is an enduring, pervasive and inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that begins by adolescence or early adulthood and causes distress or impairment. In DSM-5 (2013), borderline personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotion, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and shown by at least 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substance use, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and transient, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Mr X shows at least five: fear of abandonment, intense and volatile relationships, impulsivity in two areas (alcohol and emotional eating), repeated self-harm and chronic emptiness. The pattern runs from early adulthood, and the current episode followed a break-up, the kind of perceived rejection that typically destabilises people with this disorder. His father's aggression and suicide fit the known risk factors of early loss and family instability. Marsha Linehan (1993) developed dialectical behaviour therapy for this disorder. Option c is right because the long-standing pattern across relationships, mood and behaviour meets the borderline criteria. Exam trap: the stem opens with depressive symptoms to pull you towards major depressive disorder, but the question asks for the diagnosis that explains the whole history. Key note: the current 4-week depressive episode would also be recorded, but only four depressive symptoms are described, and a DSM-5 major depressive episode needs five; the long-standing pattern fits borderline personality disorder, the diagnosis the case is built to test, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Complex PTSD (ICD-11) requires exposure to prolonged or repeated trauma, the core PTSD symptoms (re-experiencing, avoidance, a sense of current threat) and disturbances in self-organisation; no such trauma symptoms are described here.", "b": "Alcohol dependence is present as one feature, but it does not explain the emptiness, self-harm, abandonment fear and unstable relationships; it is a co-occurring problem, not the diagnosis that accounts for the whole picture.", "d": "Major depressive disorder covers only the current 4-week episode, and with four symptoms described it falls short of the five DSM-5 requires; it ignores the lifelong pattern the case is built around."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a practical consideration in evaluating a psychological test? 1. If reading is required by the examinee, does his or her ability match the level required by the test? 2. How appropriate is the length of the test? 3. Will the test produce accurate measurements in the context and for the purpose for which you would like to use it? 4. Have specialised subgroup norms been established?", "concept": "The answer is c: items 3 and 4 are not practical considerations. Gary Groth-Marnat, in the Handbook of Psychological Assessment (first edition 1984), sets out the questions a clinician should ask when choosing a test under five headings: theoretical orientation, practical considerations, standardisation, reliability and validity. Practical considerations ask whether the test suits the examinee and the setting in everyday terms: whether the reading level matches the examinee's ability, whether the length suits the person's attention and the time available, and how much training the examiner needs to give and score it. Standardisation asks whether the norm group resembles the person being tested, whether adequate specialised subgroup norms exist (by age, sex, education or ethnicity, for example), and whether the instructions for administration are clear. Validity asks whether the test measures what it claims and will produce accurate measurements in the context and for the purpose intended. So item 1 (reading level) and item 2 (length) are practical; item 3 (accurate measurement for your purpose) is a validity question; and item 4 (subgroup norms) is a standardisation question. Suppose Meera, a clinical psychologist, must assess a farmer with five years of schooling: she checks that the inventory's reading level and length suit him (practical), that rural Indian norms exist (standardisation) and that the scores predict what she needs to know (validity). Option c is right because both 3 and 4 belong to other headings, so neither is a practical consideration. Exam trap: choosing only one of them (a or b) is a half answer, since both items fall outside the practical heading.", "traps": {"a": "Item 4 alone is not a practical consideration (it belongs to standardisation), but item 3 also falls outside the practical heading, so choosing 4 alone misses half the answer.", "b": "Item 3 alone is a validity question, but item 4, specialised subgroup norms, is also not practical (it belongs to standardisation), so 3 alone is incomplete.", "d": "Item 2, whether the length of the test is appropriate, is a genuine practical consideration in Groth-Marnat's scheme, alongside reading level and examiner training."}}, {"q": "In retrograde amnesia, which of the following kinds of problem is seen?", "concept": "The answer is a: retrograde amnesia is a problem with recalling material learned before the onset of the amnesia. Amnesia is a loss of memory, usually after brain injury, disease or psychological trauma, and it is described by its direction in time from the moment of onset. Retrograde amnesia (retro, backwards) is the loss of memories formed before the injury. It often follows a temporal gradient: the memories closest to the injury are lost first and most completely, while older ones survive, a pattern called Ribot's law after Theodule Ribot (1881). A footballer concussed in a tackle may remember the morning but not the minutes before the collision, because those memories had not yet been consolidated. Anterograde amnesia (antero, forwards) is the inability to form new lasting memories after the onset. The classic case is patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), whose medial temporal lobes, including both hippocampi, were removed in 1953 to control his epilepsy: William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported that he could no longer form new declarative memories, yet he could still learn motor skills such as mirror drawing. When Riya wakes after a road accident unable to remember the drive but recalls her childhood clearly, she shows retrograde amnesia with Ribot's gradient. Option a is right because retrograde amnesia means losing previously learned material. Exam trap: poor encoding of new verbal material also sounds like memory loss, but failing to lay down new memories is anterograde amnesia, the opposite direction in time.", "traps": {"b": "A specific inability to recognise familiar faces is prosopagnosia, a visual agnosia usually from damage to the fusiform gyrus; it is a recognition disorder, not a loss of past memories.", "c": "Difficulty finding routes is topographical disorientation, linked to damage in right parietal, parahippocampal or retrosplenial regions; it is a problem of spatial navigation, not a general loss of earlier memories.", "d": "Poor encoding of new verbal material is anterograde, not retrograde, amnesia, and when it is confined to words it points to damage in the left medial temporal lobe."}}, {"q": "The term 'linguistic relativity' refers to the idea that:", "concept": "The answer is b: linguistic relativity is the idea that language influences how people think. It comes from the anthropologist Edward Sapir (1929) and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf, whose essays were collected in Language, Thought and Reality (1956); together the claim is called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Whorf argued, partly from his study of the Hopi language, that the categories built into a language shape the way its speakers carve up experience. The hypothesis has two versions. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language determines thought, so speakers of different languages think in ways that cannot be translated. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says language influences habitual thought, perception and memory without fixing them. Research has largely rejected the strong form: Eleanor Rosch (1972), then publishing as Eleanor Heider, found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour terms, still remembered focal colours better than non-focal ones, just as English speakers did. Weaker effects are well supported; for example, Russian has separate basic words for light blue and dark blue, and its speakers tell those shades apart faster. Imagine Arjun, a Hindi speaker, whose words chacha, tau, mama and fufa distinguish uncles that English lumps together: he may notice and remember which side of the family a relative belongs to more readily than an English speaker. Option b is right because relativity means that thought is influenced by the language one speaks. Exam trap: do not confuse the weak version (relativity, influence) with the strong version (determinism, control), and note that the idea says nothing about some languages being better than others.", "traps": {"a": "That some languages are better than others is a value judgement linguists reject; every natural language is equally rule-governed and able to express what its speakers need.", "c": "Finding it easier to talk to family than to friends is a matter of interpersonal closeness and comfort, not a claim about how language shapes thought.", "d": "That there are different languages around the world is a plain fact of linguistic diversity; linguistic relativity is the further claim that these differences shape thinking."}}, {"q": "Who introduced the term \"intelligence quotient\" (IQ)?", "concept": "The answer is d: William Stern introduced the term intelligence quotient. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence test in 1905, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's performance as a mental age (MA), the age level of the hardest items the child could pass. Mental age on its own is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. William Stern (1912), a German psychologist, solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), a ratio he called the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove decimals and popularised the abbreviation IQ: IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. So if Kabir, aged 8, has a mental age of 10, his ratio IQ is (10 / 8) x 100 = 125. Because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, the ratio IQ breaks down for adults, and David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Option d is right because Stern coined the quotient in 1912. Exam trap: Terman popularised IQ and added the 100, which tempts students to credit him; he is absent here, and the listed names (Jensen, Thorndike, Cattell) are famous for other work on intelligence.", "traps": {"a": "Arthur R. Jensen (1969) is known for the debate on the heritability of IQ and for Level I (associative) and Level II (conceptual) abilities, not for coining the intelligence quotient.", "b": "Edward L. Thorndike proposed a multifactor theory of intelligence (abstract, mechanical and social intelligence) and the law of effect; he did not coin the intelligence quotient.", "c": "Raymond B. Cattell divided intelligence into fluid and crystallised abilities (1963) and built the 16PF; the quotient came from Stern half a century earlier."}}, {"q": "According to the ______ model of motivation, a physiological need such as thirst stimulates an appropriate response.", "concept": "The answer is a: in the drive-reduction model of motivation, a physiological need such as thirst stimulates a response that reduces it. Clark Hull (1943), in Principles of Behavior, built the most formal version. A need is a biological deficit, such as a lack of water or food. The need creates a drive, an aroused, unpleasant state of tension that energises the organism and pushes it to act. Behaviour that reduces the drive (drinking, eating) is reinforced, so the response becomes more likely the next time the need arises; Hull saw drive reduction as the basis of reinforcement and learning. The idea rests on homeostasis, the body's tendency to keep its internal conditions steady, which Walter Cannon popularised in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Primary drives come from biological needs; secondary (acquired) drives, such as a desire for money, are learned through association with primary ones. When Meera finishes a long badminton match, her body is short of water, she feels thirst (the drive), and drinking a glass of nimbu pani reduces it, so reaching for a drink after games becomes a habit. The theory struggles with behaviour that raises arousal, such as curiosity, thrill-seeking or exploring when no need is pressing, which led Donald Hebb (1955) and others to propose optimal arousal theories. Option a is right because the stem describes a need producing a drive that prompts a response to reduce it. Exam trap: Yerkes-Dodson also concerns arousal, but it links arousal to performance, not need to response.", "traps": {"b": "The Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908) says performance rises with arousal to an optimum and then falls, with a lower optimum for difficult tasks; it explains how well we perform, not how a need triggers a response.", "c": "The hierarchy of needs (Abraham Maslow, 1943) ranks needs from physiological up to self-actualisation; physiological needs sit at its base, but the model is about which needs dominate when, not the need, drive and response mechanism.", "d": "The James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885) is a theory of emotion, in which bodily arousal comes first and the feeling follows from perceiving it; it is not a model of motivation."}}, {"q": "The group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone proposed the group factor theory of intelligence. Using multiple factor analysis, a method he developed to extract several independent factors from a set of correlations, Louis Thurstone (1938) gave 56 tests to University of Chicago students and published Primary Mental Abilities. He found not one general factor but seven group factors, each shared by a cluster of tests but not by all: verbal comprehension (V), word fluency (W), number (N), space (S), associative memory (M), perceptual speed (P) and reasoning (R). He called them primary mental abilities and built tests to measure each, so that every person gets a profile rather than a single IQ. They are \"group\" factors because they sit between Charles Spearman's (1904) one general factor, common to every test, and the specific factors unique to one test. Later Thurstone found that his primary abilities were themselves correlated, suggesting a second-order general factor, so the two views moved closer. Cyril Burt and Philip Vernon (1950) then placed group factors in a hierarchy under g, with major group factors (verbal-educational and practical-mechanical) and minor ones below them. If Riya scores high on space and number but only average on verbal comprehension and word fluency, Thurstone's profile shows that pattern, which one IQ number would hide. Option d is right because group factor theory is Thurstone's primary mental abilities model. Exam trap: Spearman is the usual distractor, but his is the two-factor theory (g plus s), the very view Thurstone opposed.", "traps": {"a": "William Brown wrote The Essentials of Mental Measurement (1911), later revised with Godfrey Thomson; he worked on mental measurement and correlation, not on the group factor theory.", "b": "Edward Thorndike proposed a multifactor theory, in which intelligence is the sum of many specific connections and abilities (abstract, mechanical, social), with no general factor and no small set of group factors.", "c": "Charles Spearman (1904, 1927) proposed the two-factor theory: one general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test, the very view Thurstone challenged."}}, {"q": "______ is concerned with the skills and underlying abilities which we have acquired during life.", "concept": "The answer is a: procedural memory is concerned with the skills and underlying abilities we acquire during life. It is memory for how to do things: riding a bicycle, typing, tying a knot, playing the tabla. It is learned gradually through practice, shown in performance rather than recalled in words, and works largely outside awareness, so it is classed as nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) showed that amnesic patients could learn a mirror-reading skill they could not remember practising, and Squire's later taxonomy of long-term memory divides it into declarative memory, knowing that, which can be consciously stated, and nondeclarative memory, which includes procedural skills, priming and classical conditioning. Declarative memory itself splits, following Endel Tulving (1972), into episodic memory for personal events with a time and place, and semantic memory for general facts and word meanings. Procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (the striatum) and the cerebellum, while declarative memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobes, which is why patient H.M. got better at mirror drawing day after day without remembering the task. When Kabir sits at the harmonium after years away, his fingers find the scales even though he could not explain the finger pattern in words. Option a is right because skills and abilities built up through practice are the content of procedural memory. Exam trap: declarative and semantic memory are also long-term stores, but they hold knowing that (facts and events), not knowing how.", "traps": {"b": "Propositional memory is knowledge stored as propositions, statements that can be true or false ('Delhi is the capital of India'); that is declarative knowing that, not skills.", "c": "Declarative (explicit) memory is knowing that: facts and events that can be consciously recalled and put into words, dependent on the hippocampus; skills belong to nondeclarative memory.", "d": "Semantic memory (Endel Tulving, 1972) is the declarative store of general facts, concepts and word meanings, such as knowing what a raga is, not the skill of playing one."}}, {"q": "Bandura's social cognitive theory is based on which perspective?", "concept": "The answer is b: social cognitive theory, which Albert Bandura (1986) named in Social Foundations of Thought and Action, is based on the learning perspective. Bandura first built social learning theory, showing in the Bobo doll studies (Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961) that children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the aggression without ever being reinforced themselves. In Social Learning Theory (1977) and then the 1986 book, he argued that people learn mainly by observing others (observational learning, or modelling). Observational learning needs four processes: attention to the model, retention of what was seen, reproduction of the act and motivation to perform it. He added reciprocal determinism, the idea that the person (thoughts and expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another, and self-efficacy (1977), the belief that one can carry out an action successfully, which shapes what people attempt and how long they persist. Personality textbooks therefore place Bandura under the learning (social learning or social-cognitive) perspective. When Riya watches her elder brother speak confidently at a school debate and be praised, she may start to volunteer in class herself: she learned the behaviour vicariously, and her growing self-efficacy keeps it going. Option b is right because the theory explains behaviour through learning, by observation and by cognitive processing of experience. Exam trap: \"ageing\" is a developmental topic, not a perspective in personality theory, and \"none of the above\" lures anyone unsure whether learning and behaviourism differ. Key note: the theory grew out of behaviourism, so c is the near miss, but Bandura rejected radical behaviourism by putting cognition (expectations, self-efficacy) at the centre, so it is classed under the learning perspective rather than behaviourism, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Ageing is a subject of developmental and lifespan psychology (gerontology), not a theoretical perspective from which social cognitive theory was built.", "c": "Behavioural is the near miss: Bandura's work grew out of behaviourism, but he rejected B. F. Skinner's radical behaviourism by putting cognition (expectations, self-efficacy, self-regulation) at the centre, so it is classed under learning rather than behaviourism.", "d": "None of the above fails because learning, option b, is the perspective textbooks assign to Bandura's theory."}}, {"q": "An increase in the value of one variable with a decrease in the value of the other variable implies:", "concept": "The answer is c: when one variable increases as the other decreases, the correlation is negative. Correlation describes how two variables vary together, and the most widely used index is the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, of Karl Pearson (1896), which runs from +1 through 0 to -1. Its sign gives the direction and its size gives the strength. In a positive correlation (r between 0 and +1) the two variables rise and fall together, like height and weight. In a negative or inverse correlation (r between 0 and -1) high scores on one go with low scores on the other: as hours of practice rise, errors fall. A zero correlation (r close to 0) means no linear relationship. On a scatterplot a negative correlation slopes downward from left to right. A correlation of -0.80 is just as strong as one of +0.80; the minus sign shows only the direction. If Arjun finds that the more time students spend on their phones at night, the lower their morning test scores, he has found a negative correlation, though correlation alone cannot show that one causes the other. Option c is right because opposite movement of two variables is the definition of a negative correlation. Exam trap: zero correlation and no correlation (a and d) mean the same thing, and a minus sign shows inverse direction, not a weak relationship.", "traps": {"a": "A zero correlation (r close to 0) means the two variables show no linear relationship; changes in one tell you nothing about the other, so it cannot describe a consistent opposite movement.", "b": "A positive correlation (r between 0 and +1) means both variables move in the same direction, rising or falling together.", "d": "No correlation is another way of saying zero correlation: no systematic linear relationship at all, rather than an opposite one."}}, {"q": "A disruption of the ability to speak is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a disruption of the ability to speak is Broca's aphasia. Aphasia is a disorder of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere. Paul Broca (1861), a French surgeon, studied a patient named Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because that was almost the only syllable he could say, although he understood much of what was said to him. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area, just in front of the motor cortex that controls the face and mouth. Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia is slow, effortful, halting speech in short, telegraphic phrases that drop small grammatical words (\"want... tea... now\"), while comprehension of everyday speech is relatively preserved; patients usually know their speech is wrong and are frustrated by it. Carl Wernicke (1874) described the opposite pattern after damage to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus: Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia, with fluent, well-articulated but empty speech full of wrong and invented words, and poor comprehension. The arcuate fasciculus links the two areas, and damage to it produces conduction aphasia, marked by poor repetition. After a stroke, Kabir's grandfather knows exactly what he wants to say but can only get out \"water... glass\", a picture of Broca's aphasia. Option b is right because Broca's aphasia is the loss of fluent speech production. Exam trap: Wernicke's aphasia also disturbs speech, but there the speech flows easily; it is meaning and comprehension that break down.", "traps": {"a": "Wernicke's aphasia (Carl Wernicke, 1874) follows damage to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus: speech is fluent but empty and full of paraphasias, and comprehension is poor, so the ability to speak is not what is lost.", "c": "'Bedecker's aphasia' is not a recognised syndrome; the name is an invented distractor.", "d": "'Warnick's aphasia' is a corrupted spelling of Wernicke's aphasia, a disorder of comprehension with fluent speech, so even read as Wernicke's it does not fit."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "concept": "The answer is a: the token economy is not a classical conditioning treatment; it comes from operant conditioning. Classical (respondent) conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflexive response comes to trigger a similar response itself. Therapies built on it change the emotional response attached to a stimulus. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs deep relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared situations, so relaxation gradually replaces anxiety (reciprocal inhibition, or counterconditioning). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape prevented, until the conditioned fear extinguishes because nothing bad happens. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus, such as a nausea-producing drug, so that the behaviour itself comes to evoke discomfort. The token economy works through consequences instead. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran one on a psychiatric ward: patients earned tokens, which are generalised secondary reinforcers, for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, just as the operant principles of B. F. Skinner (1938) predict. In a children's hostel, Meera might give stars for making the bed and finishing homework, to be traded for a Sunday outing. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour by reinforcement after a response, not by pairing stimuli. Exam trap: ask whether a technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical); aversion therapy feels like punishment, which makes it look operant, but its standard textbook basis is classical pairing.", "traps": {"b": "Aversive (aversion) therapy is classical conditioning: an unwanted behaviour or stimulus (alcohol, a fetish object) is repeatedly paired with an aversive unconditioned stimulus, such as an emetic drug or mild shock, until it evokes discomfort itself.", "c": "Flooding is a classical conditioning extinction technique: prolonged, full-intensity exposure to the conditioned fear stimulus without the feared outcome lets the fear die out; Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy is its imaginal version.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) counterconditioning technique, pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy, so it rests squarely on classical conditioning."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a true indicator of the validity of items during test construction?", "concept": "The answer is a: face validity is not a true indicator of validity. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure, and evidence for it is gathered during test construction. Face validity is only whether a test looks, to the people taking it, as if it measures what it says. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, first edition 1954) stressed that face validity is not validity in the technical sense: it concerns appearance and acceptability, not what the test actually measures. A test can look right and measure nothing useful, or look odd and predict well; many MMPI items have little face validity yet separate clinical groups empirically. It still matters in practice, because test takers cooperate better with items that seem relevant. The true indicators are three. Content validity asks whether the items adequately sample the whole domain, judged systematically by experts against a test blueprint. Criterion validity asks whether scores correlate with an outside criterion measured at the same time (concurrent) or later (predictive). Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether scores behave as the theory of the construct predicts, including convergent and discriminant evidence (Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959). Suppose Kabir writes an anxiety scale with items like \"I often feel anxious\": it has high face validity, but only correlations with clinical ratings and other anxiety measures can show that it is valid. Option a is right because face validity is a surface impression, not an empirical index. Exam trap: content validity also relies on judgement, which makes it look like face validity, but it is a systematic expert check against the domain, not a first impression.", "traps": {"b": "Content validity is genuine validity evidence: experts judge systematically whether the items represent the whole domain, often against a table of specifications, which is central to building achievement tests.", "c": "Construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955) is often called the most fundamental kind: evidence that scores relate to other measures as the theory of the construct predicts, through convergent, discriminant and factor-analytic evidence.", "d": "Criterion validity is empirical: test scores are correlated with an external criterion measured at the same time (concurrent) or later (predictive), giving a validity coefficient."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is one of the most influential theories of depression?", "concept": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck's cognitive theory is one of the most influential theories of depression. Aaron Beck (1967), an American psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' thinking was persistently and systematically negative, and proposed that depression is driven by how people think. At its core is the cognitive triad: negative views of the self (\"I am worthless\"), the world (\"nothing goes right\") and the future (\"it will never get better\"). These views come from negative schemas, stable beliefs formed in early experience, often around loss or rejection, that lie dormant until a stressful event activates them. Once active, they produce automatic negative thoughts and systematic cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, all-or-nothing thinking and personalisation. The theory led directly to cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979), in which patients test their negative thoughts against evidence; it is among the most researched psychological treatments for depression, and the Beck Depression Inventory (1961) is one of the most used depression scales. If Meera fails one exam and thinks \"I am stupid, my teachers despise me and I will never get a job\", she shows all three parts of the triad. Option b is right because Beck's model is the theory of depression that textbooks single out as most influential. Exam trap: \"Berne's humanistic theory\" borrows a real name, but Eric Berne is linked to transactional analysis, not to any theory of depression. Key note: Martin Seligman's learned helplessness (1975) and its attributional (1978) and hopelessness (1989) reformulations are also major theories, so c is the near miss; Beck's cognitive theory is the one textbooks single out as the most influential, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud's psychodynamic theory (Mourning and Melancholia, 1917) saw depression as anger at a lost loved object turned inward against the self; it is historically important but has far less research support than Beck's model.", "c": "Martin Seligman's learned helplessness theory (1975), from dogs that stopped trying to escape unavoidable shock, is a major theory and the near miss; it is usually taught alongside Beck's, and its later attributional and hopelessness versions moved towards cognition.", "d": "Eric Berne founded transactional analysis (Games People Play, 1964), a theory of ego states and social transactions; it is not a humanistic theory of depression, and no such 'Berne's humanistic theory' exists."}}]});
